  "text": " Images 2.6 | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Images 2.6 ( openai.com ) 328 points by davidbarker 13 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 197 comments help \n \n DanHulton 11 hours ago | next [–] \n That stained glass whale thing makes me especially grumpy. When it scrolled onto the page, my first thought was \"whoah, what a neat idea\" and then it was _immediately_ followed by \"what a terribly execution.\" Like, I'm not even an artist, so I'm sure there's a million things I'm missing, but it's just so upsetting to look at. There's weird random reflections on things that shouldn't have reflections, the coloured light absolutely doesn't actually track through the stained glass onto any surface that makes any sense, light is coming from every which way, it's just... gross to look at for anything more than a quarter of a second. And this is what they're using to hype people up for it. /shrug reply ChrisRR 58 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n I don't personally see the issue. It's a highly stylised image so there's not any reflections or anything that immediately jump out to me as obviously wrong reply swiftcoder 42 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > anything that immediately jump out to me as obviously wrong Trace any light ray through the scene, and you will see they are bending in ways that light does not. I'm sure we could teach a world model about light, but this image model fundamentally does not understand how light behaves (which is one of the primary things an artist needs to understand). reply roenxi 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yeah. I think that the pelican SVG benchmark is actually a much better reflection of the state of AI art than the visual quality of the images. Based on current trends I would not be at all surprised if LLMs working with tools, SVGs and looking at their own work get there before diffusion models. I've stopped keeping up with the image models so maybe I'm underestimating the progress but I dropped out because they were so frustratingly bad at composition and integrating details together. That whale image makes me think that the situation hasn't improved as much as I'd expected. It is a really cool idea and a pretty picture, but the whale looks a lot like it is flying over the top of some sort of huge glass dome on the ground. reply phoghed 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [5 more] \n [flagged] sethaurus 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Please don't fulminate. Please don't sneer, including at the rest of the community. > Please respond to the strongest plausible interpretation of what someone says, not a weaker one that's easier to criticize. Assume good faith. > Eschew flamebait. Avoid generic tangents. Omit internet tropes. https://news.ycombinator.com/newsguidelines.html reply Conscat 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n If not by the pixels, how else could you assess the quality of an image? reply mindcandy 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's a reference to the very old https://knowyourmeme.com/memes/this-looks-shopped Long before generative AI, unpleasant nerds would poop on fun images by claiming they could \"just tell\" that they were altered in photoshop. Today I'm seeing a complaint that the path tracing from the multi-layered stained glass into the cloud simulation is not as accurate as a human would have painted by hand. And, that apparently makes us grumpy. There have been unending examples of people liking an image until informed it was made by AI. Then, suddenly they see all sorts of fine-detail excuses to dislike it. And, of people calling out \"obviously AI\" problems in the works of human master artists when someone lies and tells them it was generated. I'm not seeing any difference in the discussion here. The whale is a bit overly high-frequency. The owl is fine. The guy at the computer would be considered great if a human made it. The travel ad is bland. The balloon letters are fine. None of these are \"terrible eye sores\". Some people don't like AI generated imagery as a process. Seeing generated images make them feel upset. That's reasonable. But, claiming these images in isolation are objectively awful regardless of their source is reactionary. If any of them were made by a person using Photoshop and/or Blender, they would be received with a resounding \"Meh\" or better. reply swivelmaster 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Vibes? reply minimaxir 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [6 more] \n [flagged] Feathercrown 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I've never understood why people have this viewpoint. If there is no preference for one property of a work (ie. lighting) to match reality just because another unrelated property (biology) doesn't, then surely the only possible scenarios remaining are an exact replica of the real world (where all properties match the real world) or an entirely arbitrary construction (where no properties should match reality). Fiction is predicated on the idea of \"reality, but...\". Saying that any single difference from reality should lead you to expect no similarities to reality at all, if followed, would collapse all fictions into pure arbitrary soup. reply post-it 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Well no. I can imagine lots of things in my head that are impossible but would be correctly lit, and I could do the same in Blender. reply casey2 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Not really, you could imagine another technique that 3D models it without force but with raytracing, puts it in that scene and takes a picture, the result would look better. That could then be a starting point for trying to get the lighting an panels just right to make the best reflections. When you forgo process the odds that you'll make good art drop to 0. Tool use is a much better path forward for self driving, artistic computer than big data. Images 2.6 only has use as a tool. reply mplewis 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n nah, the physical light is still a real concept, and it looks really shitty here. reply bowsamic 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n The point of fantasy or even surrealism is to contrast with the parts of the work that are chosen to be realistic. When random parts of the work are broken in meaningless ways it becomes slop reply minimaxir 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Using the grid trick for animations to generate the individual frames has been around for awhile and not specific to ChatGPT: I'm surprised it took this long for one of the official providers to make it explicit. However, adherence between frames is generally tricky, and even in the examples there is a lot of noise between frames although that could be due to compression. SVG support is interesting but I suspect shenanigans are afoot since that's not how image generation models work, and it would likely strip any SynthID watermarks. It's possible OpenAI has pipelined a raster-to-vector tool that does the conversion automatically. (I developed a similar pipeline and it works extremely well; I'll open source it at some point) EDIT: Per documentation, there is an explicit `output_format: \"svg\"`, and I am indeed correct there is raster-to-vector in play: > The API creates a reference image and uses it to produce the SVG. The quality setting controls that reference image. Decode data[0].b64_json as base64 and save the resulting UTF-8 XML bytes with a .svg extension. OpenAI claims 50% faster generation with Images 2.6 but curiously price is the same as Image 2.5. reply vunderba 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n From what I can tell after some preliminary testing, you have to pass an additional large language model to the OpenAI API when generating SVGs. I couldn't find this requirement in the documentation, but if you don't pass one in, you get this error: 400 {\"message\": \"SVG generation requires 'svg.model'.\",\n \"param\": \"svg.model\", \"code\": \"missing_required_parameter\"}\n\n \nI tried it with Sunburst with both gpt-5.5 and gpt-5.6-sol, and the results were nothing spectacular. It’s something you could already do in the ChatGPT interface by asking it to convert an existing image to an equivalent SVG. https://imgpb.com/bkUrc Recraft is probably the only group I know of that had a native text-to-SVG generator pretty early on, but since it’s a closed model, I’m not familiar with what they were doing under the hood technically. https://fal.ai/models/fal-ai/recraft/v4/pro/text-to-vector reply bizer 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Image 2.6 costs more tokens for generating low and medium quality images, so it ends up being a bit more expensive. Could refer to https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generati... reply padolsey 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yeh using sprites to make frames consistent (kinda tilting latent space towards a character consistency, zero-shot ~LoRA hack?) is old-school. I did this 3 years ago. https://j11y.io/dance/ reply preommr 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Feels like ai image generation has plateaud. Coding related have had massive step-up improvements over time. The same cannot be said for image generation. If anything, I swear it's gone backwards. It peaked with very early midjourney (like 2022), maybe follow ups with stable diffusion, and then with some of the nano banana models for actual editing. But overall, very lackluster in comparison to other areas. reply pnt12 21 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n I think with software engineering the biggest jumps have been agentic platforms or work flows. You can create a ticket and this triggers na agent to read the relevant docs and submit an MR, another to validate multiple concerns (correctness, security, performance, costs), another to uodate docs, etc. That's far more impressive than \"dev sits down and asks LLM to write code\". With images, I don't see such potential. A builder and a reviewer iterating on improvements? Maybe the reviewer noticing it just needs programmable improvements, eg contrast, color balance etc and triggering a script on top of the LLM generation? reply reisse 15 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Feels like ai image generation has plateaud. I'd bet the future is in combined AI-driven 3D modelling + rendering + AI post-processing. This pipeline solves quite a bit of typical image generation problems. reply vunderba 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There have been a lot of strides in open-weight models, well beyond SDXL. Flux, back in August of 2024 was a game changer, finally allowing us to move past SDXL’s nuclear pile of comma-delimited keywords for prompting and allowing more natural language. And this summer we got Ideogram V4 and Krea2, both of which are highly capable.\nYou can see how the open/closed models have advanced since SDXL in the Progress tab. https://genai-showdown.specr.net MJ is pretty out-of-date by this point though it can still be fun from an image space exploration perspective. It scored a rather pitiful 2 out of 15 on my bench back in the summer of 2025. reply jere 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Progress peaked in 2022? Otherwise you’re implying the quality plateaued 4 years ago which is ludicrous. reply abroszka33 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Feels like ai image generation has plateaud.\n> Coding related have had massive step-up improvements over time.\n> The same cannot be said for image generation. If anything, I swear it's gone backwards. I would argue both are exactly the same. The images are getting nicer, the AI can alter pictures better. It's the same as coding. The difference is our perception. Looking at image takes 2s, and we can spot what we don't like. Looking at a code change takes hours to understand. It's very difficult to tell how much code generation is improving because almost nobody is reading it. Image and code generation don't correlate exactly, but I would be surprised to see a big jump of capabilities in either without the other quickly following. reply algoth1 16 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Krea has kept improving editing reply mgw 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Your feeling is generally right. There have been no seminal moments in image generation anymore in the last couple of months. Most attention from the big labs has gone to video since that‘s where the money is.\nWe have a page to track the history of it all here https://lumenfall.ai/arena/history (Right now missing some models which I‘ll add today.) reply JohnQPulp 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n You could argue that progress has been relatively flat since Nano Banana Pro, but that's still less than a full year old (just barely), and that was the first model that could really fully maintain visual consistency across complex edits. The GPT ones are a little stylistically disappointing in ways, but they've still been making steady progress too. I don't think we can claim a peak just yet, even if it's been slower. reply tiffanyh 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There’s less money to be made with images, than code gen. Investment in new models follows the money. reply Jonovono 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Seedream 4 continues to be the best model by far at character consistency. I havnt done my tests on this model yet, however. reply ChrisRR 57 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n \"It takes 90% of the time to do 90% of the work. The other 10% takes the other 90% of the time\" reply Jtarii 59 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Turns out you can't brute force art, to the surprise of no one except tech bros. reply lifeisloving 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Coding flattened out like 9 months ago. In reality, people are getting dumber and lazier from over using them, so they feel like they're improving when they aren't really, outside some areas like RE. In reality RE has only gotten better because they RLd it into the models because they NEED data, so now every kid is decomping proprietary softwate and sending them back great training data. reply mixtureoftakes 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n >Coding flattened out like 9 months ago. It absolutely has not. You do have a nice point with data though, somehow haven't thought about that reply ChrisRR 56 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Coding flattened out like 9 months ago Opus 5.5 begs to differ reply lifeisloving 50 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Even on benchmarks it hasnt gone up that much. Ya'll are extermely one shot by marketing and twitter posts of one page demos. Not everything runs in a browser, it still creates fires in codebases and generally produces spaghetti code. It gets the job done for 50th percentile problems for sure. Turns out not everything is a 50th percentile problem. reply laserlinus 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I miss the outpainting feature they used to have (3 years ago?) in the dall-e days. It feels like tooling overall for image generation has been standing still or almost regressed the past few years, while the quality has improved reply sroussey 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n https://chatgpt.com/share/6aebc6f8-d660-83e8-9cb3-49bdf8caa7... I guess I should have been careful about “half”? These models have a hard time imagining men with naturally blond hair getting grey. reply minimaxir 12 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I would caution against using the webapp to test the quality of this new model because OpenAI does not indicate which image model and effort is being used to generate the image, to my continued frustration. That said, I gave it one of my more difficult image prompts and the model now surprisingly passes (without plagiaristic cheating): https://chatgpt.com/share/6aebcbcd-6b8c-83e8-b52b-dbedf20ce4... reply vunderba 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think it's more impressive that it managed so much coherence in a single image than anything else. Very few problems with each of the pokemon anatomically speaking. Mew's kind of a mess but the rest are pretty accurate. That being said, the ukiyo-e artwork looks more like a standard colored pencil illustration, and it sort of tried to cheat by adding some vaguely Japanese natural scenery. See this Kickstarter all the way back in 2013 for a great example of what Pokémon might actually look like as ukiyo-e woodblock prints. https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/1499165518/ukiyo-e-hero... https://ricedigital.co.uk/this-japanese-ukiyo-e-pokemon-art-... reply mitchdoogle 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I remember that Kickstarter. I follow the woodblock carver who worked with the artist. His name is David Bull and he has some great videos on woodblock carving on YouTube: https://m.youtube.com/@seseragistudio reply vunderba 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Oh, thanks for showing me these videos! The way that he presents is so calm - he's like a woodprint Bob Ross. reply xp84 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Wow. Are those the correct Pokemon and everything? Quite cool. reply minimaxir 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, the Pokemon are correct. Compare to my attempt a year ago with Nano Banana Pro, which plagiarized the images, didn't do interesting things with the background, and got 271 wrong: https://minimaxir.com/2025/12/nano-banana-pro/pokemongrid_hu... When I did a similar prompt with ChatGPT 2.0 it was worse, so interesting to see improvement. reply Computer0 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n On the back end a language model is effectively writing a long detailed prompt describing this image exactly right? The image model can't know this. reply minimaxir 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n ChatGPT Images (and some others like Nano Banana) are autoregressive models, and the text model generates the tokens in the same way which are then passed in the correct form for an image model to decode. That is how it can reason the prime numbers + Pokemon + art style conditional. It is different from earlier image models which just use a simple text encoder and struggle with conditional logic if not explicitly trained for it. reply jackbrookes 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If you look at the thinking steps it clearly includes: > * Mapped Pokémon primes\n> * Organized the grid before generating the image. So really it is first figuring out the exact specification of the layout, then prompting the image generation tool with this specification - perhaps unless you do it with an \"instant\" model. reply weird-eye-issue 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yes it thinks about it and then calls the image tool with the detailed prompt with exactly what to include in the image reply numpad0 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think that particular one would be a prompting problem. Instagrammers and anime artists do left-right two-tone dyes all the time, so left-right two-tone rather than meshed gray blonde would be a reasonable interpretation of \"half blond and grey hair\" based on representations on Internet collected data. reply nananana9 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think the super-AGI should be able to figure this one out. This is the kind of things I would expect to have to think about when prompting a local 4B model, not the latest Sol or whatever is driving the image model here. reply numpad0 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I don't think this ChatGPT's interpretation was necessarily wrong, given the prompt \"Image of a middle aged man with half blond and grey hair\". Maybe it should have asked back for clarification but that's it. This to me as if someone is complaining for a basket full of extremely thin sliced fried potatoes for an order of \"chips\", rather than like wood and metalworking scraps. reply crystaln 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It precisely matches your prompt, more accurately I think than your expectation which it meets if described accurately: https://chatgpt.com/share/6aec2ba7-6edc-83ea-9a48-b41b53874a... reply apetresc 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n That prompt seems almost deliberately obtuse. The natural phrasing would be “greying blonde hair”. If you described someone as having half-X, half-Y hair, I’d expect to see something like that image, too. reply E-Reverance 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Still looks like an eyesore, I genuinely want to know if the engineers who work on the product don't notice it reply nashadelic 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n whats incredible is when in their video they're talking about complexity, the cat's images have extra limbs in low and medium complexity. They could've easily generated more versions to get the clean versions. But it just doesn't seem like they care. Maybe this is what someone who is so used to using AI to move fast becomes: someone who drops the quality bar to 80%? reply slipusaururgf 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n They mentioned multiple times in previous blog posts that they do not cherry pick examples. Would you appreciate more if their model generated cats with 5 legs in 99% of cases, and they cherry picked that 1 in 100 image where it has 4 legs? Do you think doing that would make them more caring and professional? reply Revanche1367 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I doubt their models are that bad for simple and common things like cats, probably takes just a handful of tries at most for something like getting the correct number of limbs. They could just mention the number of tries on the side like a little bubble showing up to show “Rendered in 5 attempts” or something along those lines. I wouldn’t think less of the tech personally. reply Fannon 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n That's an interesting observation, and that's also my feeling: Everything is now expected to move so fast, dropping quality bar to 80% or below is not uncommon. Not like this is the fault of the AI progress, we could use AI to go beyond the quality what we used to deliver, too. It's just the pace expectations that are now \"in the room\" ? reply miki123211 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n People who make things often have an unrealistic expectation of how much users care about quality (and don't realize how much users actually care about price). We've seen this play out over and over again, in many different sectors. The whole shtick of the old (Bellhead) telecom world was quality at the (literal) expense of price, which didn't work out well for them. Same for CD versus pirated mp3s, DVD versus pirated videos (and, to a lesser extend, theaters versus bootleg cam releases), native texting / GSM calls versus WhatsApp and similar, mainstream newspapers and media versus blogging and social media, traditional electrical appliances versus Chinese-made, and so on. Because of the innovator's dilemma, In many cases, the cheaper, lower-quality product later surpasses the quality of its more expensive counterpart. AI is just one more example of this happening. It lets you make more things which otherwise would be far too expensive to make, even if that often means a lower quality product. reply SapporoChris 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It could be that they've been using AI so long that they're unaware of the problem. reply cousin_it 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yeah, I've been mystified for years why they can't just train the \"AI look\" out of images. Is it, like, something undetectable by machine? But then how come we can often detect it so easily? reply Conscat 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think this is fundamental. People will probably dislike whatever it is that AI images look like, because whatever they look like is going to be associated with cheap. Th