t to do for models currently. reply baalimago 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n My biggest surprise with this release is the amount of upvotes on HN. It's literally the same as what we've had the past 1.5 years, or more. reply bananaflag 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Stupid question: why is there a separate image model? Shouldn't GPT-6.x be multimodal? reply armcat 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n It's not a stupid question. It is multi-modal but it's about efficiency. A model that is trained purely on generating images is smaller and faster and has its own set of optimisation techniques, compared to a \"generalist\" multi modal LLM. Same applies for speech models. reply spi 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I... doubt that's true? I don't know all the details how these recent image generation models work, but last time I checked people still used diffusion models for it. Diffusion pipelines are _slower_ and _more expensive_ than the language model, not cheaper. \"generalist\" multi-modal LLM mostly uses images (and videos, sounds, etc.) as input. I'm quite sure they have an output head for it, as well, used during training, but their outputs are probably not as good. They generate images much faster (hence probably cheaper), too: typically a token is 16x16 pixels, so to generate an image of modest 512x512 resolution would be the same as generating a 1024 token length string, that nowadays is pretty fast and cheap (e.g. with Sol it would be about $0.01). But the result won't be great. Diffusion models instead are trained to generate a \"denoising\" step of the image. Typically they have some inputs (e.g. the owl sketch in the blog post and an embedding of your textual request), start either from pure noise or from a noisy version of an input image, and then repeatedly slightly modify it in anywhere between 4 and 100 (or more) steps (hyperparameter to be chosen, and also depends if you start from noise or from something non-pure-noise), each time making a version that the model believes to be a bit closer to where you want to get. The underlying model _could_ be similar to the language model, but I think it isn't, because (1) if you want to call it 100 times in 4 seconds it must be smaller and dedicated, and (2) it's trained to do _exactly_ that, i.e. remove 1/100th of the noise, no more, no less. So it must be a dedicated one. A few years ago people did research on \"one shot\" methods (earliest were GANs of course, but also some autoregressive model like a multimodal LLM directly), but as far as I know, quality has always stayed well behind diffusion models. It was faster, though, not more expensive. reply armcat 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If you know how an LLM could have an output image head, that is purely generative without using diffusion, and not a classification/selection head, I’m really interested in learning more. I think you are onto something very new and novel here reply rawoke083600 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Lol honestly i like chatgpt image function. I also take it for what it is, incredible none determinstic engineering. That being said, i always feels a bit of sympathy for \"the machine\" when they say things like: - Oh it horrible to use - It changed everything instead of the one thing i asked (probably biggest misconceptions and misunderstood technical part among my none-techie friends). I've heard the \"reason\" from my non-techie friends \"oh 'they' do that on purpose ! So you have to upgrade. Ive once or twice had the non-deterministic discussion with them.. but dont think explained it very well. Either way for all its warts etc i still find Ai-images extremely useful, as someone that before couldnt really produce any visual-assets. reply pointlessone 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I don’t care for image gen, but I appreciate that they drew the rest of the owl. reply DeliCheese 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Thats why it became completely incompetent today trying to generate an image! I love half baked \"test it in production\" updates... reply alexpadula 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I really don't like this generative AI. I may look at it like an artist but completed generated software, writing, music and imagery is a robbery of human worth. Absolutely. reply iamgopal 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Generative AI is created by human worth. reply alexpadula 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No, it's created by data scanned from the internet, books and fed to the models. How is that a good thing for anyone? I'd love your take. People spend years giving themselves value to others to live a life of worth and value. reply ion_trapper 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The data just scanned itself and organized itself into an neural network? Would be an awesome display of bootstrapped intelligence if that happened! Sadly, it was the result of decades of human research. reply creata 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Decades of human research and mountains of work used without permission. Not that they legally did or didn't need that permission, but ethically, kind of a dick move. reply alexpadula 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Yep, that's the sad part. reply pizzafeelsright 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Human wealth? Humans, some, are worth more than others, per birth rates and varying investments by institutions. reply jawns 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n My only use case for this is animating custom Slack emojis. But trust, I'm a have a field day with that. reply gcanyon 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n That has to be a \"draw the rest of the fucking owl\" reference in the middle there, right? https://imgur.com/how-i-feel-when-somebody-gives-me-advice-g... reply seabass 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n “Preserve the background” blurs background reply tintor 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n SVG? Who can generate a pelican on a bicycle? reply brainwad 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Why do their images still have the classic piss yellow tint? Gemini ones don't look like this. Is it a deliberate way to make their slop identifiable? reply algoth1 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Can it finally do pixel art? reply vunderba 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n If you define pixel art as just a vague overall look, then yes, it can do pixel art. If you define pixel art as being limited to a fixed color palette, like 16 or 256 colors and aligned to a square grid, then no, not without some manual post-processing. It's not helped by the fact that a lot of people who are not artistically inclined will generate pixel art on these image generators at 1MP resolutions (far higher than traditional pixel art), such that even the casual observer can typically see the bad smearing and fringing in the final result. reply minimaxir 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n That depends on your definition of pixel art. (people are exceptionally pedantic about this) With the right prompt engineering, it (and similar quality models) can indeed do relevant pixel art, but due to the existence of blotchiness/SynthID watermarks you'll need to do some sort of postprocessing regardless. reply Dwedit 11 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There are SDXL pixel art models out there that do actual pixel art. They generate images that are 8x too large, you need to downscale them and reduce colors externally. reply vunderba 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n yeah, the original Retro Diffusion models were an early example of that - IIRC it had models separately fine-tuned for 32x32, 64x64, etc. so you'd nearest neighbor squash'em down to that size after the initial gen to kill the high frequency noise. reply hridyaagrawal 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n worse than images 2.5, the yellow wash slop is back\nimage models regressing? reply Papazsazsa 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n You may notice that luxury brands are making stuff by hand now. It's already painfully uncool to use AI images, regardless of how \"real\" they look – because they're cheap reply teamolHuang 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n wait...\ndid we get (sort of) sora back??? reply minimaxir 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Video generation models work much differently than what this GIF pipeline is doing here. reply xgulfie 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"keep the shallow depth of field\" adds more depth of field reply jjhfarmer 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n they always use horrible examples in these blog posts - that whale picture a the bottom is terrible reply Computer0 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n The gifs really accentuate the artifacting, not sure I would've lead with that. reply jansan 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Do they have an example of an SVG image created by the new model somewhere? reply minimaxir 12 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Documentation has two: https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generati... reply jrmg 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It looks like the images in there are 'based' on the \"hot springs symbol\" - a Japanese map symbol encoded into Unicode that seems to have become synonymous with \"piping hot bread\" in the West! https://emojipedia.org/hot-springs reply rprwhite 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The images in the GPs link show a common pictogram for artisanal bread. The image itself is actually a close representation of the real thing. An image search for sourdough will return many images showing bread with a similar appearance. \nAnecdotally, I’ve never seen that hot springs symbol used for bread, and I’ve lived in the west my whole life.\nNot without irony, but connecting the bread symbol with the hot springs symbol feels like the sort of mistake an llm would make. reply phoghed 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I always thought it was coffee like the Java logo lol reply jansan 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n 