  "text": " \n \n \n \n \n Downloads - iTerm2 - macOS Terminal Replacement \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Menu \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n Home \n News \n Features \n FAQ \n Documentation \n Downloads \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Stable Releases \n\n\n \n\n\n \nStable releases update rarely but have no serious bugs.\n \n\n\n iTerm2 3.7.7 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on November 3, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.7 of iTerm2 was built on November 3,\n2026.\n\nNew Features:\n- `it2 session set-status` has a new --progress\n option that starts or stops the tab’s progress\n indicator for a program that reports through\n hooks but does not draw the indicator itself.\n The set_session_status Python API function\n takes the same progress argument.\n- The hook program for Claude Code and Codex is\n now `it2 handle-agent-hook` (add --agent codex\n for Codex), so the hook works wherever it2\n does. Existing Claude Code installs keep\n working; reinstalling the integration updates\n the hook entries.\n- `it2 session add-clipping` and\n `archive-clippings` accept --tmux and\n --tmux-pane, and every addressing option\n accepts an empty value as “not given”, so a\n script can pass all of them and let it2 pick.\n- The Python API can now run JavaScript in a\n browser session’s page and get the result\n back with Session.async_eval_javascript, so a\n script can read a page or fill in a form\n without clicking on the screen. The first time\n a script does this on a site, iTerm2 asks you\n to allow it. You can review and change these\n permissions, and those for loading URLs, in\n Settings > General > Magic.\n- You can hide a browser session’s toolbar so the\n page fills the pane with Web > Show Toolbar, or\n from the Python API with\n Session.async_set_browser_toolbar_hidden.\n- nushell is now a supported shell for shell\n integration, including automatic loading and\n SSH integration.\n- Passwords saved in the Password Manager’s\n Keychain storage can now be exported to a\n passphrase-encrypted .iterm2passwords file and\n imported on another Mac. Use Export Passwords…\n and Import Passwords… in the Password Manager’s\n settings menu, or double-click the file.\n Settings > General > Export All Settings and\n Data can also include them.\n- When a host reached through SSH Integration\n asks to use it2, you can now choose “Always\n Allow This Host” so it is not asked again on\n later connections. Review or remove these hosts\n in Settings > General > Magic > API access\n over ssh.\n- The Claude Code integration can now be\n installed on a host reached through SSH\n Integration, so Claude Code running there\n reports its status too. Choose iTerm2 > Install\n Claude Code Integration from a session on that\n host, including a tmux Integration pane there.\n Uninstalling removes it from every host you are\n connected to.\n\nImprovements:\n- Python API context menu items can be grouped\n into submenus and shown only in sessions where\n an expression is true, such as a user variable\n your script sets. See ContextMenuProviderRPC’s\n submenu and visible_when arguments.\n- If you customized the Code Review system\n prompt, iTerm2 offers to update it the next\n time a review starts, so reviews work in tmux\n and on remote hosts. It shows the changes\n first, and changes nothing else in your prompt.\n- Downloading a file or folder with “Download\n using SSH Integration” is much faster. It\n used to wait for each kilobyte before asking\n for the next.\n- The Python API now has type annotations, so\n editors and type checkers such as mypy can\n check scripts that use it.\n- “Support basic HTML tags in tab titles” now\n applies to pane title bars too, so , ,\n and render as styled text there instead of\n appearing literally. The window title shows the\n text without the tags, including titles set by\n a program or by tmux.\n- In iTerm2 Buddy, you can now tap, scroll, and\n type into a web browser session from your\n phone, and use back, forward, reload, and an\n address field. The phone’s keyboard comes up\n when a text field on the page has focus.\n Requires an up-to-date iTerm2 Buddy.\n- On a host reached through SSH Integration, it2\n is now a real command in ~/.iterm2/bin rather\n than a shell function, so scripts, sh -c, and\n tools such as Claude Code’s Bash tool can run\n it, not just an interactive shell. Commands\n that read standard input now receive it.\n- On a host reached through SSH Integration, an\n it2 command that names no session now acts on\n the session it runs in rather than the focused\n one.\n- A command run on a host with it2ssh host\n command, or by an SSH profile, can now use it2,\n as an interactive shell there can.\n- it2ssh now loads shell integration on the\n remote host by default, whatever the current\n profile’s Load Shell Integration Automatically\n setting. Run it2ssh --no-shell-integration to\n skip it. SSH profiles still follow their own\n setting.\n- it2 now works on a host reached through SSH\n Integration when the shell is tcsh, and when\n shell integration is not loaded there. Without\n it iTerm2 copies nothing to the host, so it2\n has to be installed there.\n- The git status bar component now shows the\n status of a repository on a host reached\n through SSH Integration, by running git there.\n It used to need it2git set up on the host.\n- Shell integration now loads inside tmux,\n including on a host you ssh to from a tmux\n pane, so marks, the current directory and\n host, and the git status work there. It used\n to need the environment variable\n ITERM_ENABLE_SHELL_INTEGRATION_WITH_TMUX. It\n still skips itself in GNU screen.\n- In a tmux Integration pane on a host reached\n through SSH Integration, the git status bar\n component and other features that depend on\n the session’s host now work without shell\n integration loaded in the pane.\n- With it2git on a remote host, the git status\n bar component now counts changes the same way\n as for a repository on this Mac: every\n untracked file counts, conflicts are not\n counted as changes, and a detached HEAD shows\n its full commit ID.\n- In a tmux Integration session on a host reached\n through SSH Integration, each pane now knows\n which program is running in it. The tab shows\n the job, the Jobs tool lists the pane’s\n processes, and triggers that watch for a\n program, such as entering the Claude Code\n workgroup, work there.\n- SSH Integration now paces output from the\n remote host, so a program that prints a lot no\n longer fills the connection’s buffers. Control-C\n takes effect at once instead of after minutes\n of buffered output on a slow link, and typing\n stays responsive while output streams.\n- On macOS 26, iTerm2 no longer lets macOS index\n its window and tab titles for search. Titles\n change constantly in a terminal, and indexing\n each one used a lot of CPU in Spotlight’s\n background processes.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Dragging a tab or pane no longer stutters\n with many windows open when the tab bar is\n always shown.\n- The browser’s address field no longer runs\n under the loading spinner while a page loads,\n and returns to its full width afterward.\n- Fixed more cases where a window could be made\n wider but not narrower.\n- If iTerm2 quits unexpectedly while tmux\n integration is attached, and you switched to\n another app at some point after attaching,\n tmux integration resumes when iTerm2\n relaunches with window restoration on.\n Before, the session that ran tmux -CC could\n show raw tmux output instead.\n- If iTerm2 quits unexpectedly after you\n detach from tmux integration, the session\n that ran tmux -CC no longer gets tmux\n commands typed into its shell when window\n restoration brings it back.\n- In an AI chat with an empty message field, the\n “↩ to submit” hint no longer overlaps a long\n placeholder.\n- With KeePassXC as the browser password\n manager, “Remember this password” for a\n website’s sign-in dialog now saves the\n password instead of failing with “Could not\n save the password”.\n- Hardened the handler for x-man-page: URLs so a\n malicious URL can no longer be crafted to run\n an unexpected command or make man render an\n arbitrary local file. Man section and page\n names are now checked against an allowlist of\n characters, and a URL with disallowed\n characters is rejected with a warning instead\n of opening. Thanks to vin01 for finding this\n vulnerability and to Gurpreet Shergill\n (@giki-shergill) for reporting it.\n- Dragging a split divider in a tmux window\n now leaves the panes the way the drag showed\n them. Before, a different divider could move,\n or panes beside the divider could jump to\n other sizes when the drag ended.\n- Fixed a crash from a trigger in a Dynamic\n Profile whose parameter has an unexpected\n type, such as a list. A text parameter\n written as a number, such as text to send, is\n now used as text instead of being ignored.\n- Shell > SSH > Disconnect now recovers an SSH\n Integration session whose local it2ssh process\n was killed, even while the remote host is\n printing output. Previously the session\n stopped showing output and ignored input.\n Disconnect also works before advanced SSH\n features have started, instead of saying it\n can’t disconnect.\n- After using tmux integration in an SSH\n Integration session, ending ssh no longer\n leaves the session ignoring typed text until\n Return is pressed.\n- Ending an SSH Integration session no longer\n sometimes types YXV0b3BvbGw= into the local\n shell, or leaves the tab unable to start\n another it2ssh connection.\n- A session no longer stops taking input when\n it2ssh is run on a host that was itself\n reached with it2ssh but lacks the Python that\n advanced SSH features need.\n- After quitting with SSH Integration sessions\n open, relaunching with window restoration on\n no longer types stray text, such as\n cmVzZXQy, into the restored tabs’ new shells.\n Likewise, when iTerm2 restarts after a crash\n and an SSH Integration session’s ssh has\n ended in the meantime, the restored session\n no longer types cmVjb3Zlcg== into its shell\n and then ignores input.\n- A file fetched over SSH Integration with\n Shell > ssh > Download Files… no longer stays\n listed as downloading in the Downloads menu\n after it finishes. Clear All in the Downloads\n menu now removes finished SSH Integration\n downloads, and their notification no longer\n says “Upload finished”.\n- With the Browser Plugin installed, “Download\n using SSH Integration” in the context menu no\n longer does nothing when the file can’t be\n found on the remote host. It now shows a\n failed download that says why.\n- When you connect with it2ssh user@host or an\n ssh:// URL, SSH Integration now records the\n user and host separately. Before, it treated\n all of “user@host” as the host name, which\n showed up in the file panel and the Downloads\n menu.\n- Running tmux -CC in a session that is already\n part of tmux integration, for example over\n ssh, now explains that tmux integration can’t\n be nested instead of silently doing nothing.\n- it2ssh no longer leaves the terminal waiting\n for Return when ssh fails before connecting,\n such as for a host name that doesn’t resolve.\n it2ssh now exits with ssh’s exit status.\n- it2ssh no longer hangs when the host or\n command contains non-ASCII text, such as\n accented letters or emoji. If iTerm2 can’t\n read the arguments, it now ends the\n connection and explains why instead of\n leaving the tab stuck.\n- When SSH Integration loads shell integration\n into a remote bash, the shell is now a login\n shell, so aliases and functions defined in\n ~/.profile or ~/.bash_profile are available.\n- The notification for a failed download or\n upload now names the file and gives the\n reason, instead of showing only an error such\n as “File not found”. Canceling a transfer no\n longer reports it as failed. An SSH\n Integration transfer that fails because of\n file or folder permissions now says so.\n- Downloading a folder over SSH Integration now\n tells you when files in it couldn’t be read\n and were left out, instead of reporting\n success with no mention of the missing files.\n- An SSH Integration download that fails or is\n stopped no longer leaves an empty or partial\n hidden file in your Downloads folder. Stop\n now takes effect right away even when the\n connection has stalled.\n- An SSH Integration download that can’t be\n saved into your Downloads folder when it\n finishes now reports that it failed, instead\n of saying the download finished. One that\n can’t be marked as downloaded from the\n internet is deleted and reported as failed,\n instead of staying listed as downloading.\n- it2 session list and other it2 commands that\n print names, paths or profile values no longer\n show JSON escapes, such as \\/dev\\/ttys001 for a\n tty.\n- On a FreeBSD or OpenBSD host reached through\n SSH Integration, sessions and the Jobs tool\n showed an environment variable such as\n VENDOR=unknown instead of the running program.\n- it2ssh now runs a command given after the host\n exactly as ssh does. Quotes in the command were\n lost, and newlines, tabs and non-ASCII text\n were mangled, so a command like\n it2ssh host \"grep 'a b' file\" ran the wrong\n thing.\n- A custom command or SSH profile command that\n contains a backslash or a double quote now\n runs as written. Such commands used to reach\n the shell broken when they ran in a login\n shell, which SSH profiles always do.\n- A tmux Integration pane whose server was not\n yet known to be local when the pane opened\n never learned which program it was running.\n- Programs that turn on the Kitty keyboard\n protocol and then request xterm’s\n modifyOtherKeys, as Claude Code does, keep\n the Kitty protocol, matching kitty. Before,\n the request turned it off, so Command keys\n stopped being reported.\n- With the Minimal or Compact theme and no tab\n bar at the top, the window buttons that appear\n when you point at the window’s top-left corner\n no longer cover a web browser’s back, forward,\n and reload buttons, which made them impossible\n to click. As with a status bar there, a small\n arrow appears in the corner instead; click it\n to show or hide the window buttons.\n- Fixed hotkey windows with the Centered or\n “Centered, No Title Bar” style returning to\n their original size each time they were shown\n after you resized them.\n- Fixed several Python API bugs. Subscribing to\n profile change notifications always failed,\n Color.hex failed for colors read from a\n profile, and items in nested submenus of\n MainMenu could not be reached on Python 3.12\n and earlier.\n- Fixed Selection.async_get_string in the Python\n API adding line breaks that copying the same\n selection in iTerm2 leaves out.\n- A program that sets the window or tab title\n over and over no longer makes iTerm2 fall far\n behind, run at full CPU for minutes, and grow\n to gigabytes of memory.\n- In an alert with a text field, such as the\n one asking how many spaces to use when you\n paste text with tabs, Cmd-A, Cmd-C, Cmd-V,\n Cmd-X, and Cmd-Z now edit the text instead of\n pressing a button. Also, two buttons in an\n alert no longer share the same Cmd-key\n shortcut.\n- Fixed a memory leak where each AI request,\n such as each AI chat message, left memory in\n use until iTerm2 quit.\n- Opening a file or link in a split pane while\n a tmux integration tab is current now opens\n it in a new tab. Before, it could take over\n the tmux pane’s place, vanish without\n running, or open a tmux shell pane without\n the link.\n- The SetWindowFrame escape sequence no longer\n shrinks a window to nothing or moves it far\n off screen when given bad values. Malformed\n values are ignored, and the window now stays\n between its minimum size and the size of your\n displays, with part of it on a display.\n- If a Python script that loads a URL in a\n browser session gave up waiting while you\n read the permission prompt, approving it\n afterward no longer loads the URL.\n- An AI chat set to Llama (Local) no longer\n sends its messages to your cloud AI provider\n when no Ollama model is available, for example\n because Ollama isn’t running. It now shows an\n error asking you to start Ollama. Naming the\n chat and drawing its icon now also use the\n chat’s own model instead of the default one.\n- Copying part of a line that mixes\n left-to-right and right-to-left text no\n longer drops the space between a\n left-to-right word and the right-to-left\n text after it, which fused the two words.\n- The file iTerm2 keeps next to its saved\n window state (restorable-state.sqlite-wal)\n no longer stays as large as the saved state\n itself. With long scrollback it could use a\n lot of disk.\n- When settings load from a custom folder,\n quitting no longer asks to copy settings to\n the folder every time just because iTerm2\n updated an older setting saved there.\n- Pressing Cmd-Y (Edit > Engage Artificial\n Intelligence) in the Cockpit’s composer no\n longer crashes iTerm2. The menu item is now\n disabled there.\n- With SSH integration, starting the remote\n shell, completing paths, and other requests\n with several long arguments no longer\n occasionally fail because the request was\n split in two on the way to the remote host.\n- In the minimal theme, a browser tab’s tint now\n matches the page you just loaded. Before, it\n showed the previous page’s background color\n until the next page loaded.\n- In the Cockpit’s composer, an @ followed by a\n space no longer keeps the session picker open,\n so Return inserts a newline instead of turning\n the @ into a session chip. After Esc closes the\n picker, it stays closed while you keep typing.\n- Sending from the Cockpit after closing the tab\n of one of its chosen sessions now sends to the\n sessions that remain, names the closed one,\n and removes its chip, instead of sending\n nothing and showing an internal identifier.\n- The Cockpit no longer covers alerts attached\n to a terminal window, such as the Broadcast\n Input confirmation, hiding their buttons.\n- The Cockpit composer’s placeholder text is no\n longer drawn twice when the panel is too\n narrow to fit it on one line.\n- In the Cockpit’s composer, Advanced Paste and\n Open Command History, which did nothing there,\n are now disabled, and its help no longer lists\n them or AI lookup.\n- Clicking the icon of the Current Directory,\n Hostname, or Username status bar component\n opens its menu again, as clicking its text\n does.\n- The folder icon in the Current Directory\n status bar component can no longer be dragged\n after its directory has been deleted.\n- With debug logging on, a password typed into\n a password field (for example, in the password\n manager) no longer appears in the debug log.\n- Fixed saving a window arrangement with “Save\n to File with Contents…” and archiving a\n session when the text contained hyperlinks,\n underline colors, similar styling, or\n right-to-left text. The arrangement file was\n never written, and the session archive was\n empty. If either kind of save fails, iTerm2\n now reports an error. Issue 13143.\n- Fixed a hang that could freeze iTerm2 with a\n beachball when GPU rendering was on and the\n system was under heavy load, for example from\n memory pressure. Issue 13139.\n- In the Claude Code Integration setup, clicking\n Show on the Show Toolbelt step more than once\n no longer darkens the window further with each\n click.\n- In tmux integration, showing timestamps beside\n the text in one pane of a window split into\n top and bottom panes no longer covers the end\n of that pane’s lines and leaves an empty strip\n at the right edge of the window.\n- With the Minimal or Compact theme and the\n Snippets tool enabled in the toolbelt, a window\n could be made wider but not narrower. Issue\n 13118.\n- When an app enables the Kitty keyboard\n protocol and “Use the Caps Lock key to switch\n to and from the last used Latin input source”\n is on, tapping Caps Lock to switch input\n sources no longer sends a malformed key report\n that some apps read as Control-C. Holding it to\n turn Caps Lock on or off now reports a Caps\n Lock press and release. Issue 13136.\n- The it2dl utility now works on systems where\n bash is not in /bin, such as Raspberry Pi OS.\n- With ligatures turned off, some fonts could\n still draw “fi” as a ligature and leave a gap\n after it, as in “confi g”. Issue 13133.\n- Turning off “Show activity indicator” in\n Settings > Appearance > Tabs now also hides\n the tab’s spinner when a program reports\n progress. Issue 13145.\n- Installing Shell Integration with “Download\n and Run Installer” installed outdated\n scripts, so the warning that this account’s\n Shell Integration scripts are out of date\n kept coming back. Issue 13155.\n- Fixed Shell Integration for xonsh, which was\n installed but never took effect. Installing\n it again fixes an existing installation.\n- Unpinning a pinned tab that was not the\n rightmost pinned tab, or one in a tab group, no\n longer breaks the tab bar (missing tabs, missing\n group headers, highlight on the wrong tab).\n Issue 13154.\n- Choosing the Make Hyperlink trigger action\n with interpolated string parameters turned on\n now fills in a working URL,\n https://\\(matches[0]). It previously filled in\n https://\\(match0), which refers to a variable\n that doesn’t exist.\n- Fixed a bug where a trigger whose interpolated\n parameter refers to its match, as in\n \\(matches[0]), could make output stall, and\n with tmux integration could freeze every tmux\n window. Triggers that use only matches[N] and\n line no longer wait on the session.\n- Fixed Kitty graphics images cropped to part of\n their height showing the wrong part of the\n image when GPU rendering is on. Multi-row\n images forwarded by some terminal multiplexers\n appeared with their rows in reverse order.\n Issue 13146.\n- Pressing an arrow key or Tab while composing\n text with an input method now finishes the\n character and performs the key in one press, as\n Return already did.\n- Opening a web browser session in iTerm2 Buddy\n now shows the web page instead of a black\n screen.\n- iTerm2 Buddy no longer connects slowly or\n keeps saying your Mac is busy while the Mac’s\n displays are asleep.\n- An AI chat started from a web browser session\n in iTerm2 Buddy is now linked to it as a web\n browser, not a terminal.\n- When AI chat asks to run a tool that can’t run\n in the linked session, the chat now reports\n the error and continues instead of waiting\n forever.\n- Window > Lock Location and the tab menu’s\n Rename Tab… item and “Tab Color:” label are\n now translated instead of always appearing in\n English.\n- The “AI-generated name” choice in Settings >\n Profiles > General > Title is now translated.\n- it2 session list no longer shows a session’s\n tty as \\/dev\\/ttys000 or its name with\n backslash escapes. It prints the plain values.\n- it2 session set-var now sets a plain value\n such as hello. Before, it reported success\n but set nothing unless the value was quoted\n JSON.\n- After window restoration, a tab whose session\n had a status (for example, one set with OSC\n 21337) shows it again in the tab bar, not\n only in the Session Status tool.\n- A trigger written by hand in a Dynamic Profile\n no longer forgets its state when the profile\n reloads, so an Instant trigger no longer fires\n a second time on the same line.\n- Uninstalling the Claude Code integration no\n longer says an empty settings.json couldn’t be\n parsed.\n- Installing the Claude Code integration when\n ~/.claude/settings.json is a broken symlink no\n longer leaves the new settings.json marked\n executable.\n- The Internet-Free shell integration install no\n longer hangs when the shell you’re using isn’t\n your login shell (for example, tcsh started\n from zsh). It installs for the shell you’re\n using.\n- An AI chat using a local Ollama model no\n longer sends its messages to your cloud AI\n provider after that model is removed from\n Ollama. It now shows an error, and you can\n pick another local model for the chat.\n- An alert shown again by “Permanently Forget\n Saved Selection” no longer presses a button\n when you type a plain letter. Its shortcuts\n need Cmd, as they did the first time.\n- Cmd-H, Cmd-Q, Cmd-W, and Cmd-M no longer press\n a button in an alert whose button happens to\n start with that letter, such as Help.\n- After you change a window’s style in Window >\n Window Style, for example to No Title Bar, the\n window keeps keyboard focus, so you can keep\n typing without clicking it first.\n- After Swap With Session between a tab with\n one pane and a split tab, each pane now shows\n or hides its title bar to match its new tab.\n- When tmux windows open as tabs in the\n attaching window, attaching from a hotkey\n window no longer moves the hotkey window to\n where tmux last had a window, often mostly off\n screen.\n- A profile setting chosen from a pop-up menu,\n such as the progress bar color scheme, now\n shows its default instead of a blank menu when\n the profile holds a value the menu lacks.\n- “Prefer integrated to discrete GPU” in\n Advanced GPU Settings is now disabled on a Mac\n with only one GPU, where it has no effect.\n- When pasting text with tabs, the prompt that\n offers to convert them to spaces now puts the\n cursor in its number-of-spaces field, so you\n can type a tab width without clicking first.\n- Shell > Close (⌘W) now closes the Cockpit when\n it is the key window. Before, the menu item\n was disabled there.\n- Advanced Paste now counts lines correctly.\n Before, text whose newlines were converted to\n carriage returns was reported as one line, and\n a trailing newline was counted as an extra\n line.\n- After you back out of the password manager\n from a website’s sign-in prompt in a browser\n session, “Remember this password” is no\n longer ticked unless you ticked it.\n- Session.async_move_to_new_tab() and\n async_move_to_new_window() in the Python API\n now return None for a tmux session instead of\n raising a JSON decoding error.\n- Calling Tab.async_update_layout() from the\n Python API without changing any sizes no\n longer shrinks the tab’s panes and window by\n a cell each time.\n- The banner shown when several nested SSH\n integration sessions end at once no longer\n ends with two periods.\n- The warning shown before opening an untrusted\n arrangement no longer counts a session’s\n command twice.\n- Typing quickly after a “Press any key to\n dismiss” message, such as the one shown when\n a program tries to clear scrollback, no\n longer loses the keys typed while the message\n slides away. Only the first key dismisses it.\n- With the Minimal theme, the git branch item in\n a workgroup’s toolbar draws its ahead and\n behind arrows in the toolbar’s text color\n instead of black on a dark terminal\n background.\n- In Low Power Mode, status bar graphs such as\n CPU Utilization move once a second instead of\n animating continuously.\n- After a screen lock ends password manager\n authentication, the trigger editor’s “Open\n Password Manager…” action no longer lists\n saved account names until you authenticate\n again.\n- Turning on generative AI while Companion\n Device Settings shows a failed pairing no\n longer erases the error and the QR code.\n- With Focus Follows Mouse, returning the\n pointer to a pane whose find field had focus\n no longer selects the whole query, so the next\n key you type doesn’t replace it.\n- With Focus Follows Mouse and “…even to views\n like Search or Composer” on, the status bar’s\n search field now takes focus when the pointer\n moves over it, like the find bar does.\n- The donation link in the Settings and Edit\n Session title bars no longer truncates the\n window title.\n- In the Add/Edit Manual AI Model sheet, the\n API key menu is no longer too narrow to show\n “Automatic (none for a local endpoint)”.\n- In Settings > Keys > Hotkey, the “App\n Hotkey:” and “Open Quickly Hotkey:” labels no\n longer run under the controls beside them.\n- The AI chat’s find bar no longer squeezes its\n search field to nothing in the inline chat or\n a narrow chat window. When space is short it\n hides the search mode menu and the match count\n instead.\n- Closing the AI chat’s find bar with Escape now\n returns focus to the message field, so what\n you type next is no longer lost.\n- Test Connection in the AI model editor can now\n be stopped while it runs. Before, a test of an\n unreachable server could take many minutes.\n- AI network errors now read “HTTP request\n failed: …” instead of a broken sentence, and\n errors from a self-hosted model name its\n server instead of “Unknown Platform”.\n- Test Connection in the AI model editor now\n rejects custom headers that are not valid, as\n Save does, instead of leaving them out and\n reporting success.\n- The AI Usage toolbar item no longer shows\n “Unreadable usage data” when Claude Code’s\n output contains color codes or other control\n characters.\n- Changing an AI chat permission from the info\n menu now updates the permission buttons shown\n in the chat right away.\n- In an orchestration chat, the notice shown\n when mentioning a session now shows names with\n underscores correctly.\n- AI command suggestions no longer repeat a\n directory you already typed in the filename\n they insert (for example, “rm logs/logs/\n error.log”).\n- AI models on Azure OpenAI in Azure Government\n (.azure.us) and Azure China (.azure.cn) now\n send their API key the way Azure expects.\n- Look Up (Ctrl-Cmd-D) on a right-to-left line\n now highlights the word where it is drawn and\n shows its definition next to it, instead of\n over blank cells at the far side of the line.\n- With the GPU renderer off, underlines and\n strikethroughs on right-to-left text are now\n drawn under the text they belong to instead\n of a few cells to the right.\n- With the GPU renderer off, Devanagari and\n other scripts with vowel signs written before\n their consonant draw the sign in the right\n place again: क्षत्रिय no longer shows ि after त्र.\n- In Copy Mode, the cursor on a right-to-left\n line is now drawn on the character it is on,\n instead of in the blank area on the other side\n of the line.\n- A scroll region set by a program that is\n still running when iTerm2 quits unexpectedly\n is kept when the session is restored, so the\n program’s output no longer scrolls the whole\n screen.\n- Undo Close of a maximized floating pane now\n brings it back maximized, and unmaximizing it\n returns it to where it was before.\n- Docking a floating pane by dragging it onto a\n pane with Control held now keeps keyboard focus\n in the docked pane when other floating panes\n are open.\n- In Swap With Session, pointing at a floating\n pane no longer also highlights the pane under\n it as a swap target.\n- In tmux integration, resizing a floating pane\n with View > Restore Text and Session Size or\n the Python API now resizes it in tmux too. It\n used to leave the pane blank or out of sync\n with tmux.\n- Window > Arrangements > Change Profile in\n Arrangement now works for a floating pane\n restored from a saved arrangement.\n- Hidden floating panes no longer count toward\n the number of panes that turns off the GPU\n renderer in a tab.\n- In tmux integration, a floating pane over pane\n %0 is no longer drawn over a different pane\n while Find > Filter is open.\n- A pane in a Workgroup can no longer be turned\n into a floating pane by dragging it onto a\n floating pane in another tab.\n- In tmux integration, the ReportCellSize,\n ReportWindowFrame, and ReportScreenFrames\n escape sequences now get a reply.\n- With AI command suggestions on, a filename\n row in the composer’s suggestion list no\n longer shows extra backslashes before\n spaces and other special characters.\n- With Archive On Closure on, closing a pane in\n a tmux integration window no longer saves an\n archive of it that could not be restored.\n- Dragging the folder icon in the Current\n Directory status bar component when its\n directory can’t be dragged (for example, on a\n remote host) no longer opens the component’s\n menu when you release the mouse.\n- When a session is restored with the “Session\n Contents Restored” banner, the shell’s output\n now starts right below the banner instead of\n after a blank row when the cursor had been in\n the middle of a line.\n- Resizing panes with Tab.async_update_layout()\n in the Python API now gives each pane the size\n you asked for. A pane used to come up a column\n or row short and the window shrank a little\n each time.\n- With the GPU renderer off, selecting part of\n an Arabic or Persian word, or a find match\n inside one, no longer breaks the letters’\n joining at the edge of the highlight.\n- it2 app get-focus, it2 profile list and it2\n profile show no longer print names, fonts and\n badge text with backslash escapes, such as a\n profile named a/b shown as a\\/b.\n- Kitty graphics placeholder cells that show a\n cropped part of an image now draw that part.\n Before, a crop could draw nothing, or a strip\n of the neighboring part of the image could\n appear at its edge.\n- An SSH Integration session restored after\n relaunching iTerm2 no longer hangs when its\n connection drops while the remote command is\n printing output. It returns to the local\n shell.\n- In AI Chat, a session mention in the message\n field now changes size along with the text\n when you change the font size from the Font\n menu or Font panel.\n- In the AI Chats window, when sending a message\n moves a chat to the top of the list, the\n highlight now moves with it instead of\n staying on a different chat.\n- An AI chat using a model you added in\n Settings > General > AI (for example, one on\n a local server) no longer sends its messages\n to OpenAI after you remove or rename that\n model. It now shows an error.\n- VoiceOver now reads the Profiles window’s\n New Floating Pane button as “New Floating\n Pane” instead of the name of its icon.\n- The browser’s page-load error page is now\n fully translated (its “Try Again” button and\n footer were in English), as is the page shown\n for an ssh: link with no ssh integration\n connection.\n- A web address containing a quote or other\n special characters can no longer inject script\n or markup into the browser’s page-load error\n page.\n- When several Dynamic Profiles files have\n errors, the alert now gives the right number\n of additional errors in the log. It counted\n each bad file twice.\n- With Focus Follows Mouse on, if you move the\n pointer into another split pane while a sheet\n such as Edit Tab Title is open, that pane now\n gets focus when the sheet closes. Before, it\n stayed with the original pane until the\n pointer left and came back.\n- AI custom headers you remove from a model no\n longer come back after launching iTerm2 on\n another Mac that shares your settings.\n- Changing your AI models in Settings when you\n used a single custom AI model no longer drops\n the custom headers you set up for it.\n- The AI Usage toolbar item’s problem report no\n longer fills the screen with the output of a\n usage command that printed too much, which\n left its buttons out of reach.\n- Searching in the AI Chats window no longer\n crashes iTerm2 when the search finds a match.\n- The setting and menu item pickers in the key\n binding editor now say “Select Setting…” or\n “Select Menu Item…” until you choose one.\n Before, they were blank.\n- Advanced Paste’s chunk size and delay\n values and the sizes in Paste History now use\n units in your language instead of English.\n- In some languages, the shell integration\n installer no longer cuts off the option to\n install the iTerm2 Utilities or its\n description.\n- The browser’s View Source command now shows\n the page’s source. Before, it always said\n “No source available.”\n- When you change the theme, an open browser\n tab now switches pages that have light and\n dark versions to the matching one instead of\n keeping the old look until you open a new tab.\n- In some languages, the shell integration\n installer no longer cuts off the titles of\n its install choice buttons or its Skip,\n Cancel, and Continue buttons.\n- In Russian, the shell integration installer\n no longer cuts off its Preview Command\n button.\n- Right-clicking in a browser tab that a page\n opened from a link now shows the context\n menu.\n- In a browser tab that a page opened from a\n link, find, copy on select, hovered links,\n autofill, the password manager, and named\n marks now work on that tab instead of the\n tab that opened it.\n- In AI chat, if you send a new message instead\n of answering a request to run a command, the\n AI is no longer told that iTerm2 restarted and\n invited to run the command again.\n- With the kitty keyboard protocol reporting\n all keys, a dead key followed by a key it does\n not combine with now reports the dead key and\n then the second key, instead of the second key\n twice.\n- In the Minimal theme, a window opened while\n iTerm2 is running now shows the profile’s\n subtitle in its title bar instead of only\n after the window is resized.\n- When settings load from a custom folder and\n are saved manually, the red “!” in the\n Settings title bar now goes away after you\n click Save Now.\n- Importing a script archive whose signing\n certificate is damaged now shows an error\n instead of crashing iTerm2.\n- Undo Close of a floating pane now puts it\n back at its old place among the other floating\n panes, so a maximized one no longer comes back\n in front of and hiding the others.\n- Help > GPU Renderer Availability now explains\n why GPU rendering is off in a complete\n sentence.\n- Cmd-hover and Cmd-click now find URLs on\n right-to-left lines that run up to a pane\n divider, such as in a tmux split without tmux\n integration.\n- Cmd-hover now underlines a URL that ends in\n right-to-left text wherever the pointer is on\n it, instead of leaving the previous URL\n underlined.\n- Command Info no longer shows a negative\n running time such as “0:-1.999” for a command\n that finishes quickly.\n- The context menu of a tab group’s label now\n matches the window’s light or dark appearance,\n like a tab’s context menu, when it differs\n from the system’s.\n- With HTML tab titles on, “&” and “<”\n in a title are now shown as “&” and “ Pointer, “…even to views like\n Search or Composer” is now dimmed when Focus\n Follows Mouse is off from the moment the pane\n opens, not only after toggling Focus Follows\n Mouse.\n- With the Settings window in front of a\n terminal window, Edit > Find > Find… now\n focuses the Settings search field instead of\n opening the find bar in the terminal behind it.\n- Command Info’s Output size no longer counts\n the next prompt or the command typed at it,\n and is no longer too large for a command that\n has scrolled into history.\n- Command Info’s Output size no longer counts\n the “%” marker and padding zsh adds after\n output that doesn’t end in a newline.\n- After Session.async_split_pane() in the Python\n API, calling Tab.async_update_layout() without\n changing any sizes no longer grows the\n original pane back to its old width.\n- In AI Chat with streaming responses on, a\n code block at the end of a reply now shows as\n code with a copy button instead of as large\n text or with a stray closing ```.\n- In AI Chat, a mention of a web browser session\n now shows a globe icon instead of a terminal\n icon, both in the message field and in sent\n messages.\n- The Internet-Free shell integration install\n no longer sometimes hangs while installing\n the utilities, waiting at a “> ” prompt.\n- The Internet-Free shell integration install\n for bash could finish without adding the line\n that loads shell integration to your\n .profile or .bash_profile.\n- The Internet-Free shell integration install\n no longer reports that it installed the\n utilities when they failed to unpack, no\n longer garbles them on a busy computer, and\n no longer types them into the shell as\n commands when the ~/.iterm2 folder is not\n writable.\n- Canceling the Internet-Free shell integration\n install while it sends the utilities now\n stops sending them right away instead of a\n minute or more later. Canceling a step or\n clicking Send Again now returns you to your\n own shell instead of leaving you inside the\n installer’s helper shells at a “>> ” prompt,\n even when your shell is slow to start them.\n If you are still in one when you try again,\n the installer says so instead of reporting\n that you use bash.\n- An scp download that asks for a password no\n longer hangs if you open the Password Manager\n and close it without choosing a password.\n- An upload over SSH Integration no longer\n overwrites a remote file of the same name when\n iTerm2 could not check whether one exists, for\n example because of a permission error or a\n connection problem. The upload now fails with\n an error instead.\n- When an administrator turns off generative AI,\n iTerm2 no longer uses Apple Intelligence for\n AI chat, AI tab titles, or the command safety\n check.\n- The Internet-Free shell integration install\n no longer unpacks the utilities into the\n current folder when it can’t open the\n ~/.iterm2 folder, as when a file has that\n name. It says they were not installed.\n- Search the Web for Selection now finds text\n that contains a plus sign. Before, “c++” was\n searched for as “c” followed by spaces.\n- The “Learn more.” links on the first page of\n the shell integration installer now use the\n same font as the text around them.\n- With AI command suggestions off, the gray\n completion the composer shows for a file name\n with a space or other special character is\n now escaped, so accepting it gives a working\n command.\n- The browser’s View Source now highlights\n attribute values.\n- With SSH Integration, the Composer now\n suggests remote file names when the remote\n host has Python 3.7 or 3.8.\n- With SSH Integration, a remote request that\n fails unexpectedly, such as a file operation,\n now reports an error instead of never\n finishing.\n- With SSH Integration, asking for an immediate\n update of the remote job list, as the Job\n status bar component can, no longer ends the\n integration for that session.\n- With SSH Integration to a Mac, output that a\n remote command printed just before it exited\n could be lost.\n- With SSH Integration, ending a connection\n while a remote file search was running could\n leave iTerm2’s helper program running on the\n remote host.\n- With SSH Integration, deleting a remote\n symbolic link to a folder now deletes the link\n instead of failing.\n- With SSH Integration, iTerm2 now reads the\n remote PATH correctly when it contains runs of\n spaces or wildcard characters, and when the\n remote login shell is one iTerm2 doesn’t\n recognize.\n- With SSH Integration, remote files and folders\n whose names contain non-ASCII characters, such\n as accented letters, can now be downloaded,\n deleted, renamed, and otherwise changed.\n Before, they were reported as missing.\n- With SSH Integration, restoring a session that\n had used ssh to reach a further host now works\n when that ssh command has non-ASCII characters.\n- With SSH Integration, non-ASCII text that\n iTerm2 reads from the remote host, such as a\n remote PATH containing accented letters, is no\n longer garbled.\n- With SSH Integration, after iTerm2 restarts\n or recovers a connection, it no longer waits\n for the remote host to print something before\n remote features start working again.\n- Answering Always to “Close tmux windows after\n detaching?” no longer closes sessions that did\n not ask, such as the tmux session itself.\n- Announcements in narrow panes are legible: when\n there is no room beside the buttons, the\n buttons go on a row below the text.\n- Shell > ssh > Download Files… now lets you\n choose any file. Before, it only allowed file\n types that the AI provider accepts as\n attachments.\n- Installing the iTerm2 utility scripts on Linux\n no longer prints tar warnings or leaves ._\n files in ~/.iterm2.\n- When a session’s program ends and iTerm2 offers\n to restart it, short output such as a custom\n command’s is no longer hidden behind the offer.\n- The Downloads and Uploads menus now show\n their translated title in the menu bar, and\n the size of each file (such as “1.2 MB of\n 20 MB”) is now translated.\n- Answering No to “Allow Terminal-Initiated\n Download?” (for example, for it2dl) no longer\n fills the terminal with the file’s encoded\n contents. The same goes for declining to\n display a file inline.\n- iTerm2 no longer crashes or stops drawing\n terminal contents if a display reports a\n refresh rate of 0.\n- Fixed a hang of about a second each time\n Dynamic Profiles were reloaded when there were\n hundreds of them. Issue 13174.\n- Choosing Paste as Text for a pasted or dropped\n file and then answering the paste confirmation\n no longer freezes the window.\n- Some apps that ask to be told about mouse\n movement, such as those built with Lanterna,\n received clicks but not movement. Issue 13184.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\n549eb2b21464d7f4606165e51c36a6b1a252ac9a79958228a240152412759da2\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmrqkVMbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bEWAP/0M27MrUgucCzNCAi7Yr\nBnC2AjC3ROVccQk+0QesIYwVIE6EAL6u9u40S0OALc4TjkyG+JMcF9kv2JwPysy/\nJZrc0HV26oSpB3UMZ4u2FI5CJAIrpzQG6/Nba5uyVJaRYbuuq/xCgO62pdx+S8Zc\nk69A3Na2TeSpbiAIoKEUSzA1lKJYkJn/ziHbqwZ4FU95v7qSiDwwE9HtHy0Rr61z\n9QF+y+tLlmlnUgif2lVq8o6fve1E3ztJm0GC5/7C+PGzYBRJyu9ifSZlYZZHvGpu\n0MG8CBxwXxAercF2b1zVrRWdmAyy1tdNgIeaU3ksdDUXodTdrIxqct8meLifb33B\nDxxT+XEd0JjyBIki8OeyNl8dJh6Onjz5RABR86mCEnEDs8z0OvLtrn+I08DHOV8E\nKdQW2dhrBy2W/9Heil5xfqWHwaENCqW56/J6XdxYD58YF5wZSu1KBgi7Et51TiIw\n1mW7uNLs0kTL+g13awIc4yrjfoqJ6jbllXa28J63YCqSC/Oo68LqHJWHwdqH8HNL\nJvWXZyBJN7rOy/vObAGRuK4Q9irDbdqMvWnAiryqr1dacXk5xkin95CH9h+3X6Br\nTN9KvdjdyL4lWUT4Y9BArOpRCk4Dxk03CxKrHfAY805ur9QlO1CGCmUsmVcEAKS1\ntcug0zNcnq3j7a7gqrms3Lqx\n=ch5Q\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n ▸ Show Older Versions \n\n\n ▾ Hide Older Versions \n Older Stable Releases \n\n iTerm2 3.7.6 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on October 15, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.6 of iTerm2 was built on October 15, 2026.\n\nVersion 3.7.5 was accidentally built without the\nbug fixes below. This release includes them.\n\nImprovements:\n- The Companion setup wizard no longer asks for an\n API key when AI already works, or when you chose\n a model manually in Settings > General > AI. If\n that model isn’t set up yet, the wizard says so\n and lets you finish setup anyway.\n- AI API keys are now sent automatically only to\n their own provider’s servers. Before, the\n Automatic API key choice could send your OpenAI\n key to a gateway such as OpenRouter or a company\n gateway hosted on Azure. Models you already set\n up keep sending the key they sent before.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed crashes when a Set Profile Setting trigger\n fired, when a trigger had no regex (such as an\n event trigger written by hand in a Dynamic\n Profile), when the bell rang in a profile whose\n Bell Sound wasn’t a sound name, and when using a\n tmux integration window that was left open after\n detaching.\n- Answering “Always” to “Close tmux windows after\n detaching?” now closes only the tmux integration\n windows left open by the detach. It used to\n close every session in the app.\n- When an administrator turns off generative AI,\n AI chat timers and watches no longer send\n anything to the AI model, and Edit > Engage\n Artificial Intelligence in the Composer shows\n the lockout alert instead of sending your text.\n- Debug logs no longer include AI API keys or the\n values of custom HTTP headers configured for AI\n models.\n- Command safety checks that use a cheaper model\n now keep a manually configured model’s custom\n headers, API key choice, and model name. Before,\n they could send the request with a different key\n or ask for a model your endpoint doesn’t have.\n- Choosing a Preset in the manual AI model editor\n now also resets its custom headers and API key\n choice.\n- Stopping an AI chat reply while another chat is\n also replying no longer cancels the other chat’s\n reply, and the stopped reply now really stops\n downloading instead of running (and using up\n tokens) until it finishes.\n- Stopping an AI chat reply and then sending\n another message no longer shows the rest of the\n stopped reply as the answer to the new message.\n- AI chats now keep their session links, watches,\n and timers across a quit when window restoration\n brings the sessions back, including watches on\n tmux integration panes. Timers no longer fire\n before windows are restored, and unlinking a\n chat from its session no longer cancels its\n timers.\n- Editing or deleting an AI chat message now fully\n removes the later conversation, so the AI no\n longer sees leftover command results from it,\n and a message you deleted while the AI was still\n replying is no longer sent.\n- Fixed Notify on Status Change reporting that a\n session changed from “none” when its status had\n already been set before you turned the\n notification on, and alerting when a program\n merely restated that status.\n- The Prevent Sleep setting in Settings > Profiles\n > Session now takes effect when you click it,\n and keeps preventing sleep after Session >\n Restart Session or Undo Close.\n- A program that sends a flood of status reports\n (OSC 7501) no longer makes iTerm2 fall far\n behind and use a lot of memory.\n- The AI Usage toolbar item now finds the claude\n command when iTerm2 was opened from the Dock,\n uses the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set in your shell,\n and no longer uses gigabytes of memory when a\n custom usage command prints too much.\n- Installing or reinstalling the Claude Code\n Integration no longer replaces a Claude Code\n settings.json that has a syntax error, which\n erased all your other Claude Code settings. It\n now leaves the file alone and asks you to fix it\n and try again. The integration’s hook is also no\n longer removed when CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR names\n ~/.claude in a different way.\n- Fixed restored sessions bringing lines that had\n been erased by a clear screen back onto the\n screen, above the newer output, with the cursor\n in the wrong place.\n- When an SSH Integration connection dropped, for\n example because the ssh process or the remote\n server was killed, the session hung: nothing you\n typed afterward appeared. It now ends the\n connection and returns to the local shell.\n- In a session that ssh’ed to another machine\n without SSH Integration, the current directory\n (and paths used by Upload and Paste Path) no\n longer switches to the local Mac’s directory\n after you run a command.\n- Uploading several files that have the same name\n in one SSH Integration paste now keeps all of\n them instead of overwriting one with another.\n- The Internet-Free Install of shell integration\n no longer gets stuck at its first step for zsh\n users, and that step can no longer be skipped,\n which made later steps write to an invalid path.\n- Cockpit fixes: Opt-Return and Opt-Shift-Return\n now send instead of erasing what you typed, only\n the selected text is sent when there is a\n selection, the caret and chips no longer jump or\n change when a session’s status changes, and text\n on a line with an @-mention chip is no longer\n misaligned.\n- Floating pane fixes: Undo Close now restores a\n floating pane even after its undo timeout,\n reattaching to a tmux session no longer moves\n floats that sit beyond column 80, and a native\n session is never put into a tmux integration tab\n as a floating pane.\n- In tmux integration, a layout change made by\n tmux while Find > Filter was open on a pane no\n longer hides pane %0 or moves keyboard focus\n away from the filtered pane.\n- Password manager fixes: in “Require\n Authentication on Every Open” mode it now asks\n every time it opens, including the standalone\n window, shows no accounts until you\n authenticate, and shows the website login sheet\n instead of a system prompt for each login. In\n “Once per Launch and after Screen Locks” mode,\n locking the screen now locks it again even when\n it was only unlocked to save a website password.\n- Fixed a bug where turning on “Use AI to check\n safety of commands before running them” on a Mac\n without Apple Intelligence was turned back off\n the first time you used AI chat, which then\n asked whether to enable it again.\n- With macOS’s “Add period with double-space” on,\n typing two spaces in the terminal after using\n the Composer no longer types “. ”.\n- Move Tab Left and Move Tab Right no longer unpin\n a pinned tab and put it into a neighboring tab\n group.\n- Session Status now follows a session rename.\n- `it2 session list --format` now works. The\n bundled it2 was out of date.\n- in an AI command suggestion now expands\n to the whole filename instead of the part you\n hadn’t typed yet.\n- With the GPU renderer off, a color change in the\n middle of a right-to-left word no longer paints\n the whole word in one color.\n- When no valid UNIX locale matches your language\n and region, the prompt to pick one now appears\n as a normal dialog the first time a hotkey\n window opens, instead of as a sheet on the\n not-yet-visible window.\n- Closing the Companion setup wizard while it is\n installing now cancels setup. Before, it kept\n going, asked for an administrator password with\n no explanation, and reopened the wizard if you\n declined. Cmd-W now closes the wizard instead of\n a terminal session.\n- Finder’s Get Info now shows iTerm2’s real\n version in every language instead of “3.7.dev”.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\nbb6102e9f57bbe41979ba7c7b1c25d4112e9a5c2142606eeccbe18dc7ee0706f\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmrRZQUbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bpW8QAKZji7qrzN7iwuoBkZqU\nBLxEdITDK31RaPDNj4UkohWD/SlCbaovD8wHUlB9LrN6RIBG9PMIqajZP7HNkW41\nR9faCEHnmBysoI058ubtkf6sN9I3qK2pKnoDnOwgtLD5X7BcSjSzNJG8Q9oAXRy8\nxfc73Agffi50IpSODb/XcjxhaUP9MDTmRwNL3bcJ8E4X5DAjQbWZ/Ot8o1gr/WDl\nmPkJLo9zXME+CzVFOkGB7JK48TJnuO5rb2MJZY8jWPeEXa9OxAs2jec1GTXYT9mC\nyEjRiYCkfUv88XHUnmKoUaXi78wWXPW5oNz8qMqQDSwnb/O6duaQ6pKJTRxXgiTs\nPpR6BoInrCWlhTZXGeBowan4Spm/Bxex7ypAcyp8d2ArH/AAKR6gltZGOxODTSJm\nof4gGqQORZPbduxn1CIarDWNeFH317py7PcKph/RbWsSHaeE+0dMo/BPrB93Sar3\nMjJIHPAQtj1PiWRwPiTdxDUr3GlTiIGwCowCUZU3zqZ8v9wbaSoKjjUEoFEzMZvL\nlhbDGqzIfuGa12VGVifGAgIvAhIjU6JJh6kCgYieYFuKkL22BmxtDASjlR23JeYJ\nxHaRWbzXjS+pS0GQ/jePPzrpdl8XhqGqXghXeGvKQLRbvCbLTlwkFbKLmhmuC42d\nZsTOO/Lk6XE7tCgDDSA7hr3a\n=eNYL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.5 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on October 14, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.5 was built on October 14, 2026.\n\nCredit to Nimanou (nuved) for the right-to-left\nrendering, selection, and copy improvements in\nthis release.\n\nMajor New Features:\n- Floating Panes: a pane that floats over a tab’s\n split panes. Create one with Shell > New\n Floating Pane with Current Profile, or with a\n key binding or pointer action. Drag its title\n bar to move it and its edges to resize it. Hold\n Control while dragging to dock it into the split\n panes or move it to another tab or window.\n Window > Floating Panes can raise, dock, move\n and hide them. They are saved with window\n arrangements. tmux integration shows tmux’s own\n floating panes (tmux 3.7 and later; moving,\n resizing, raising and docking them needs tmux\n 3.8).\n- iTerm2 is now localized: the interface follows\n your Mac’s language and is available in\n Brazilian Portuguese, German, Japanese, Russian,\n and Simplified Chinese.\n- Support for the Program Status Protocol (OSC\n 7501), which lets a program report that it is\n working, waiting on you, done, or failed. The\n tab shows the most urgent status the program\n reported, along with its message and progress. A\n program reporting on several tasks at once gets\n them listed under its session in the Session\n Status tool, and each task that needs you can\n post its own notification.\n- Session Status has new “done” and “error”\n statuses for work that finished or failed while\n you were away. They sort just below “waiting”,\n above “working”, and “idle” now sorts last.\n Pressing a key in the session marks the result\n as seen and turns it idle. Claude Code now\n reports a finished turn as “done” and an API\n error as “error”. Customized priority lists get\n the two new statuses added below “waiting”,\n notifying if “idle” did.\n\nNew Features:\n- Ollama is now a first-class AI provider\n alongside OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, and\n DeepSeek. Choose it in Settings > General > AI\n and iTerm2 lists the models installed on your\n local Ollama server, reading each model’s\n context window and capabilities (thinking,\n tools, vision) from the server, so there is\n nothing to configure. You can pick one model for\n chats and a smaller one for quick background\n tasks. For a server on another machine, use the\n new “Ollama (auto-discover)” or “Ollama\n (native)” presets, or the manual model editor’s\n new Fetch Models button and Vision checkbox. The\n native API type, formerly named “Llama”, is now\n named “Ollama”; it sizes the context window to\n your prompt so long prompts are no longer\n silently truncated, and the chat’s Think toggle\n now controls whether a reasoning model thinks.\n- ⌘Z now undoes text editing in the field you’re\n editing (the composer, browser address bar,\n annotations, notes, and AI chat), and ⌘⇧Z\n redoes. Undo Close is now solely Shell ▸ Undo\n Close (⌘⇧T). The first time you press ⌘Z with a\n recently closed session still restorable, a\n one-time notice explains the change and lets you\n choose Undo Close or a regular undo.\n- The Session Status tool can now follow Codex CLI\n as well as Claude Code. There is no installer\n for Codex yet: in ~/.codex/hooks.json, point\n each hook event at a script that runs the\n cc-status tool from the utilities folder inside\n iTerm2.app’s Resources with --agent codex, and\n set timeout to 3 on the Interrupt and SessionEnd\n hooks. The tab then shows Codex’s status and a\n progress indicator through each turn. Codex also\n reports a finished turn as “done” and an API\n error as “error”.\n- The Cockpit can now send a command to many\n sessions at once from a composer docked along\n its bottom. Type @ to pick a session, tab, or\n window (selecting rows in the list does the\n same), then type the command and press\n Shift-Return to send it to every chosen pane.\n The Cockpit also now shows each session’s live\n status and detail line in every grouping, and a\n filter bar along the top narrows the list to one\n status.\n- A new “Set Profile Setting” trigger action turns\n a profile checkbox on or off for the session.\n Paired with Job Started and Job Ended matches it\n gives declarative “while this job runs” logic.\n- A new “Prevent sleep” profile setting keeps the\n Mac from idle-sleeping while a session with the\n setting enabled exists. Combine it with the new\n “Set Profile Setting” trigger action to keep the\n Mac awake only while a long-running job, like an\n AI coding agent, runs. It applies only while the\n Mac is on AC power.\n- AI chat can now set a timer to act after a delay\n or at a set time (for example, “in two hours,\n run this command”). The timer survives restarts,\n fires immediately if its time already passed,\n and can push your phone when it fires.\n- You can now press Cmd-F in an AI chat to search\n the current conversation. A find bar appears at\n the top with match counts and next/previous\n navigation; matches are highlighted in place.\n Supports smart-case, case-sensitive, and regular\n expression modes.\n- Right-clicking your own message in AI chat now\n offers Delete, which removes that message and\n everything after it. Edit, Fork, and Delete are\n also available when right-clicking inside the\n message text. Editing or deleting a message now\n also syncs to a paired iPhone viewing the same\n chat (and the phone can delete messages too).\n- New OSC 1337 codes let a program move and resize\n its window across monitors using global\n coordinates: SetWindowFrame=x;y;w;h sets the\n frame, while ReportWindowFrame and\n ReportScreenFrames report the current frame and\n the layout of all displays. Unlike the older CSI\n 3;x;y t sequence, these are not limited to the\n monitor the window is already on.\n- The OSC 1337 SetColors escape sequence now\n accepts a palette index as the value, written\n i:N where N is 0-255. Indices 0-15 follow the\n loaded color preset and light or dark mode, so a\n program can set the badge or tab color to track\n the preset instead of hardcoding an RGB value.\n For example, SetColors=badge=i:3 uses ANSI\n yellow. Issue 12991.\n- iTerm2 now answers CSI 16 t (report character\n cell size), which was previously ignored.\n- Each profile can now choose what its bell sounds\n like, in Settings > Profiles > Terminal > Bell\n sound. Pick one of the sounds installed on your\n Mac or choose “Other…” for any audio file of\n your own, and give noisy sessions a bell you can\n tell apart from the rest.\n- There is a new “Centered, No Title Bar” window\n style, in Settings > Profiles > Window > Style\n and in the Window > Window Style menu. It stays\n centered on the screen like the Centered style\n but has no title bar or window controls, which\n is how Centered windows looked before 3.7.0.\n- Window > Lock Location pins a window to its\n current display and position. If anything moves\n the window it goes right back, and if its\n display is disconnected, as happens while a\n laptop sleeps, the window returns when the\n display comes back. Dragging the window turns\n the lock off.\n- The built-in web browser can now remember HTTP\n basic-auth user names and passwords. Check\n “Remember this password” in the sign-in dialog\n and it is saved in the browser password manager\n (Keychain, 1Password, and the other supported\n back ends) and offered automatically next time.\n The dialog also has a Password Manager button to\n pick an existing login. View or remove saved\n passwords from the password manager.\n- The Compact and Minimal themes, which have no\n title bar, can now show the window name beside\n the tabs. It appears once you name a window with\n Window > Edit Window Title, so windows you\n haven’t named look the same as before.\n Double-click the name to rename the window.\n- The workgroup toolbar can show an AI Usage gauge\n with your Claude subscription’s session and\n weekly usage as progress bars. Add it to a\n session’s toolbar in the workgroup settings and\n choose how often it refreshes; it reads the\n numbers by running the Claude Code command-line\n tool. You can point it at your own command\n instead.\n- New variables iterm2.workingSessionCount and\n iterm2.waitingSessionCount count the sessions\n whose status reads “working” or “waiting”. Use\n them anywhere interpolated strings work, such as\n badges, tab titles, and the status bar.\n- New setting in Settings > Appearance > General\n shows an iTerm2 icon in the menu bar, optionally\n followed by an interpolated string such as\n \\(iterm2.workingSessionCount).\n- New per-profile setting “Use HDR cursor when\n available” in Settings > Profiles > Colors. When\n on, a white cursor renders brighter than white\n on an HDR-capable display, with either the GPU\n or the legacy renderer, and a slider sets how\n bright it gets.\n- Apps can now control right-to-left text\n reordering with the BDSM and SCP escape\n sequences. CSI 8 l enters explicit mode, in\n which text is drawn exactly as written, and CSI\n 8 h returns to implicit mode. This keeps\n full-screen apps that lay out their own text\n from being scrambled. CSI 1 SP k and CSI 2 SP k\n make reordered lines left-to-right or\n right-to-left, and CSI 0 SP k restores the\n default.\n- it2 can now target the session showing a\n particular tmux pane. From inside a tmux\n Integration pane, pass --tmux \"$TMUX\"\n --tmux-pane \"$TMUX_PANE\" to session set-status,\n get-status, set-var or get-var. This also works\n on a host reached through SSH Integration.\n Claude Code’s status is no longer reported from\n a pane of a plain tmux session (one not using\n tmux Integration), since iTerm2 can’t tell which\n tab, if any, is showing that pane.\n- New `it2 session get-status` prints a session’s\n status indicator as JSON. `set-status` no longer\n requires --session and defaults to the active\n session.\n- A session status can now say when it stops being\n true. With it2 set-status --expires-on, a\n program gives the status to switch to once the\n work it describes is over, so a status doesn’t\n outlive a program that was stopped partway\n through.\n- The it2 command-line tool can now send a push\n notification to your paired companion device\n with “it2 notify «title» «body»”.\n\nImprovements:\n- A warning that iTerm2 shows while you are away\n from your Mac no longer cuts off your paired\n iPhone. The iTerm2 Buddy app stays connected,\n shows the warning, and lets you answer it,\n including “don’t ask again” and password prompts\n (a password manager’s master password, an scp\n password). Other kinds of dialogs can’t be\n answered from the phone, but it now says the Mac\n is waiting instead of appearing disconnected.\n Requires an up-to-date iTerm2 Buddy.\n- Pairing a companion iPhone no longer requires\n AI. The setup wizard (iTerm2 > Companion Device\n Settings) now lets you choose “Terminal viewing\n & control only”, so you can browse sessions,\n watch live output, and type from your phone\n without an AI plugin or API key. Chat features\n appear disabled with an explanation until you\n turn AI on.\n- If a profile archives sessions when they close\n (Settings > Profiles > Session), Undo Close\n keeps working after its time limit runs out.\n Archived sessions come back from their archives\n in their original split panes, tabs, and\n windows. The last 20 can be undone even after\n restarting iTerm2.\n- iTerm2 now recognizes when a terminal is running\n on your own Mac by machine identity rather than\n by hostname, so features that only work locally\n keep working over a VPN or Tailscale connection\n where the hostname no longer matches. Reinstall\n shell integration (iTerm2 > Install Shell\n Integration) to enable this.\n- The AI timeout is now an idle timeout (the\n maximum gap between bytes received) rather than\n a cap on the total time a request may take. A\n slow but healthy generation, such as a reasoning\n model that is still thinking or a cold local\n model, keeps running as long as it makes\n progress, while a dead connection still fails.\n The default was also raised to 20 minutes. This\n fixes spurious timeouts against local Ollama\n servers.\n- The manual AI model editor can now set custom\n HTTP headers and an API key for each model, so\n an authenticated self-hosted or gateway endpoint\n gets its own Authorization header and key.\n Existing global headers are copied to your saved\n models. The new API key setting can send a\n provider’s stored key, a key entered just for\n that model, or no key. The default, Automatic,\n shows which key it picked and sends a provider’s\n key only to that provider’s own servers, so a\n gateway such as OpenRouter no longer receives\n your OpenAI key unless you choose it. Models you\n already set up keep sending the key they sent\n before. Issues 12975 and 13105.\n- Hidden hotkey windows no longer redraw at the\n full active cadence. Sessions with continuous\n output (builds, tails, agents) in a hidden\n hotkey window used to burn CPU and GPU rendering\n frames nobody could see, spinning fans on\n laptops. Hidden windows now drop to the\n background refresh rate and repaint the instant\n they are revealed.\n- iTerm2 no longer gradually slows down when left\n running for days. The Snippets and Jobs tools no\n longer rebuild their lists every time you switch\n tabs, and the Script Console now keeps only the\n 200 most recent finished scripts and API\n connections; tools that connect once per\n command, like it2, had piled up thousands of\n them.\n- Reduced idle CPU usage that previously grew with\n the number of open windows.\n- Typing in a session connected with SSH\n Integration is more responsive on slow or\n distant connections. When you typed faster than\n the network round trip, each keystroke waited\n for the previous one to be acknowledged before\n it was sent. Issue 13013.\n- The session list in iTerm2 Buddy now updates\n live as sessions are opened, closed, or renamed\n on the Mac. It used to change only when you\n reopened it or pulled to refresh. Requires an\n up-to-date iTerm2 Buddy.\n- The built-in AI model list is up to date. It\n adds GPT-6 Astra, GPT-6.1 Sol, GPT-6 Sol, GPT-6\n Luna, GPT-5.4 Pro, GPT-5.4 mini, Gemini 3.8\n Flash, DeepSeek Flash (V4.1), Claude Opus 5.5,\n Claude Sonnet 5.5, Claude Fable 5.1, Claude Opus\n 5, and Claude Fable 5, and the recommended model\n for each provider is now its newest flagship.\n Newer Claude models also get their full 1\n million token context window. o4-mini, which\n OpenAI is shutting down, has been removed.\n- The Move Tab Left and Move Tab Right shortcuts\n now move just the current tab instead of its\n whole tab group. Moving a tab past the edge of\n its group pops it out of the group, and moving\n an ungrouped tab into a neighboring group adds\n it to that group.\n- Dragging a tab into a collapsed tab group now\n adds it to that group. The group stays collapsed\n unless you dragged the active tab, in which case\n it expands. While you drag, the group's pill\n highlights to show the tab will drop inside it.\n- Duplicate Tab now opens the copy immediately to\n the right of the original tab instead of at the\n end of the tab bar.\n- The password manager’s settings menu now offers\n a single choice of how often to ask for Touch ID\n or your password: never, once per launch, once\n per launch and after the screen locks, or every\n time it opens. The new “every time it opens”\n option also closes the password manager when the\n screen locks.\n- Focus Follows Mouse no longer takes focus away\n from the find bar, composer, or other fields in\n a pane by accident. To have focus follow the\n pointer onto those views and back onto the\n terminal, turn on “…even to views like Search or\n Composer” under Settings > Pointer.\n- Uploads over SSH Integration are about three\n times faster.\n- “Upload and Paste Path” now works in tmux\n integration windows reached through SSH\n Integration, and before shell integration has\n reported a directory (the file goes to your home\n directory). Pasted screenshots are converted\n from TIFF to PNG and each gets a unique name.\n- Dropping files into a session connected to a\n remote host now offers Paste as Text for a text\n file, which types its contents, and “Upload and\n Paste Paths” for several files, which uploads\n them all and types their remote paths. Local\n paths are no longer offered there, since they\n name nothing on that host.\n- AI command suggestions can now recognize when\n you have typed a plain-English request instead\n of the start of a command. In that case the\n suggested command replaces what you typed rather\n than being appended to it.\n- Right-clicking a tab now offers Rename Tab,\n which opens the same dialog as double-clicking\n the tab.\n- “it2 session list” takes a --format option: an\n interpolated string evaluated in each session,\n such as '\\(session.path)'. It prints one line\n per session, or with --json adds the result to\n each session as “formatted”. This lists every\n session’s directory or job in one command.\n- Pausing a spinning progress indicator with OSC\n 9;4;4 now shows a paused spinner: a still yellow\n band in the session and a pause symbol in the\n tab, instead of a yellow bar at 10%.\n- The git branch shown in the session toolbar can\n now be right-clicked to copy its name, or\n dragged into a text field to paste it (or\n dropped on the Finder as a text clipping).\n Available only while a branch name is showing,\n not during a rebase or similar.\n- The Current Directory status bar component’s\n folder icon is now a proxy icon: drag it to\n another app to drag the session’s working\n directory, like the proxy icon in a window title\n bar.\n- The Interpolated String status bar component now\n shows [undef: x] for a reference to an undefined\n variable instead of silently rendering an empty\n string, making typos and unavailable variables\n obvious. Use \\(x?) to treat a missing value as\n empty.\n- The session’s status is now available in the\n session variable “status”, so you can show it in\n a badge, title, or status bar component.\n- AI chat asks for approval less often when it\n types a message you dictated into another\n session, such as telling a coding agent to\n re-run its tests once a watched session\n finishes.\n- The Session Status toolbelt tool now lists\n Claude Code sessions running inside tmux\n Integration panes on this Mac. They never\n appeared before, and their status went\n unreported to the rest of the app. Issue 13049.\n- If you launch a second copy of iTerm2 while one\n using the same settings is already running,\n iTerm2 now warns you that the two could\n overwrite each other’s preferences and saved\n windows, and offers to quit and switch to the\n running copy or launch anyway.\n- Browser tabs now show a title (page title, or\n the host, or the profile name) instead of a\n blank tab and window title.\n- In the minimal theme, a browser tab's light or\n dark appearance and tint now follow the page's\n background color instead of the profile's\n terminal background color.\n- The Session Status toolbelt tool has a snooze\n button next to the notify button that toggles\n snooze on the selected session.\n- The Companion setup wizard no longer asks for an\n API key when AI already works, or when you chose\n a model manually in Settings > General > AI. If\n that model isn’t set up yet, the wizard says so\n and lets you finish setup anyway.\n- AI command safety checks now use your provider’s\n smaller, cheaper model when one is available, so\n they cost less.\n- Settings search now reveals controls that live\n on an inner sub-tab (such as AI > Features). It\n switches to the correct nested tab before\n highlighting, instead of dimming a bogus area.\n- With the tab bar on the left or right, the tab\n and tab group context menus now say “New Tab\n Below” and “Close Tabs Below” instead of “to the\n Right”, matching the direction the tabs actually\n run. The confirmation for closing the tabs after\n a group follows suit.\n- The About window now follows the current theme,\n updating live when you switch between light and\n dark. On macOS 26 the backer list sits in a\n rounded container matching the sponsor cards;\n before that, dark themes get a frosted-glass\n look.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Opening an Instant Replay recording no longer\n applies settings embedded in the file that could\n run commands, such as triggers. Only appearance\n settings like colors, fonts, and cursor style\n are taken from a recording. Issue 13128.\n- Answering “Always” to “Close tmux windows after\n detaching?” now closes only the tmux integration\n windows left open by the detach. It used to\n close every session in the app.\n- Restarting or logging out of macOS while Claude\n Code was running in iTerm2 could cause your\n windows not to be restored at the next login.\n Claude Code’s status updates could relaunch\n iTerm2 just as it was quitting, and that brief\n launch replaced the saved windows.\n- After iTerm2 restarted with your sessions\n restored, which happens after an update, a\n crash, or a force quit, commands in newly opened\n sessions could fail to reach devices on your\n local network with “No route to host” until you\n quit and reopened iTerm2. New sessions now get\n local network access right away. Sessions\n restored from before the restart still need to\n be restarted to regain it. Issue 12106.\n- When an SSH Integration connection dropped, for\n example because the ssh process or the remote\n server was killed, the session hung: nothing you\n typed afterward appeared. It now ends the\n connection and returns to the local shell.\n- Stopping an AI chat reply while another chat is\n also replying no longer cancels the other chat’s\n reply, and the stopped reply now really stops\n downloading instead of running (and using up\n tokens) until it finishes.\n- Fixed various crashes, including ones when\n splitting, maximizing, swapping, or rearranging\n panes in a tmux integration window left open\n after detaching; when a maximized split pane was\n torn down, such as when a tmux connection\n dropped; at every launch when a profile’s name\n began with a combining character; when a trigger\n had no regex, such as an event trigger written\n by hand in a Dynamic Profile; when opening a tab\n while a paired iPhone was connected; and when\n saving a debug log that captured malformed text.\n- Pressing space twice in a terminal no longer\n types a period when macOS’s “Add period with\n double-space” setting is on. In vim this could\n repeat your last command. Issue 13123.\n- iTerm2 no longer applies macOS’s automatic\n spelling correction, capitalization, or smart\n quotes and dashes. They could change what you\n type in a terminal. Text replacements still\n work.\n- A tab name you set yourself with Edit Tab Title\n is no longer replaced by the session’s name\n whenever the tab’s subtitle changes or its label\n is otherwise refreshed. Issue 13072.\n- Make Text Bigger works again when you type it as\n ⌘⇧= (the way you type ⌘+). Since 3.7.0, that\n keystroke opened the Clippings panel instead,\n because Show Clippings had claimed it. Show\n Clippings is now ⌘⇧⌥G. Issue 13022.\n- In a session that ssh'ed to another machine\n without ssh integration, the current directory\n (and paths used by Upload and Paste Path) no\n longer switches to the local Mac's directory\n after you run a command.\n- Fixed restored sessions bringing lines that had\n been erased by a clear screen back onto the\n screen, above the newer output, with the cursor\n in the wrong place.\n- On macOS 26, hiding a hotkey window when no\n other iTerm2 windows are open once again returns\n focus to the previous app right away, so text\n you type immediately afterward no longer goes to\n the terminal.\n- A hotkey window set to appear on all spaces no\n longer switches you to a different space when\n revealed after reopening the laptop lid. It is\n now revealed on the current space and rejoins\n all spaces afterward. Issue 12968.\n- Fixed a bug on macOS 26 where pressing Cmd-Tab\n to leave a full screen window would flash the\n other app and immediately switch back to iTerm2,\n sometimes leaving you unable to change apps\n without the mouse. This affected windows using\n the older full screen style with menu bar hiding\n turned on.\n- Fixed dark triangles that could appear over a\n background image near the edges of the window\n with the GPU renderer. Issues 12604, 12642,\n 12653, and 12791.\n- Fixed “Remember my choice” applying the wrong\n choice in the dialog that appears when you paste\n a file, folder, or image. The answer was\n remembered by button position, so a choice saved\n for a local file could turn into a different\n button in a session connected to a remote host,\n silently uploading the file. Each set of choices\n the dialog can offer now remembers its own\n answer, so silencing it for a local file no\n longer silences it for a folder or for a remote\n host. Any answer you had saved for these dialogs\n is cleared, so you will be asked once more.\n- Fixed a beachball with a large file, or a file\n on a slow disk, copied from the Finder. Opening\n Edit > Paste Special read the whole file just to\n decide whether to enable Paste File\n Base64-Encoded, and pasting read it again before\n offering the paste options. Opening the menu no\n longer reads the file at all, and pasting looks\n at only the first 50 KB.\n- Fixed a lag when switching or closing tabs with\n the Session Status tool open in the toolbelt.\n- Fixed a rare race where a new session could\n silently lose some of the first output it\n received.\n- Attaching with tmux Integration no longer fails\n when a pane’s scrollback contains raw tmux\n control-mode text (for example, from an earlier\n tmux -CC run in a terminal without tmux\n Integration). iTerm2 mistook a %exit line in\n that text for tmux quitting, dropped the\n connection, and printed the rest of the protocol\n stream into the window.\n- In a tmux Integration session, keys such as\n Shift-Enter are now encoded the same way before\n and after a detach and reattach. Previously a\n pane could encode them one way before a detach\n and another way after, until the app was\n restarted. With tmux 3.9 or later, iTerm2 lets\n tmux encode each key for the app in the pane,\n including with the Kitty keyboard protocol.\n Issue 13076.\n- In tmux integration, a layout change made by\n tmux while Find > Filter was open on a pane no\n longer hides pane %0 or moves keyboard focus\n away from the filtered pane.\n- In tmux integration, a new pane now picks up\n user variables, the host name, the current\n directory, and the shell integration version\n that its shell reported while the pane was\n opening. Before, a badge such as\n \\(user.host_name) stayed empty until the shell\n showed its second prompt. Issue 13006.\n- In tmux integration, a window created with “tmux\n new-window -d” now opens as a background tab\n instead of taking focus. Issue 13112.\n- In tmux integration, iTerm2 could miss a window\n switch made by another tmux client or a script\n and keep showing the old tab.\n- Fixed tmux integration sessions not resizing\n when the tab bar hides or shows in full screen\n with “Hide tab bar when there is only one tab”\n enabled, which left an unused strip at the\n bottom of the window.\n- When a tmux integration window first loads, its\n tab now shows the finished title right away. It\n used to briefly show a bare session name, with\n neither the “↣” marker that flags a tmux tab nor\n the second line under the title.\n- Stopped falsely offering to reset the key\n reporting mode after every command when your\n shell turns the keyboard protocol on and off\n itself, as Fish 4 does. 3.7.2 fixed this only\n for Fish with oh-my-posh.\n- Fixed slow replies to cursor-position and other\n status queries. Programs that send many of them\n at once, such as during capability detection,\n waited seconds instead of milliseconds. Issue\n 13035.\n- Fixed Kitty keyboard protocol base-layout\n reporting for non-US keyboard layouts, so\n shortcuts such as Ctrl-C work without switching\n layouts.\n- With “Report keys using CSI u” turned on and\n Option set to Meta, pressing Option with a\n letter sent nothing at all. It now sends the\n meta byte, as it does without CSI u.\n- Fixed a bug in the Kitty graphics protocol where\n transmitting a new image under an image id\n already in use kept drawing the old image in\n places that already showed it. The newest image\n is now used, so one program no longer shows a\n stale picture left behind by another.\n- Fixed double-width and double-height lines (ESC\n #3, #4, and #6) staying double-sized after the\n line was erased, scrolled, or reset. A\n full-screen program that drew a large title and\n then repainted could show giant, clipped text\n where ordinary text belonged; entering the\n alternate screen, Reset, and Clear Buffer left\n the same stale lines behind.\n- Fixed a bug where a subparameter written after a\n full CSI parameter list was dropped even though\n it belonged to a parameter that had been stored,\n such as the 16th.\n- Parentheses, brackets, and other mirrored\n punctuation now draw correctly in right-to-left\n text, so a bracket around an English phrase\n inside Persian is handled the same way as in\n other Mac apps.\n- Fixed misplaced letters in right-to-left words\n that combine a lam-alef ligature with a\n diacritic, such as the Persian کاملاً and لطفاً.\n- Selecting part of an Arabic or Persian word no\n longer breaks its cursive joining. Previously a\n selection or a mid-word color change split the\n word (for example کامل) into isolated letter\n forms. Arabic and Persian text could also\n sometimes be drawn with each letter in its\n isolated form.\n- Selecting text on right-to-left lines now\n selects exactly the columns you drag over.\n Previously dragging over a line with embedded\n English or digits could highlight a different,\n broken-up span, and the first or last character\n of a right-justified line could be impossible to\n select. Copied text stays in reading order, and\n extending a selection across several lines\n follows each line’s direction.\n- Fixed a bug where a skin-toned emoji like 👍🏻 was\n drawn as the plain emoji followed by a separate\n color swatch, taking four columns. It is now a\n single emoji two columns wide, matching other\n modern terminals.\n- Fixed text written over the right half of an\n emoji or other wide character landing two\n columns too far to the right, a bug introduced\n in 3.7.3.\n- Find now matches text that spans a blank the\n program created by moving the cursor instead of\n printing a space. Before, searching for more\n than one word could fail on output from programs\n like Claude Code even though the words appeared\n separated by a space.\n- Resizing a window while only blank space was\n selected left behind an empty selection, so Copy\n and other selection-based actions acted on\n nothing.\n- Fixed a bug where a smooth-blinking cursor\n passed through light gray while fading in the\n GPU renderer instead of fading directly to the\n background color. Issue 12990.\n- The “Animate large movements” cursor option no\n longer streaks a cursor-colored trail across the\n screen when a program such as top redraws the\n screen on its own. The animation now plays only\n for cursor jumps that follow your own keyboard\n or mouse input.\n- Fixed a bug where pressing Backspace while\n composing text with an input method such as the\n Korean 2-Set IME could leave a stray character\n behind that needed an extra press to remove.\n Pressing Return while composing now submits the\n line in one press.\n- Voice Control corrections, like “replace world\n with there”, now replace the text when it ends\n at the cursor. Before, the new text was typed\n after the original. Corrections elsewhere, or in\n full-screen apps like vim, are ignored.\n- Key bindings saved by older versions of iTerm2\n no longer fire when you press A while “Interpret\n key bindings based on physical key” is on, which\n could make the A key stop typing. iTerm2 also no\n longer mistakenly offers to turn that setting on\n when you press a shortcut like ^A. Issue 13110.\n- Pasting or dropping a file or image into a local\n tab no longer offers only to upload it when\n another tool in your shell (such as oh-my-zsh)\n reports an outdated host name. When iTerm2 can’t\n be sure a session is on another machine, the\n paste dialog now also offers to paste a local\n path. Issue 13117.\n- Dragging a file into a local terminal now\n inserts its path on VPN or Tailscale networks,\n where it had instead been offering to upload the\n file. Requires the updated shell integration\n scripts.\n- The file paste options (Paste Path, Paste Paths,\n and Upload and Paste Path) now honor the “When\n pasting a dropped filename, wrap it in double\n quotes” setting in Settings > General, as\n dragging a file into a local session already\n did. They previously always used double quotes,\n ignoring the setting.\n- Uploading several files at once to a host you\n connected to with an SSH profile now uploads all\n of them instead of only the first, and “Upload\n and Paste Path” now pastes the path the file\n actually landed on, such as “name (2)” when the\n name was taken.\n- Cancelling an upload over SSH Integration no\n longer leaves a temporary file on the remote\n host or gets stuck at “Cancelling…”.\n- Archive Sessions on Closure failed with “The\n file doesn’t exist” whenever the session’s name\n contained a slash, such as a title that shows\n the tty or the current directory. Slashes and\n other characters that can’t appear in a filename\n are now replaced with underscores, the time in\n the archive name uses periods instead of colons\n so Finder shows it correctly, and very long\n names are shortened to fit. The same cleanup now\n applies to automatic session log filenames,\n which used to handle only slashes. Issue 13094.\n- Archive Sessions on Closure failed with “the\n volume is read only” when no archive folder was\n chosen. Turning it on with an empty folder now\n fills in ~/Library/Application Support/\n iTerm2/Archives, and profiles that already have\n it on with no folder archive there. Issue 12980.\n- Fixed several bugs archiving sessions on close:\n sessions closed shortly before quitting are now\n always archived, split panes with the same name\n are all saved instead of only one, and closing\n an archived session soon after opening it no\n longer warns that it ended very soon after\n starting.\n- Duplicate Session no longer stamps a permanent\n working-directory override on the new session.\n Previously that override was inherited by split\n panes and could go stale, dropping the shell in\n the wrong directory. Issue 12981.\n- The SCP-download menu animation no longer leaves\n an invisible window behind. That stray window\n could make clicking the Dock icon fail to open a\n new terminal after the last window was closed.\n Issue 12996.\n- Fixed a bug where auto-update failed with a\n message about running from the download location\n when iTerm2 was unzipped or moved to an\n Applications folder from the command line rather\n than dragged in the Finder. iTerm2 now fixes\n this automatically on launch, and no longer\n offers to move a copy that is already installed.\n- Semantic History can once again open files in\n Xcode when the active developer directory is set\n to the Command Line Tools rather than Xcode. It\n used to show a “Semantic History Command Failed”\n alert instead. Issue 13091.\n- Semantic History could open the wrong file in\n TextMate, BBEdit or MacVim when the path\n contained a space, an ampersand, or another\n character with special meaning in a URL.\n- “Open Selection as URL” now works on links\n without a scheme, like example.com/path, and is\n hidden when the selection isn’t a URL. Issue\n 13092.\n- Opening a link from a browser tab in a tab group\n (with “Open in New Tab” or a link that wants a\n new window) now puts the new tab in that group\n instead of leaving it ungrouped in the middle of\n the group. Issue 13029.\n- The control sequence that resets a tab color to\n the profile’s now restores it correctly when the\n profile has separate light and dark mode colors\n but its Tab Color predates them. It could leave\n the tab with a color you never chose, often red,\n or with no color at all. Issue 13058.\n- On macOS Tahoe, the progress ring around a tab\n is no longer missing from the first and last\n tabs. A tab that is partly scrolled under the\n window controls now keeps the visible part of\n its ring, and its activity indicator, instead of\n losing them entirely, and the ring stays within\n the tab bar’s rounded ends rather than drawing\n past them.\n- Fixed the git ahead/behind arrows in the session\n toolbar and the git status bar component staying\n black in dark mode instead of matching the text\n color.\n- On macOS 26, Maximized and Centered windows, and\n windows opened for a single command, now show\n the tab bar in the title bar like ordinary\n windows. Before, their tab bar background didn’t\n match the title bar and a dark line separated\n the two.\n- With the Minimal or Compact theme and no tab bar\n at the top, the window buttons that appear when\n you point at the window’s top-left corner no\n longer cover a status bar or Workgroup toolbar\n there, which made its leftmost item impossible\n to click. A small arrow appears in the corner\n instead; click it to show or hide the window\n buttons. Issue 13100.\n- Fixed a bug where dismissing a confirmation in\n certain Settings sheets (such as the hotkey\n configuration or special exceptions panels)\n would bring a terminal window to the front.\n- Help popovers, such as the ones shown by the ?\n buttons in Settings, no longer cut off the last\n line of text, and no longer scroll when all of\n their text fits.\n- Choosing Show Session Note from Open Quickly no\n longer makes an empty note appear briefly and\n then disappear.\n- When no valid UNIX locale matches your language\n and region, the prompt to pick one now appears\n as a normal dialog the first time a hotkey\n window opens, instead of as a sheet on the\n not-yet-visible window.\n- Pairing a companion iPhone no longer fails on\n networks that can’t reach iterm2.com. iTerm2 now\n falls back to a second copy of its list of relay\n servers. If neither can be reached, the pairing\n window says so and suggests another network;\n hover over the message to see what went wrong\n with each server.\n- Installing the Claude Code Integration now\n honors $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, so per-tab status\n indicators work for people who keep Claude\n Code’s settings outside ~/.claude. It used to\n always install into ~/.claude, where Claude Code\n never looked.\n- The Claude Code Integration no longer warns that\n it looks broken when you edit its hook commands\n in Claude Code’s settings.json to use $HOME, ~,\n or a wrapper script. Reinstalling also keeps\n such commands instead of adding a second copy of\n each hook. Uninstalling and reinstalling leave a\n hook command alone if it runs other programs\n besides the integration’s, and Uninstall lists\n any it left in place.\n- Installing, reinstalling, or uninstalling the\n Claude Code Integration now updates the file\n that a symlinked settings.json points to (as\n with GNU Stow or chezmoi). It used to replace\n the link with a plain file that other users\n could read, and Claude Code stopped seeing edits\n to the original file. Issue 13098.\n- Installing or reinstalling the Claude Code\n Integration no longer replaces a Claude Code\n settings.json that has a syntax error, which\n erased all your other Claude Code settings. It\n now leaves the file alone and asks you to fix it\n and try again.\n- Interrupting Claude Code with Esc now returns\n its Session Status to idle. The tab kept reading\n “working”, or “waiting” if a permission prompt\n was open, until the next thing you asked it to\n do.\n- Fixed Notify on Status Change reporting that a\n session changed from “none” when its status had\n already been set before you turned the\n notification on, and alerting when a program\n merely restated that status.\n- Debug logs no longer include passwords sent to\n or received from password manager integrations,\n AI API keys, or the values of custom HTTP\n headers configured for AI models.\n- The Bitwarden, KeePassXC, and Keeper password\n manager integrations now find command-line tools\n installed by Homebrew or npm on your shell’s\n search path instead of asking you to locate\n them. Homebrew’s “bw” is a script that needs\n “node”, which the integration could not find, so\n Bitwarden showed an empty list.\n- If you choose a password manager’s command-line\n tool by hand, iTerm2 now keeps the path you\n chose, so it keeps working after the tool is\n upgraded.\n- The Bitwarden integration now reports the\n Bitwarden CLI’s own error message instead of\n showing an empty list or a vague error. It lists\n only items in a Bitwarden folder named “iTerm2”,\n and now says so when your vault has no such\n folder.\n- Fixed a bug where the manual AI model editor’s\n Test Connection button ignored the “Supports\n temperature” toggle and always sent the\n temperature parameter, failing against endpoints\n that reject it.\n- Fixed a bug where the manual AI model editor’s\n Test Connection button sent your API key to an\n AI endpoint on your local network, even though\n real requests never do. An authenticated local\n server could report a successful test and then\n reject every chat message. The test now behaves\n like a real request, and names the header to add\n under Custom headers when the endpoint wants a\n credential.\n- Fixed a bug where the host in an AI model’s URL\n was compared letter for letter, so its\n capitalization, or a trailing dot, changed which\n API key was sent. A local address written that\n way (http://LOCALHOST:11434) was not recognized\n as self-hosted and received your provider key,\n and a provider address written that way\n (https://API.DEEPSEEK.COM) was attributed to\n OpenAI, so the wrong key was sent.\n- Fixed a bug where a custom HTTP header for AI\n requests whose name differed only in\n capitalization from one iTerm2 sets itself (such\n as “authorization”) was sent alongside the\n built-in header instead of replacing it, leaving\n it to chance which value the server saw.\n- Fixed a bug where turning on “Use AI to check\n safety of commands before running them” on a Mac\n without Apple Intelligence was turned back off\n the first time you used AI chat, which then\n asked whether to enable it again.\n- Fixed a bug where, if the AI asked for\n permission in a chat and you ignored the request\n and sent a new message instead, the chat would\n stop responding.\n- Fixed a bug where an AI chat permission button\n (such as “Check terminal state”) did not update\n its label after you changed the setting, and\n re-clicking it appeared to do nothing.\n- Fixed a bug where quitting iTerm2 unlinked each\n AI chat from its terminal session and canceled\n what the chat was watching, even when window\n restoration brought the session back at the next\n launch.\n- Editing an AI chat message now fully removes the\n later conversation, so the AI no longer sees\n leftover command results from it.\n- Stopping an AI chat reply and then sending\n another message no longer shows the rest of the\n stopped reply as the answer to the new message.\n- The API key field shown when AI features ask for\n a key now hides what you type instead of showing\n the key in plain text.\n- In AI chat, a reply that mentions two or more\n sessions now shows each one as a clickable link.\n Before, the session names could turn into plain,\n unclickable text.\n- Fixed Tab.async_update_layout in the Python API\n making a browser pane many times too big,\n squeezing the panes beside it, and sometimes\n crashing iTerm2.\n- Fixed the Python API reporting an underline\n color, block ID, or hyperlink on cells that did\n not have one. The first plain cell after a run\n that had one was described as part of that run,\n so scripts reading screen contents saw the\n attribute spill past where it ended.\n- iTerm2 writes to disk less often while it saves\n window state for restoration. A setting meant to\n reduce this had been silently turned off.\n- Installing shell integration without internet\n access now works on systems that don’t have\n bash, such as FreeBSD and OpenBSD. Before, the\n installer could hang partway through writing the\n script when your shell was tcsh.\n- The Internet-Free Install of shell integration\n no longer gets stuck at its first step for zsh\n users, and that step can no longer be skipped,\n which made later steps write to an invalid path.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\n404340110c5148d711a613002767160b3a4733477dfeca5168b6b6ab0ff30f45\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmrQTWobFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bHgEQAIgdHxT1V2BYwv58AWjU\nM2acn8aZcFnJFyL6ja4V2WEDun5rXorDGUwqPWpsY2XEFySmfiQ6wRzRkTpa1XwV\na3PjdAjr8htLcJwfPkDuUSCA19ozXVkc9iBTWGLkhWIm7/1lwFRaQpjYZCWkfytO\nLeCPugulzw6NsDXsi1CTBLZjOOjX2HXyAbkoXlMACrgvYI0tySmrZpQdUQTzL0ix\nhsElqCblUSo8+5VNmr/OY8eOsRHKVcZnKavUPbig0MUJF2bih4zOcCkS85sF/cyk\nt/f6w+gVbifDuw+gRxe6RfkGM4cSS346uy9jpmvrXEfmIR6jitDzbFLoPNSL5TAM\nLJPePEbOy5gZe7KBQ/eMBAA5dn0uG5uIKe1KkP7bpWhU+w+47K+f6xNdRSNnzpF3\nayS1p/lt8MrrwsImGCLTl35DOSj4sz1bbQ9AJzpu6LvmbXqscsFTiGjzS+ovfY+G\nxp63dFbuHptmmlMUr8bkspFT6nM2/IZF/4dM+N8T4R/YJrzj9uozcf/4LUe7fh3f\nqAIjVjDnsLyuheOuo8HXwCwCE+T5W2EJOGkhJ3Tgq3ClIhUsmAWHX/2qHG8gryLs\nt7Ns8BNToD8wsIDIqnsN7LOiRdPanl2cpiwVWwaOwAA3Q0J6G3iEjOhD70z3R0cp\np4rdXesWQz9cp3c7liOZeK/a\n=6PVx\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.4 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on October 8, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.4 of iTerm2 was built on October 8, 2026.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed a crash that could recur every time\n iTerm2 saved window state. Quitting while a\n save was in progress could store duplicate\n entries in the saved-state database, which\n later saves could not handle.\n- Fixed a crash when opening a new tab next to a\n tab in a tab group.\n- iTerm2 no longer crashes when its app was\n moved, deleted, or replaced while running,\n for example by an installer. It now explains\n that the app is damaged and quits.\n- Fixed a crash when exiting full screen in a\n window without a title bar, such as a tmux\n integration window, on macOS 26.\n- Fixed a crash with the GPU renderer that\n could happen after changing the font size\n with Cmd-+ or Cmd--.\n- Fixed a crash that could happen when the GPU\n renderer had been running for several weeks\n without restarting iTerm2. Issue 13103.\n- Fixed a crash when pressing Cmd-Z after\n closing a pane, toolbelt tool, or other view\n that had text you had edited.\n- Fixed a crash when a program printed a\n letter followed by many spacing marks (such\n as Devanagari vowel signs) in a later write.\n Issue 13073.\n- Fixed a crash in “Import All Settings and\n Data” when the import changed a setting that\n requires your administrator password.\n- Fixed “Export All Settings and Data” failing\n with “Failed to copy file” after using SSH\n Integration. Temporary connection files it\n could not copy are now skipped.\n- Fixed crashes loading Dynamic Profiles files\n with values of an unexpected type, such as a\n null value or a Keyboard Map that is not an\n object. These values are now ignored or\n reported instead.\n- Fixed a crash, repeated on every relaunch,\n when a profile’s name began with a combining\n mark.\n- Fixed various other crashes, including ones\n involving Find, the composer, splitting\n panes, Smart Selection, right-to-left text,\n VoiceOver, the GPU renderer, and quitting\n while iTerm2 was still starting up.\n- Pressing space twice in a terminal no longer\n types a period when macOS’s “Add period with\n double-space” setting is on. In vim this could\n repeat your last command. Issue 13123.\n- iTerm2 no longer applies macOS’s automatic\n spelling correction, capitalization, or smart\n quotes and dashes. They could change what you\n type in a terminal. Text replacements still\n work.\n- Make Text Bigger works again when you type it\n as ⌘⇧= (the way you type ⌘+). Since 3.7.0,\n that keystroke opened the Clippings panel\n instead, because Show Clippings had claimed it.\n Show Clippings is now ⌘⇧⌥G. Issue 13022.\n- Help popovers, such as the ones shown by the ?\n buttons in Settings, no longer cut off the last\n line of text, and no longer scroll when all of\n their text fits.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\n4bf02ae616752600cd195ca504e55392afb21741cda7314199e375ed8a46f90c\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmrHw+4bFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bXtEQAIKgng87ANS/M3pFKolL\noowAbsokfuascIOg52iJ+7CS4QtDR3J2eexdcHfXr6r8rZsuNBOs8USRdeNmROWl\nKPKFBq/hryx2FNiIwiiVyVZY91UsSYP8KYNT4X6gntRUCdtWuVaNozPv4HeTgy5c\nuy6w8NAnuVIWQvcWUubpdhxOtLJoKIGrXDrkZt9QE/7I4da93nz88cddxKwu9qMr\nTSHjhxnQmRqvAAjCKdvG1loIXOiaROzqdjgZkSGjkbihEsLPeaPJtG553QsxIdJj\nAjCKV1gubBtDpN+ydI6s8D7C4FDqVQxJFkTFmCeQjrzSGf1mbVNnlbC779Y25jb1\nP2mUu9aweQPlJwHaUUMMIHVuu9Iun6PlJnZxFokkiVhZaCs+WaVZq1tQihsHr5De\nije2DaRpU7s7hF7HYiHefvzaMah2ZFy9GOHEeMeXCUjj9A8zQw0+LZhEE/ET1Th0\nL1kDdnaDNyiJH6ugGx2rv0kFFO3R+SMRCt76lYV6VNfHrOo0LamA3n3vDUKYE2NW\nqA5WRTzTpUnqnUADZ0+75uXsHysME/ddRvsF3819w0QEqdvht6uiA2SDuh4tiMHi\nnX7BBdhqnHlIsBvedEjWWcEEWDuvV0QrhFZFhHQ4HBN3vfx+y5buF7Wm68MohuTv\ntwHFevsVFb8YyrP+LVsRQD38\n=a6sT\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.3 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on September 22, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.3 of iTerm2 was built on September 22, 2026.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed the Python API not reporting OSC 8\n hyperlinks. A cell’s CellStyle.url is now\n populated, so scripts reading screen contents\n can see the link target.\n- Fixed the Screen setting in Settings >\n Profiles > Window. Choosing “Main Screen”\n left the popup blank and showed no checkmark\n next to it when reopened, although the\n setting was saved correctly.\n- Fixed a crash when a program wrote a hyperlink\n whose web address contained an emoji or other\n character outside the Basic Multilingual\n Plane. The link is now handled correctly\n instead of terminating iTerm2, which could\n recur on restore because the saved session\n replayed the same link.\n- Fixed severe typing lag in a tmux Integration\n session running on a host you connected to\n with an SSH profile. Keystrokes and the output\n they produced were each held back about a\n second, so typing arrived in slow bursts.\n- Fixed occasional delayed screen updates in a\n session that had gone quiet. A redraw with no\n other output behind it could be held back\n until something else happened in the session.\n- Fixed a bug where a profile whose custom\n working directory used a tilde (such as\n ~/www) wrongly reported the directory as\n unavailable and started in the home\n directory instead.\n- Fixed a bug where editing only the keystroke\n of a Select Menu Item or Toggle Setting key\n binding erased the menu item or setting it\n was bound to.\n- Fixed a bug where a flag emoji took four\n columns instead of two, leaving a gap after\n it. A flag is now always two columns, the\n width other terminals report for it, so the\n advanced setting “Lone regional indicators\n render full-width” no longer affects flags.\n Skin-toned emoji are still affected and will\n be fixed separately.\n- Fixed a bug where a character that combined\n with the one before it could come out the\n wrong width when the two halves arrived in\n separate writes, leaving the cursor inside\n the character.\n- Fixed Cmd-number tab shortcuts becoming\n non-consecutive after changing a tab group\n from the context menu. Removing a tab from a\n group reordered the tabs but left the\n shortcut numbers on their old positions.\n- Fixed Kitty graphics placeholder cells\n drawing nothing when the image id fit in\n three bytes and the program omitted the\n optional diacritic that carries the high\n byte. Other terminals render these, and now\n iTerm2 does too.\n- Fixed a crash when asking for notification\n permission. The reply block could be called\n after it had gone out of scope, which could\n terminate iTerm2 at the moment a\n notification was first requested.\n- Installing the Python runtime used by scripts\n now trusts the certificates installed on your\n Mac. On networks that inspect secure traffic,\n it could fail with a certificate error even\n though the same download succeeded in a\n browser.\n- Fixed unbounded memory growth with programs\n that animate using the Kitty graphics\n protocol. Each redrawn frame was kept for the\n life of the window, so a long-running\n animation could consume many gigabytes.\n- Fixed a bug where blank space rendered as a\n stray “~” with the GPU renderer in profiles\n that have Anti-aliased turned off. It showed\n up most often in bold runs and in the padding\n of full-screen programs like k9s, and it\n persisted for the rest of the session once it\n appeared.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\neb7a166061e58602e3d4bdf69d92f2c8cf6a63feed002f6adc07128a71c8dc39\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmqzP2MbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bCEUQAKtQBBPcwS56eEafgcnZ\nxgu/pN0vUfYrU2gSAkIeDxyTseJvZm8X4504FCcxhfKKJ9TM/qP7KjF/Fq+fiC6n\noScCg0ozknBVnn7VlZj/5omhMnywRJS8iosuw6ec2lurOlDeidaAELaSnT3a2Hgj\nr4LXA/T+JHJwfBRW/aqd5yvrtOP8Zu+wcnUBVlT+hNiNiBwxCAeMAQE1O1y4anoY\nHjlkTLIsHIDFtcpdWPQWn7Gt8hobjUWjINBppvBPDnITYPcBVNr1JYLlkb4CVCDc\nK8MvPg0s4mZtLHQxa5quSul2Xu5vIJVOWlWFQGdq1e3d+xrUGCLnMJzHaQrgEPki\nY+jispMQXFSufJdfpsweUIXRQG+M7qJ/jBWaloENqC6lG9ddv7P0iHMcWhybDADr\nNAgltbHjEnmJWQkTJGtPohmorDtUvMM0/78DRfkiwliFb0OKUZMpLtQM66c/dujr\nmBvHstsZxQOxlJmA0raZ/BUB8kEuJmi4CBx8/LLCUGlPHbMKityXPybP+FhjV1Z9\nWXxAE39wcb6TRyD50AQt4jJJb9L3ORyfGpWslKCJWxZsukU+2CVE4HT7HBlxZBrq\nQfj7Ap2XTWTk0000gbQhUcvS8SsLtQkERDqumkPOTwUqNAtE58dLc4HtSb1CQSmg\nd6RvZg2LXBOSddanuIXvLtzj\n=qMsS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.2 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on September 14, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.2 was built on September 14, 2026.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed a crash when editing a dynamic profile\n whose file had been changed on disk.\n- Fixed a crash when clicking the bookmark\n button on a command in the terminal.\n- Fixed a crash in the GPU renderer on macOS 26\n caused by configuring the drawable timeout off\n the main thread.\n- Fixed a crash that could occur when quitting\n iTerm2 while several Claude Code sessions were\n running.\n- Fixed a crash when attaching over tmux -CC to\n a window whose panes are all floating, which\n tmux 3.8 reports with an empty layout.\n- Fixed the filter bar briefly matching every\n line when you changed the query while a search\n was still in progress, such as by typing\n quickly.\n- Fixed tmux pane sizing when a per-session\n toolbar (such as the workgroups toolbar) is\n shown. Panes were told they had more rows than\n actually fit, so content rendered behind the\n toolbar and appeared to jump.\n- Stopped falsely offering to reset the key\n reporting mode after every command when using\n Fish with oh-my-posh, which together emit a\n duplicate command-finished mark after\n re-enabling the keyboard protocol for the next\n prompt.\n- Fixed a performance regression from 3.7.0 that\n made typing lag or arrive in bursts when using\n terminal multiplexers (such as tmux -CC) and\n other programs that query the terminal’s colors,\n especially when switching tabs or windows.\n- Fixed a bug on macOS 26 where the tab bar\n became short and shifted down after entering\n and exiting Lion fullscreen, and where the\n tabs sat jammed against the top of the screen\n in fullscreen. Affects the Tahoe tab bar with\n tabs on top.\n- The Companion setup wizard now saves the\n pasted API key for the provider you chose\n in the popup, so choosing a provider\n other than the current default no longer\n stores the key under the wrong service.\n- Fixed the workgroup toolbar’s\n auto-request-review and auto-send-clippings\n toggles showing a stale off state after a\n restart even though they were actually on, so\n the button now always matches whether the\n behavior is enabled.\n- Notify on Status Change no longer claims\n ⇧⌘X as a default keyboard shortcut. That\n shortcut could shadow a macOS Service bound\n to the same keys. Assign your own shortcut\n in System Settings > Keyboard > Keyboard\n Shortcuts > App Shortcuts if you want one.\n- Fixed extended key reporting being lost after\n a restart with session restoration on. An app\n that had turned on modifyOtherKeys (so keys\n like Shift-Enter send a distinct code) now\n keeps working when its session is restored,\n instead of quietly reverting to plain keys.\n- Fix performance problems with window titles\n changing in tmux.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\ne77f7c35292b40bfdecaaa699cbbfa7ceea6e65ebed9c9ff720eeed210daae87\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmqovmkbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4b43IP/02iGDGxM7se2bb3nNn3\nnCtm5Z7SC0BVmtE5DIzsGkfMyXm92IsSUxnkAs3uA0i2dpk244ByE5Xg55CganD+\n9Pnr6PsrSfln4azuJY6mo31fGACPrwFgYcC3hXpzOU4lWt1c3wkKeEaRw5/LnSb4\nADpXla0+s3+69v8Cna4fWmAFAB/f+pU94A6cM/q1QQbO5qUO+Pnb412ceZ2fntmR\nArypKA6r92t5R1XD3Gh+kUInyUQnRGZ5iy3eSUgDCPqzzCxkNyRMDUY17BjuFme/\nTxmDrTlErXZ1I6EIZbqYKqpxvGb3tAHg1RVtpJW64cfi/WCaUYECYM+N6Do97K8z\nhSOB5FlTHklyrqVEiVeCX/1s2Cb+7ml4sH7OV36dccsmSsCRxt4fBk36oECbchVT\nJb7pIExp3YZm9wQ6f+O9CuJ+VrPmVK/caQxdj/kRf126BqhtnLuX3aGthOG/HbMa\nFReGeJQHNmAjYEPAGyXv5BnAu1vCXVXUsNCzS/DWu15if6+5ic7EqcJ7NsmSJrdg\nekeDjRqbLfV8+/QCL1TBIwlAA4jtApAXvK3tNkdqN+Ym+TYqjZiqml/iwEP2LmlF\nnUkcyHvIOgR+ro5YrLK8AmH5OrN7D3myiSASB9pA1hvzxWfW5/k3qFjqQNOKr0i0\nLeyUGN0Gg6aFBtEPzVUOMyZc\n=wTQn\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.1 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on September 10, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.1 was built on September 10, 2026.\n\nImprovements:\n- Newlines embedded in a completion suggestion\n (such as a command-history entry or file name)\n now show as a return glyph so the entry stays\n on one line instead of spilling across rows.\n The value inserted when you accept the row is\n unchanged.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed a crash on launch when restoring saved\n sessions that contained right-to-left text\n written by version 3.6.11. This could force you\n to discard all of your restored sessions.\n- Fixed a memory leak where browser panes were\n never freed, leaking a WebKit process and a\n system power-management handle on every page\n load. Over time this could exhaust a\n system-wide limit and break other apps that\n manage power (such as OrbStack).\n- Fixed a bug where navigating between tmux\n sessions or windows could freeze the UI for\n several seconds.\n- Fixed dragging a file out of a session that\n failed when the terminal app used kitty's\n drag-and-drop protocol.\n- Fixed cmd-clicking a URL at the end of a line\n in a full-screen app (such as irssi or mutt),\n which could open the URL with text from the\n next line appended.\n- Fixed AI tool calls failing with Ollama when a\n tool used a non-string argument such as a\n number or boolean.\n- Fixed a bug where a Python script that created\n a window and immediately read its current tab\n or session got nothing back, which broke\n scripts that split panes or changed the font of\n a freshly created window.\n- Fixed Duplicate Tab so a tab duplicated from a\n tab group stays in that group instead of being\n left as a stray one-tab group.\n- Fixed a bug where removing a tab from the\n middle of a tab group left the group\n discontiguous.\n- Fixed a spurious offer to reset key reporting\n mode after running a command in shells that use\n the kitty keyboard protocol, such as Fish 4.\n- Fixed the tab group outline in the Tahoe theme,\n which was being clipped: the top and bottom\n lines, the outline around the group chip, and\n the rounded end caps of the first or last group\n in the tab bar were partly or fully missing.\n- Fixed a faint vertical line that could appear\n inside a selected tab in the dark Tahoe theme.\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\ned5c0f623e584cddc123f87d8bda90cf4d1d18dbbae2b9b0e85939f989c640b0\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmqjDncbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFudTIsMi41KzEuMTIsMCwzAAoJECYs/l55Gr4bTuAP/2413m/0kTT3juW0FIt6\nhsHDyirgm1d6pEYq2SrBOohqpSGv8eMuBx4540wpggGSvD3d55d65ZhusiW3KRgE\nJK+CElxc3jPc3Qft59wXT1ibcJZQyJfq8xeJIuGlqBt7wfqkrJ+aBfgHLuDWom+w\nqJgyB6kmhLYzzB7VR2POkXoN9vTNd3C9LE7jKMQVJViCIWinZiTAfo1UOgn2Lmw4\n7cesPNZRdYb9yPCFThjP5dQ2Vx4LGdlBwIMyu1kOND1igccDS/8sBJS3QcIJ+WGs\n5ghMR0CkZLb8tLJMjrPf7HQbMhvgf8fuqmmL0JIdLRVfy3334vmwVrI6fiDZbvxh\nHObLb9HY2ygY4DADssXvAKJ8YNP6il+5jNkGlmlUr899ese94Tmo+RAbOk19N6wK\ntf9+bT0JTT1TlFXRX4tQzyyIIat6fdvo8noAe+7qupp58HTiCZ1Z7x2BkSaumFsF\nNgp6MZtVuZaHghXJQI6hIcdVBbPv8lC2THezXwWHOIRUTCb3isPyccPeIyIb9+Vh\nCNdM577sTCQT2kalYqQ2UPVXjxyKEHymk1BYHBVbjoRGGSiej/RrXnoevzXPMwF6\n8X19qt28wyP1dVfBTeyb39lAD0ZNPhcSD00lyxuW6hxsKaG4ySVzqnabGTxu2cmp\nNkUuX0Q6PKG3XXAwvOBvF0q+\n=JX/a\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n \n\n\n \n\n iTerm2 3.7.0 (macOS 13+)\n \n\n\n \nThis is the recommended build for most users.\nBuilt on September 8, 2026.\n \n ▸ Show Changelog \n ▾ Hide Changelog \n Version 3.7.0 was built on June 8, 2026\n\nMajor New Features:\n- Companion App: a new iOS app, iTerm2 Buddy,\n pairs with your Mac by scanning a QR code\n (iTerm2 > Companion Device Settings) and\n connects over an encrypted Noise channel so you\n can interact with iTerm2 via AI chat from your\n phone.\n Devices connect through an encrypted relay, so\n they work even when the Mac and phone are on\n different\n networks, and end-to-end encryption ensures that\n your data is only visible to you.\n First-time setup is now a guided wizard: it\n installs the AI and companion plugins, turns on\n both features with a single password prompt,\n takes your API key, and walks you through\n pairing your phone.\n\n- There's a new kind of AI chat called\n \"orchestration\" which can view all your\n sessions and operate iTerm2 for you.\n\n- A new Session Status system was added. Sessions\n can now report status that appears on their tab\n with a custom subtitle, color, and dot icon.\n Status can be set by a control sequence, the it2\n command, or by triggers. The Session Status tool\n lets you view the status of all sessions in a\n window at a glance and the new Cockpit window\n lets you browse status across windows.\n\n- Claude Code integration was added. It enables\n the new Session Status feature for claude code\n sessions so you can see at a glance which\n instance is waiting for you. It also ties in\n with the new Workflows feature to facilitate\n viewing diffs and performing code reviews. It\n ties in with clippings so that AI-authored code\n review comments can easily be sent back to\n Claude code.\n\n- Session Notes were added: floating,\n draggable, resizable, collapsible multiline\n text notes attached to a session that\n persist across restarts.\n\n- The new Workgroups feature let you define how to\n transform a session into a cluster of related\n sessions. When a session enters a Workgroup, the\n cluster of related sessions is created. They can \n be in split panes, separate tabs, or \"peers\"\n which are like tabs within a split pane.\n Sessions in a workgroup have a unique name, a\n custom command, a customizable toolbar, and can\n even have custom behavior such as to drive AI\n code review or perform in-terminal diffs of a\n git repo.\n When a Workgroup spawns extra tabs, they are\n now collected into a tab group together with\n the session you entered from, and each spawned\n split or tab takes the name you gave it in the\n Workgroup so its tab title is recognizable.\n\n- Added tab groups: right-click a tab and choose\n Add Tab to Group to group Chrome/Firefox-style\n tabs. A named, colored chip heads each group;\n right-click it to rename, recolor, duplicate,\n move to a new window, ungroup, or close the\n group. Drag the chip to move the whole group,\n within a window or into another one; drag\n single tabs into, around, or out of a group.\n Groups persist their name and color with the\n member tabs.\n\n- You can now create screenshots including\n highlights and redactions using the Make\n Screenshot menu item.\n\n- Session archives were added. The new Session >\n Archives menu has “Save Archive of Current\n Session…” and “Restore Archive…” commands, and\n remembers your ten most recent archives. A\n per-profile setting can automatically archive a\n session to a configured directory when it\n closes. Restoring an archive reopens the\n session’s scrollback in a new window.\n\nNew Features in Terminal Sessions:\n- Right-to-left script support (bidi\n reordering plus Arabic and Persian\n cursive joining) is now enabled by\n default. You can turn it off in\n Settings > General > Experimental.\n- Double-width and double-height lines\n (DECDWL, DECDHL, DECSWL) are now supported\n via ESC #3, #4, #5, and #6.\n- Custom in-terminal buttons with SF Symbol\n icons that emit a user-defined escape code\n when clicked. To add one, send\n OSC 1337 ; Button=type=custom;code=N;\n icon= ST. The code must be\n positive. To invalidate all custom buttons,\n send OSC 1337 ; Button=type=custom ST.\n- A control sequence to programmatically\n fold and unfold blocks of output. Send\n OSC 1337 ; UpdateBlock=id= ;\n action=fold ST (or action=unfold) where\n blockID matches a previously declared\n block ID (see custom escape sequence docs\n \"Blocks\" section for details).\n- A new SetProfileProperty control sequence\n lets you change profile properties at\n runtime. Send\n OSC 1337 ; SetProfileProperty= =\n [; = ...] ST,\n e.g. set “Cursor Type” to 2 by encoding\n the JSON value 2 as base64.\n- OSC 52 clipboard query responses now work\n with tmux 3.6+ in tmux integration.\n- Added OSC 21337 for session status. Send\n OSC 21337 ; indicator= ;\n status= ; status-color= ST\n to set the tab’s dot indicator, subtitle,\n and subtitle color. Send the same sequence\n with empty values to clear.\n- Smooth-typing animation for underscore and\n vertical-bar cursors can be enabled in\n Settings > Profiles > Text.\n- Relative timestamps (set via the Set\n Baseline for Relative Timestamps menu)\n now use Go-style unit suffixes such as\n +1m23s and +2h5m, giving sub-minute\n precision in the 1m–1h range that was\n previously rounded to whole minutes.\n- The new Fold All menu item folds everything\n (aside from folds at the very top of history).\n This makes it easy to keep history around\n without it getting in the way.\n- Added support for the Kitty drag-and-drop\n protocol (OSC 72). A program running in the\n terminal can accept files, text, and images\n dropped onto the window, and (when it has mouse\n reporting on, e.g. a full-screen TUI) can offer\n its own data to be dragged out to other apps.\n For a program on a remote host, dropped files\n are transferred to it over the connection. Hold\n Option while dropping to use the old\n paste/upload behavior instead.\n- Clippings is a new side panel that contains a\n list of items formatted as markdown. They're a\n useful place to store text that you need\n temporarily.\n The Claude Code integration uses Clippings to\n store code review comments so they can be shared\n between related sessions in a Workgroup.\n\nNew Features in Tabs, Windows, and UI:\n- Left and right tabs in the Minimal theme are now\n as tall as top tabs, giving subtitles room to\n render legibly. The height follows the existing\n Minimal tab bar height advanced setting.\n- Most window types support initial size as a\n percentage of the screen (used to be only number\n of rows and columns).\n- Tabs can be pinned so they stay put and are\n protected from reordering.\n- New Lock Size menu item and per-profile\n preference to prevent window resizing.\n- New Lock Layout menu item (in the Window\n menu, next to Lock Size) that freezes a\n window’s layout: it prevents splitting,\n adding or closing tabs, reordering tabs,\n closing or dragging panes, and moving\n panes or tabs to other windows. Resizing\n panes, opening new windows, and closing\n the window still work.\n- Panes can be locked to prevent accidental drag\n and drop.\n- Tabs can now be placed on the right side\n of the window.\n- The control-sequence-controlled progress\n indicator is now shown in the tab bar.\n- An active pane border can be displayed to\n highlight the focused pane.\n- You can now choose to show a Proxy icon in\n Compact and Minimal themes.\n- The tab color menu now has a color picker,\n recent colors, and an eyedropper tool.\n- Asymmetrical broadcasting via a per-session\n broadcast-source flag lets you prevent\n accidental typing in non-designated sessions\n from being broadcast.\n- New key binding action to send text without\n broadcasting.\n- Nerd Font glyphs are now rendered in pane title\n bars and tabs.\n- A unified dock badge combines bell count and tab\n status, and the old 999 cap on the bell count\n was removed.\n- Split pane icons were added to the context\n menu.\n- Hold Option when clicking a Split button in the\n Profiles panel to open the selected profiles as\n splits in a new window instead of the current\n one.\n- Background images can now rotate through a\n folder of images at a configurable interval.\n Pick a folder instead of a file in\n Settings > Profiles > Window > Background.\n- In the Jobs view (toolbelt and status bar\n popover) hovering on a row shows the full\n command as a tooltip, and pressing space\n opens a popover with the full command,\n process ID, start time, working\n directory, environment variables, and a\n list of open files and network\n connections.\n- The status bar can now draw a separator line\n between itself and the terminal content. Enable\n it in the status bar layout’s advanced settings.\n- Improve tab dragging: keep Metal enabled\n for normal tab drags, switch reorder\n slots near tab midpoints, and stabilize\n split-pane target highlights.\n\nTriggers Improvements:\n- Event-based triggers can fire on events such as\n a session ending or a hostname change.\n- A trigger can now match when a session variable\n changes to a value matching a regex.\n- Triggers can be imported and exported as\n .it2triggers files.\n- A regex can now be used to define word\n characters for a trigger.\n- Nulls are now treated as spaces in trigger regex\n matching.\n- You can now assign a \"job\" to a trigger, which\n ensures the trigger is only active when that job\n is running. This makes it possible, for example,\n to write triggers that are only active while\n Claude Code is running.\n\nAI Improvements:\n- The AI chat “View History” permission is now\n “View Contents”. When it is set to provide\n contents automatically, a session-bound chat\n includes the terminal’s visible screen with\n each message, not just on-request history\n tools.\n- An AI chat linked to a terminal session now\n stays linked when the session's shell restarts\n (which happens often with the Claude Code\n integration). Previously the link, its command\n permissions, and orchestrator @-mentions of the\n session were lost on every restart.\n- Chats in the AI chat list now get an icon\n drawn by your AI model from the chat\n title. Chats without one show a default\n icon. The prompt can be edited in\n Settings > General > AI > Prompts.\n- The AI command safety check now uses your\n configured AI model. It runs deterministic\n terminal rules first and only asks the model\n when those rules cannot decide. It can send\n commands to your (possibly cloud) provider, so\n it is opt-in. Users who already enabled it keep\n using free on-device Apple Intelligence and are\n asked once whether to switch to their model.\n- AI chat can connect to LAN-hosted models\n over HTTP.\n- Anthropic prompt caching now also covers the\n conversation history, not just the system\n prompt and tools. A rolling cache breakpoint\n on the latest message lets long chats reread\n the prior turns from cache instead of paying\n full input price every turn.\n- AI models updated.\n- The built-in AI model list is now data-driven\n and refreshes in the background, so new models\n appear without updating iTerm2.\n- DeepSeek v4 thinking mode is now supported.\n Toggle the lightbulb in the chat toolbar to\n enable. Reasoning summaries appear live in\n the conversation and round-trip correctly\n on multi-turn tool calls (fixes a 400 from\n the API when reasoning_content was missing).\n- The “Configure AI Model Manually…” popover\n now has a section for custom HTTP headers\n that are appended to every AI request (chat,\n vector store, and file upload). Useful for\n proxy auth or routing tags. Custom headers\n override built-in headers with the same\n name. Values are stored unencrypted in\n iTerm2’s preferences.\n- PDF chat attachments now work on Anthropic.\n- When AI chat types into a terminal, a newline\n is now sent as Return (carriage return) so it\n submits the line in programs like Claude Code\n instead of inserting a literal newline. The\n tool also reports how many characters were\n inserted.\n- Added a Zero Data Retention setting under\n Settings > General > AI. Turn it on if your\n OpenAI organization has Zero Data Retention\n enabled. iTerm2 then stops sending\n previous_response_id and sets store=false, so\n chats no longer fail on the second message.\n- AI chat can now restart a session for you.\n The “Type for You” permission was renamed\n “Control Terminal” and now also gates this.\n- AI chat linked to a terminal session can now\n watch that session and tell you when it reaches\n a state or a described condition, without\n polling. Previously only orchestration chats\n could set watches. If your phone is paired it\n can also push you a notification when the watch\n fires.\n- The manual AI model editor now offers presets\n for OpenAI-compatible providers (Ollama,\n OpenRouter, z.ai, Qwen, MiniMax) that fill in\n the endpoint for you, plus a Test Connection\n button that verifies your settings and API key\n before you save.\n- AI chat history entries can now be deleted\n from the chat list.\n- AI chats now keep the provider chosen at\n creation time. Settings choose the default\n provider for new chats, while existing\n chats only offer models from their saved\n provider.\n\nPython API Improvements:\n- Move sessions to new tabs or windows via the\n Python API.\n- Load URLs in browser sessions from the Python\n API.\n- The Open URL API now supports a per-domain\n allowlist.\n- App.async_apply_layout atomically reshapes\n one or more tabs to a target layout in a\n single transaction. Supports swapping panes,\n rearranging the split tree, and moving\n sessions across tabs and windows. New\n sessions can be created in place with\n new_session leaves.\n- The API server no longer lets a client flood it\n with frames to exhaust memory: it bounds how\n much unparsed inbound data it buffers, and a\n client that floods pings but stops reading the\n pongs is disconnected as misbehaving rather than\n having its pongs buffered without limit. The it2\n command-line client handles a flood of ping\n frames iteratively instead of recursively.\n Credit to NSEcho for the find.\n- The Script Console has a Clear Terminated\n button that removes stopped sessions from the\n list, so it no longer grows without bound when\n you repeatedly restart a script.\n- The warning that a script’s pinned Python\n version will be bumped for the new uv runtime\n now has an Upgrade Now button that migrates\n those scripts immediately, instead of only\n when each one next runs.\n\nPassword Manager Improvements:\n- Bitwarden is now a supported password manager.\n- Keeper Security is also supported.\n\nShell Integration Improvements:\n- xonsh is now a supported shell.\n- Better interop with Fish 4.3, which builds in\n some of shell integration.\n- A new it2 command-line utility lets you perform\n common actions like creating a window at the\n command line. Run `it2 --help` for details.\n It's automatically in your path.\n\nSSH Integration Improvements:\n- Better passphrase handling for SCP and\n private keys. Wrong passphrases now retry\n instead of falling through to an empty\n password, and encrypted-key detection was\n improved.\n\nTmux Improvements:\n- Bracketed paste state is restored on attach\n (prep for tmux’s upcoming bracket_paste_flag\n format).\n- The per-pane modifyOtherKeys state is now\n restored on attach (using tmux 3.5+’s\n pane_key_mode format), so reattaching while\n nvim or another app is running keeps the\n correct key reporting mode active.\n- Saner defaults for which window and tab a tmux\n attach uses.\n- The advanced setting for where tmux windows\n created outside iTerm2 open is now a three-way\n choice: a new window, the focused window, or\n the topmost window already hosting that tmux\n session.\n- A new tmux window opened with Command-T now\n picks up the profile’s custom tab title, if\n one is set.\n\nExpressions and Variables:\n- Arithmetic expressions and comparisons are\n now supported in interpolated strings.\n- You can right click on a color in settings and\n choose \"Bind to Expression\" so that it will\n takes its value from an expression (e.g., a\n user-defined Variable or registered function:\n see the Scripting Fundamentals section of the\n documentation for details). The value should\n be a string like #ff8844.\n- Same for checkbox settings. The value should be\n a boolean.\n- A new global variable iterm2.appBundlePath\n exposes the path to iTerm2.app for use in\n interpolated strings.\n- A new window variable window.title holds the\n rendered window title, alongside the existing\n session.presentationName and tab.title.\n\nUI Improvements:\n- A new setting in Settings > Appearance makes\n the tab bar scrollable. When the tab bar has\n more tabs than fit, instead of moving the\n extras into the “More tabs” overflow menu they\n stay in the bar and you scroll to reach them.\n Works for top, bottom, left, and right tab\n bars. A horizontal bar scrolls with a trackpad\n swipe or shift + mouse wheel. On by default;\n turn it off to restore the overflow menu.\n The “Tab width when the tab bar is scrollable”\n advanced setting sets the scrolled tab size.\n- A new preference hides the session status\n text from tab subtitles while keeping the\n colored dot. Toggle it from the gear menu\n in the Session Status toolbelt tool.\n- Right-click a row in the Session Status\n toolbelt tool and choose Snooze to move it\n below a divider at the bottom of the list\n and dim it. A snoozed entry un-snoozes\n automatically the next time its status\n changes.\n- Contextual alternates (calt) are enabled by\n default for ligature-capable fonts.\n- Rename dialogs, the pane menu, and the context\n menu now respect dark/light mode.\n- Tab colors are written to all appearance\n variants so they survive light/dark switches.\n- Customizable IME color.\n- Cleaner glass material on the jobs popover.\n- Scroll command marks to top (instead of\n centering) on Next/Previous Mark.\n- A new profile setting, “Use ANSI colors\n for marks” (Settings > Profiles > Colors),\n draws command-prompt mark indicators using\n the color preset’s ANSI blue, red, and\n yellow instead of the built-in colors, so\n they harmonize with any theme. Off by\n default.\n- ⌘-click now linkifies very long URLs in\n full. Previously only the first couple\n thousand characters were clickable; the\n linkifier now scans onward to the next\n separator so the whole URL is detected.\n\nInput and Accessibility:\n- VoiceOver now announces terminal output and\n deleted characters.\n- Voice dictation now works.\n- A repair button was added to fix key bindings\n corrupted by a 3.4.x-3.5.x migration bug.\n- Ctrl/Option-modified function keys now work with\n third-party global shortcuts.\n- 3-finger drag is now treated like a regular drag\n for mouse reporting.\n- Offer to reset the key reporting mode when it\n gets stuck on.\n\nSearch and Selection Improvements:\n- Folded content is searched in global search.\n- URL search results extend across soft boundaries\n such as tmux pane dividers.\n- “$” is now considered part of a URL. Used when\n detecting URL boundaries for cmd-click.\n- Search results refresh after window resize.\n- New “Copy or Paste” pointer action. If there's a\n selection, it copies. Otherwise, it pastes.\n- New pointer action to copy link address.\n\nMenus and Miscellaneous:\n- New menu item to open new tabs next to the\n current tab or at the end (opposite of the\n default).\n- New “Always show alerts with remembered\n selections” option. This temporarily turns off\n the \"Remember my selection\" feature of alert\n boxes that you already saved in case you change\n your mind and want to make a different\n selection.\n- New “Buffer input by default” profile setting\n with menu item, indicator, and trigger\n integration. Buffering causes text you type to\n be stored until buffering is disabled by\n selecting Shell > Toggle Buffer Input.\n- \"Copy\" menu item on status bar components to\n copy relevant information.\n- Smart selection action URL is shown on hover.\n- Offer to restore titles when an ssh session\n ends.\n- New \"Suppress notifications from the active\n session\" setting.\n- Undo Close now has the ⌘⇧T shortcut. Show\n Tabs in Fullscreen moved to ⌘⇧U. The first\n time you press ⌘⇧T in full screen, an alert\n explains the change and can restore ⌘⇧T to\n Show Tabs in Fullscreen with a key binding.\n- Added Edit > Marks and Annotations >\n Annotate Selection, so annotating a\n selection can now be given a keyboard\n shortcut. Previously this was only on the\n right-click menu.\n- The job name shown in titles, the session\n icon, and automatic profile switching now\n reflect the foreground job actually attached\n to the terminal, hiding helper subprocesses\n that run in the foreground process group but\n are piped to their parent (for example an MCP\n server spawned by claude, or caffeinate). The\n raw deepest job is still available as the\n deepestJob variable. Note that automatic\n profile switching now matches the tty-attached\n job, so a rule keyed on such a helper name will\n no longer fire for it.\n\nCore Improvements:\n- Significant Metal rendering speedups for\n non-ASCII text should result in higher frame\n rates.\n- Faster rendering of text with many colors (e.g.\n syntax-highlighted code) by reusing attribute\n dictionaries across runs instead of rebuilding\n one per color span.\n- Framerate is capped at 30 fps in low-power mode.\n- Crash-log search was moved off the main thread\n so app launch does not hang when there are a\n huge number of crash logs.\n- Prompt for local-network permission on launch.\n- New iTerm2 menu item, Save Retrospective Debug\n Logs, writes recently captured low-frequency\n diagnostics to a file you choose, even when\n debug logging was never turned on.\n- iTerm2 now probes the local network on\n every launch so macOS keeps it in the\n Local Network privacy list. This recovers\n the permission after a tccutil reset or an\n OS upgrade, fixing commands like\n “ping 10.0.0.1” failing with “no route to\n host”.\n- Added support for light/dark color scheme\n detection and reporting (DECSET 2031 and\n DSR 996/997). Programs can query whether the\n terminal is light or dark and ask to be\n notified when it changes, so tools like vim\n and delta can adapt automatically.\n- You can now run multiple concurrent\n instances of iTerm2 side by side with\n independent settings. Launch with\n arguments:\n -suite com.iterm2.unique-id-of-your-choice.\n\nSettings Improvements:\n- Window frames are remembered per-profile rather\n than globally. When you open a window, the\n default frame will come from a previous window\n with that profile, if possible.\n- Font zoom is preserved during Automatic Profile\n Switching.\n- User-set colors are remembered separately\n from control-sequence-set colors so that they\n don't get reset by control sequence.\n- Special Exceptions has a new Add Preset\n button with common Unicode ranges such as\n Han, Hangul, and Cyrillic.\n- Profiles using a custom Command can now opt\n to run the command through the user’s login\n shell so dotfiles set $PATH and exported\n variables (API keys, GEM_HOME, etc.) before\n the command starts.\n- A linked AI chat can be hosted inline in\n its session’s right margin. Use the chat\n menu’s “Put Chat in Linked Session” item\n and toggle visibility from View > Show\n Inline Chat.\n- Open Quickly now uses recency as a\n tiebreaker, so the session list (whether\n the query is empty or matches several\n panes) is ordered most-recently-used\n first across tabs and windows. The\n current session is omitted from results\n since it is never a useful jump target,\n so Cmd-Shift-O followed by Enter now\n jumps to the most-recently-used other\n pane. A new preview side panel shows a\n snapshot of the highlighted session.\n Open Quickly can also be assigned a\n system-wide hotkey in Settings > Keys\n that works while another app is in\n front, without activating iTerm2 until\n you commit a selection.\n- Blinking cursors can now fade smoothly\n in and out instead of toggling abruptly.\n Turn on “Smooth blink” in Settings >\n Profiles > Text, where you can tune the\n fade-in and fade-out durations, easing\n curves, and how long the cursor dwells\n fully visible and fully hidden between\n fades. Off by default.\n- When you load settings from a custom\n folder, snippets, global notes, and\n session icon customizations can now sync\n along with your other settings. Because\n notes and snippets can hold sensitive\n text, iTerm2 asks once before including\n them. If the same item changed both on\n this Mac and in the folder, it asks which\n copy to keep rather than overwriting your\n changes.\n- Added Window > Tab > Select Tab > Select\n Next Tab Group (⌃⇧⌘]) and Select Previous\n Tab Group (⌃⇧⌘[). When the current tab is\n in a group, these skip over the rest of\n that group. For an ungrouped tab they\n behave like Select Next/Previous Tab.\n- Added Window > Tab > Add Tab to New Group\n and Remove Tab from Group, mirroring the\n tab bar’s right-click menu but acting on\n the current tab.\n\nBug Fixes:\n- Fixed a local privilege issue where the\n orphaned-session server adopter connected to a\n socket in a world-writable directory without\n checking who owned it. A local attacker could\n pre-create the socket and man-in-the-middle a\n restored session, capturing keystrokes and\n output. The peer's identity is now\n authenticated before anything it sends is\n trusted (CVE-2026-88514). Credit to NSEcho.\n- The selected tab’s bright HDR outline on\n macOS Tahoe is now off by default. It is\n gated behind a new “Allow HDR effects”\n advanced setting because forcing the\n display into HDR could hang some systems.\n- In the tmux Dashboard, “Open in Tabs” is now\n enabled when a single hidden window is\n selected, matching “Open in Window.” Issue\n 12994.\n- Companion Device Settings no longer prompts\n for Touch ID on its own. Opening the window\n or unpairing a device now shows a “Show QR\n Code” button, and authentication happens only\n after you click it.\n- Cmd-W now closes the Companion Device\n Settings window instead of beeping.\n- Fixed a beachball that could occur when the\n tab style preference changed: every session’s\n announcement view was re-fitted synchronously,\n each fit re-running an expensive text-layout\n search. Measurements are now cached and reused,\n and a resize to the same size no longer\n re-fits.\n- Fixed a multi-second hang that could occur\n when a window changed appearance (for example\n when another app or a second iTerm2 instance\n stole focus) while in traditional fullscreen.\n Setting a window to its current appearance no\n longer re-triggers a refresh loop.\n- On macOS Tahoe, dark-theme selected tabs no\n longer draw their chrome at a fixed image\n height while the tab cell uses a different\n height (for example left tabs with a custom\n Default tab bar height). Cap images scale to\n the pill so the dual-layer look goes away\n without needing a custom tab color.\n- Toggling Caps Lock no longer inserts an “A”\n in programs that use the Kitty keyboard\n protocol with “report all keys as escape\n codes.” Now it reports the key’s own code\n (57358) instead of a stray modifier value\n that got read as an “A.” Issue 12922.\n- Split-pane dividers in different rows or columns\n can now be dragged into alignment: a divider\n snaps to a matching divider elsewhere in the tab\n when it comes within half a cell, fixing a\n sub-cell misalignment that whole-cell steps\n could not correct. Double-clicking a horizontal\n divider to equalize the two panes also no\n longer computes the wrong size.\n- The Centered window style now looks and behaves\n just like a normal window (title bar,\n close/minimize/zoom buttons, and the same chrome\n in the Minimal and Compact themes), except that\n it stays centered. It also no longer changes\n size when you minimize and restore it.\n- In tmux -CC sessions with pane-border-status set\n to top or bottom, the pane no longer ends up a\n row too tall. Previously the bottom line went\n unused and the cursor landed a row high after\n reattaching.\n- Retrospective debug logging no longer fills its\n in-memory ring so quickly on busy hosts. Several\n high-frequency sites that logged large payloads\n (window descriptions, view hierarchies, and stack\n traces) were trimmed or moved back to regular\n debug logging, extending how far back the ring\n reaches.\n- The proxy icon in Compact and Minimal theme\n windows now hides and reveals along with the\n stoplight buttons instead of staying visible\n when they are hidden.\n- Hiding a hotkey window on macOS 26 no longer\n briefly flashes background iTerm2 windows to\n the front. The window now waits for the\n previously active app to come forward before\n ordering out.\n- Global search no longer shows blank snippets\n for results found on the main screen while an\n alternate-screen app (such as a full-screen TUI)\n is running. Snippets are now extracted from the\n same snapshot the search ran against instead of\n a freshly captured one that could drift.\n- Smart tab-title truncation no longer flickers\n between head and tail truncation when a tab\n title begins with an animated status glyph\n (such as a Claude Code spinner). The glyph is\n no longer counted when deciding which end of\n the title to truncate.\n- Session Status tool: with “Merge workgroup\n statuses” on, a merged row now shows the status\n of the peer you’re actually looking at (the\n visible peer of the switcher) instead of\n whichever peer changed most recently. A busy\n code-review peer holding a steady “working”\n status no longer loses the row to a sibling\n chat peer the moment it goes idle.\n- Tab subtitle: when a tab is split into\n multiple panes, the status of the pane you’re\n focused on now drives the subtitle instead of a\n background pane winning on status priority. For\n example, an idle pane no longer overrides the\n working pane you’re looking at. A focused pane\n with no status falls back to the highest\n priority sibling pane.\n- Code review and diff workgroup sessions now\n restore showing their last output instead of\n launching a stray replacement shell when the\n original program is gone. The code review prompt\n panel reappears only if it was open when you\n quit, and its reload button remembers the prompt\n you last submitted.\n- Session restoration no longer breaks when iTerm2\n receives a signal (such as ^C when it was\n started from a terminal): the background process\n server is now insulated from signals sent to\n iTerm2, so your running programs survive to be\n reattached on the next launch.\n- Fixed a rare case where a Claude Code workgroup\n was torn down even though its claude process was\n still running, due to a momentary misread of the\n foreground job ancestor list.\n- Fixed a case where a Claude Code workgroup pane\n (such as Diff or Code Review) could close itself\n when its command exited and take the whole\n workgroup down with it. Spawned workgroup panes\n now pin their end action so the profile’s “Close\n Sessions On End” setting can no longer flip them\n to close on their own.\n- AI chat: fixed a race where a streamed tool\n call could be dispatched with empty arguments\n when the response completed before its argument\n chunks were consumed. The agent would loop\n retrying the same call, and the turn could end\n with an empty message. Finalization now\n reconciles against the full response body.\n- SSH integration now runs the ssh command\n through your login shell, so dotfiles set up\n your environment first. A custom SSH_AUTH_SOCK\n (pointing at an agent that holds your keys) is\n now honored, fixing “Permission denied\n (publickey)” failures that only happened with\n the SSH command type.\n- On macOS Tahoe the tab activity/progress\n indicator now draws as an outline around the\n pill-shaped tab instead of a bar that crowded\n the tab title and status text.\n- Fixed a crash in Replace Selection (e.g.,\n Replace with Pretty-Printed JSON) when the\n replacement had more lines than the selection.\n Also, saved locations (marks, prompt ranges)\n inside the replaced region are now invalidated\n instead of drifting onto unrelated lines.\n- tmux integration now parses pane state\n correctly on macOS 26 and later. A new\n system NSString method had shadowed ours\n and caused numeric fields to be dropped.\n- The companion device no longer triggers a\n keychain confirmation prompt when it connects\n or when a notification is sent. The paired\n identity is now read once at launch, while you\n are present, instead of mid-connection.\n- When a companion device is paired, your AI\n API keys are now loaded at launch, and when a\n phone pairs, so a query sent from your phone\n while you are away never has to prompt for\n keychain access.\n- Opening Settings no longer reads the keychain\n or prompts for access. The AI API key row no\n longer shows an “N of 4 configured” count.\n- Restoring a window with an open inline AI chat\n panel now reopens the panel on the chat it was\n showing, instead of widening the terminal.\n- Chat lists now mark orchestrator chats with a\n wand glyph. The inline chat switcher bolds chats\n linked to the current session.\n- Orchestrator chats now handle full-screen apps\n on the alternate screen (like Claude Code)\n better: they see just the current screen and\n can use a new scroll-wheel tool to page back\n through the app’s own history.\n- Saved and copied screenshots now fill margins\n and other uncovered areas with the session’s\n background (including a background image, with\n its blend and scaling) instead of leaving them\n transparent.\n- The session-logging and archive folder\n pickers now use the folder you select rather\n than its parent when you don’t open it first.\n- SSH integration no longer leaves Ctrl+C unable\n to interrupt programs on remote hosts whose\n Python is older than 3.7 (e.g. CentOS 7,\n AlmaLinux 8). The login shell handoff was\n leaving signal generation disabled on the tty.\n- Clicking a notification posted by a background\n tab of a dedicated hotkey window now selects the\n source tab and pane instead of leaving the\n previously-selected tab in front.\n- AI chat with DeepSeek no longer fails with an\n \"insufficient tool messages\" error after a tool\n call that was interrupted or left unapproved.\n- A live filter no longer drops content that\n arrives while it is mid-search; such lines\n could previously be missing from the filtered\n view until more output came in.\n- Copying selected text from a porthole (such as\n rendered markdown) now works in apps that\n prefer rich text.\n- Clicking in a porthole in an inactive split\n pane now activates that pane.\n- Fixed several crashes: registering a profile\n hotkey for a profile with no GUID, loading a\n profile whose default-bookmark value is a\n boolean, listing processes over SSH when a\n process has an empty command, drawing margin\n background colors with a nil color, a crash when\n macOS rejected presentation options during a\n Spaces transition (now retried once it settles),\n and a divide-by-zero when the Metal view has a\n zero-size drawable.\n- iTerm2 no longer spuriously offers to reset\n terminal state or restore the tab and window\n title when the local hostname changes (for\n example when mDNS renames your .local host)\n or when you run sudo. Any two localhosts are\n now treated as the same host.\n- AI chat no longer fails with a tool_use_id\n error after an auto-approved command. The\n tool call was dropped from saved history\n while its result was kept; the prompt is now\n repaired when reloaded, healing chats saved\n by earlier versions too.\n- Session notes now keep their distance from\n the top-right corner of a session when it\n is resized, instead of from the bottom-left.\n- A tab no longer shows a stale or missing\n session status (such as “waiting”) when a\n pane carrying a status is closed, moved\n between tabs, or reparented (for example by\n entering or exiting a workgroup). The tab’s\n aggregated status is now recomputed whenever\n its set of sessions changes.\n- Full-screen programs that don’t use the\n alternate screen buffer (such as Claude\n Code) no longer destroy on-screen content\n when, on startup, they move the cursor to\n the top of the screen and repaint over it.\n The visible screen is now scrolled into\n history first. Requires shell integration.\n- When such a program moves the cursor above\n a fold and repaints, the fold is now\n scrolled into history instead of being\n overwritten and left with an orphaned\n unfold icon in the margin. This works even\n without shell integration installed.\n- The “taking a long time to check state\n restoration” prompt no longer appears so\n readily right after login, when the system\n is busy and disk I/O is slow. The integrity\n check timeout is now extended in the first\n ten minutes after boot, the prompt's\n wording is friendlier, and it defaults to\n continuing rather than discarding windows.\n Issue 11809.\n- Editing a color in a color well no longer\n silently switches the picker from sRGB to\n P3 (or back). The color space the well is\n showing is now preserved across the edit\n roundtrip. Issue 12720.\n- The Python API server now takes an\n advisory lock on its socket directory at\n startup. A second iTerm2 instance sharing\n the same application-support directory\n (e.g. two non-suited copies launched from\n different .app bundles) will refuse to\n start the API server instead of silently\n unlinking and rebinding the original\n instance’s socket.\n- Localhost detection no longer breaks when\n your computer's hostname changes (for\n example after switching networks). Whether\n a session is on the local machine is now\n recorded when the host is first reported,\n rather than recomputed later by comparing\n names. This fixes the git status bar, the\n workgroup diff list, opening local files,\n and uploads/downloads going stale after a\n rename.\n- Pressing Enter to commit an IME\n composition (e.g. Chinese, Japanese,\n Korean) in the AI Chat input no longer\n prematurely submits the unfinalized\n candidate text.\n- Command-clicking links in terminal output\n no longer crashes when command-mark data\n is unavailable.\n- Open Quickly now updates the MRU order\n when you jump to a session, so repeated\n Cmd-Shift-O Enter cycles between the two\n most-recently-used panes instead of\n drifting onto a sibling pane that\n happened to be active in the destination\n tab. Issue 12799.\n- Open Quickly recency now strictly follows\n focus order. Jumping to a pane no longer\n bumps the previously-active sibling in the\n destination window, and structural changes\n (closing a pane, splitting, restoring) in\n background windows no longer reorder the\n list, so Cmd-Shift-O Enter reliably\n toggles between the last two focused\n panes. Issue 12799.\n- OSC color overrides (foreground, background,\n cursor, selection, palette, and the iTerm2\n SetColors variants) now survive macOS Light\n / Dark appearance switches and toggles of\n the separate light / dark colors profile\n setting. The override is written to every\n appearance variant of the profile key so\n it persists until OSC 110/111 or a manual\n reset clears it.\n- In an AI orchestration chat, @-mentioning a\n session or workgroup now grants the chat\n standing permission to control it, so the\n agent acts there without the one-time\n approval prompt. A notice with a Revoke\n button is posted so you can take the\n permission back whenever you want.\n- The orchestrator’s send_text tool\n now routes payloads containing control bytes\n (ESC, Ctrl-C, etc.) as raw keystrokes instead\n of bracketed pastes. The previous wrap turned\n embedded control bytes into literal pasted\n data, so e.g. sending ESC :q ENTER to a vim\n session inserted “^[:q” as text instead of\n quitting. Pure prompt-style text still goes\n through as a paste, as Ink-based TUIs expect.\n- The orchestrator’s get_screen_contents\n tool now marks faint text with ⟨dim⟩…⟨/dim⟩ and\n the cursor with ⟨cursor⟩. Inline shell\n suggestions and ghost completions are rendered\n faint and used to be indistinguishable from\n text the user actually typed, so the agent\n would sometimes mistake a suggestion for an\n entered command.\n- Showing the clippings panel in a window\n zoomed to fit the display no longer grows\n the window horizontally. The tab content\n area absorbs the panel’s width instead,\n mirroring the toolbelt’s behavior.\n- The Install Claude Code Integration intro is\n now presented as a real sheet attached to the\n installer panel, rather than a subview\n overlay. Enter now correctly activates\n Continue instead of the Install/Next button\n it was visually covering.\n- Workgroup peer-switch and toolbar shortcuts\n now fire no matter which view in the terminal\n window has keyboard focus, including the Diff\n peer’s Run Anyway button, the Code Review\n prompt’s text view, and the clippings table.\n They are now dispatched centrally instead of\n relying on the focused view to forward them.\n- The Claude Code installer now reliably adds\n Enter/Exit Workgroup triggers to divorced\n sessions whose original profile was picked.\n The previous pass matched on the dict’s\n KEY_ORIGINAL_GUID, which could be stale\n after an arrangement reload. The walk now\n also consults each live or buried session’s\n runtime originalProfile reference.\n- The Code Review prompt overlay no longer\n drifts out of sync with light/dark mode. The\n panel cached a CGColor snapshot of\n windowBackgroundColor resolved against an\n arbitrary NSAppearance.current, so its\n background could disagree with its own text\n view. It now uses the existing layer-backed\n solid color view, which refreshes the color\n in updateLayer under the correct drawing\n appearance.\n- The heavy window border no longer leaves its\n corners unbordered on low-opacity windows. The\n corner-radius detector failed on transparent\n windows (their interior is not opaque enough),\n so the border fell back to a wrong radius and\n the corner arc was clipped away. Detection now\n works at any opacity, with a better per-OS\n fallback radius. Issue 12882.\n- The heavy window border now stays flush with\n the window edges instead of sometimes drawing\n as a smaller inner rounded rectangle. Its view\n could be left at a stale size and was not kept\n in sync with the window. Issue 12882.\n- The heavy window border now has a uniform\n width: its corners no longer look thinner than\n its edges and no longer pick up semi-\n transparent pixels. It is drawn entirely\n inside the window instead of straddling the\n window edge and relying on the system mask to\n clip its outer half. Issue 12882.\n- The window border color advanced settings now\n accept a four-part color like #rrggbbaa (or\n p3#rrggbbaa) to set the border opacity. A\n color without an alpha component is still\n drawn at 75% opacity. Issue 12882.\n- The legacy (non-GPU) renderer now invalidates\n only the lines that actually changed, instead\n of repainting the entire view on every cell\n update. Heavy TUI animations (e.g. spinners\n that rewrite a single line at high frequency)\n use significantly less CPU on hosts where the\n GPU renderer is disabled. Issue 12790.\n- Anthropic tool calls reloaded from a chat\n transcript would fail with HTTP 400 because\n the function output got serialized as plain\n text instead of a tool_result block. The\n parser stored the tool_use id only on the\n inner FunctionCall while the serializer\n looked on the outer wrapper, so the id was\n lost on round-trip. Both fields are now\n populated so persisted tool calls survive\n the round-trip.\n- Anthropic requests no longer 400 when a\n stray user message gets persisted between a\n tool_use and its matching tool_result. The\n request builder reorders so the tool_result\n is always emitted immediately after the\n tool_use, with any intervening messages\n bumped to after it.\n- Fix a launch crash when a saved status bar\n layout referenced an RPC-provided text\n component whose configuration was missing\n the registration request (for example, from\n a partial profile restoration). The base64\n decoder dereferenced a NULL pointer instead\n of returning nil. Issue 12861.\n- The “Respect shortcuts from System Settings >\n Keyboard > Keyboard Shortcuts > Keyboard”\n option used subset matching on modifier keys,\n so a system shortcut bound to Ctrl+Right (or\n any other strict subset of an event’s\n modifiers) would also intercept\n Ctrl+Shift+Right and similar keystrokes.\n Modifier comparison is now exact. Issue 12859.\n- When launched with -suite, orphan adoption\n could no longer reattach to surviving\n multi-server daemons after a state-database\n loss. The application-support directory is\n already isolated per suite, but daemon\n sockets were also being named with the\n suite in the filename, while the orphan\n adopter (and the settings-export exclusion\n list) still expected the plain\n iterm2-daemon- prefix. The suite is now\n carried only by the containing directory,\n matching how the dotdir fallback already\n worked. A transitional fallback accepts\n the {suite}-daemon-N.socket form so betas\n that wrote that name don't strand their\n daemons across the upgrade. Issue 12860.\n- Fix a crash when a Python API client invoked\n a function whose argument string contained\n non-BMP characters (for example, emoji). The\n expression tokenizer’s optional-path\n recognizer indexed by Unicode scalar while\n the surrounding tokenizer tracks UTF-16\n positions, so the offset could run past the\n scalar count and trap. Issue 12856.\n- When ESC #3, #4, or #6 (DECDHL/DECDWL) was\n received mid-line, characters already on the\n line were correctly doubled in size but the\n cursor was not advanced to compensate, so\n text printed afterward overwrote the right\n half of the existing characters. The cursor\n is now repositioned when the line’s width\n class changes.\n- ssh:// URL handling and Bonjour-discovered\n host profiles now find ssh through the user’s\n login shell PATH, so Homebrew’s ssh (or any\n ssh outside /usr/bin) is used. The advanced\n setting SshSchemePath, when changed from its\n default, still wins. Issue 3415.\n- The faint-text opacity slider in Profiles >\n Colors now has a minimum of 0.1, and existing\n profiles with a stored value below 0.1 are\n clamped at runtime. A value of 0 caused SGR 2\n text to render in exactly the background\n color, making it invisible. The slider’s\n tooltip was also incorrect (it described\n minimum contrast); it now describes faint\n text opacity.\n- The “open password manager automatically”\n feature no longer triggers when a shell\n script briefly disables terminal echo to\n suppress the visible echo of an OSC report\n response (e.g. it2getvar, it2profile -g).\n ECHO must now stay off for at least 0.2\n seconds — tunable via the\n DetectPasswordInputDebounce advanced\n setting — before iTerm2 treats it as a\n password prompt.\n- Fix the Minimal-theme tab bar overlapping the\n terminal in fullscreen when adding a second\n tab with “Hide tab bar when there is only\n one tab” enabled. The titlebar accessory\n hosting the tab bar grew from zero to its\n full height but the content view was not\n re-laid out to leave room for it. Issue\n 12811.\n- The Touch ID for sudo offer no longer relies on\n AppleScript admin elevation, which fails on\n macOS 26 with EPERM when writing to\n /etc/pam.d/. Accepting the offer now lets you\n copy the install command or run it in a new\n iTerm2 window where you can enter your sudo\n password.\n- Fix a bug where suppressing a warning would\n not stick across launches when prefs are\n loaded from a custom folder with “Save\n changes: Automatically.” The debounced save\n could lose the race against app exit, so\n the next launch overwrote the just-saved\n suppression with the stale remote copy.\n applicationWillTerminate now flushes\n synchronously when Automatic mode is on.\n- Fix Ctrl-letter keystrokes appearing as\n literal text (e.g., “0x4” for Ctrl-D) in\n tmux -CC mode when the tmux server has\n extended-keys enabled and an inner app\n has turned on modifyOtherKeys. Control\n bytes 0x00–0x1F are now sent through\n send-keys with the -H flag on tmux\n 3.0a or newer, which bypasses tmux 3.5’s\n silent literal-text fallthrough for\n unmodified C0 bytes. Issue 12845.\n- Fix dragging a tab or split pane onto a\n window with only one tab. The destination\n window’s tab bar is hidden in that case, so\n AppKit could not deliver drag events to it\n and a brand-new window was created instead.\n Tab bars are now temporarily shown for the\n duration of any tab or session drag.\n Issue 12846.\n- Fix a latent use-after-free in Core Text run\n enumeration when the font does not return\n contiguous glyph or string-index pointers.\n The fallback NSMutableData backing buffers\n could be released before the closure ran.\n- Fix the cursor jumping to the end while\n typing in an annotation when the caret was\n not already at the end. The optimistic\n main-thread write echoed back through the\n delegate and reassigned the text view’s\n string, which resets the selection.\n- Fix a crash when invoking Undo after closing\n a split pane that contained an annotation,\n session note, or chat input. These text\n views now own their own undo manager so\n text-edit undo actions can’t outlive the\n text view in the window’s undo stack.\n- Fix UI hangs when opendirectoryd is slow or\n wedged. The user’s login shell is now\n fetched in the pidinfo XPC service (so a\n daemon hang only burns the helper, never\n the main app) and cached in-process. Every\n call still kicks off a fresh background\n lookup so the cache stays current after\n chsh, but a slow lookup no longer stalls\n the UI — the cached value is returned if a\n fresh result doesn’t arrive in time.\n- Fix a multi-second hang when closing the\n Settings window. PreferenceInfo now only\n registers a notification observer when an\n observer block is set, avoiding O(N²)\n teardown across the many controls in the\n panel.\n- Fix a state-restoration crash where the\n recovery path could leave records with no\n rowid in the in-memory tree, causing the\n next save to abort with MissingRowID. The\n recovery now operates on a deep copy of\n the failed save’s tree so it can’t corrupt\n records other code still holds, and it\n dedupes siblings by (key, identifier) so\n every record gets a fresh rowid.\n- Fix the window growing wider when a\n clippings panel appears in a non-current\n tab. Window resize now happens only when\n the visible tab needs the space.\n- Fix Code Review (and any other long\n command) failing immediately with a zsh\n \"unmatched '\" error. The shell-arg\n splitter used by startProgram was running\n every unquoted argv element through\n -stringByExpandingTildeInPath, which\n silently truncates at 1024 chars. Long\n arguments now skip the expansion when\n there's no leading ~.\n- Fix shell escaping of embedded apostrophes\n and CR/LF in single-quote (POSIX) and\n $'...' (bash) modes. Apostrophes now use\n the close-escape-reopen idiom ('\\''), and\n control chars round-trip cleanly through\n the receiving shell instead of either\n injecting a literal backslash or producing\n an unmatched-quote parse error.\n- Fix the password-manager CLI picker\n greying out interpreter scripts (such as\n the Homebrew-installed Bitwarden ‘bw’,\n which is a Node.js script). Also accept\n the symlink target name (e.g. bw.js).\n- Improved energy efficiency.\n- Fix bell and other user notifications not being\n posted when iTerm2 is not hidden behind another\n app. Notifications now post whenever iTerm2 is\n not the active app or the session’s window is\n not frontmost.\n- Fix ⌥Y and the Character Viewer inserting\n a backslash instead of ¥ on non-JIS\n keyboards. Restore ⌥+¥ sending ESC+\\\n (with Option=Esc+) on JIS keyboards.\n- Fix a bug where a restored session whose\n working directory was on an unmounted drive\n could fail to relaunch, closing the tab. The\n session now starts in your home directory and\n shows a notice. Issue 12955.\n- Fix various crashes.\n- Fix wrong terminal size after exiting\n fullscreen with multiple tabs.\n- Fix tmux window shrink when a pre-tmux\n session is buried after attach.\n- Fix theme not updating when changed via\n the Python API.\n- Fix legacy hotkey window profiles\n defaulting to 100% width and height.\n- Fix NSTextInputClient to use consistent\n absolute coordinates.\n- Work around a macOS SVG bug that dropped\n elements referencing IDs nested in\n wrappers inside .\n- Fix trigger color wells not prefilling\n with the trigger’s actual colors.\n- Fix session tab color across appearance\n changes.\n- Fix GPU renderer not respecting a custom\n underline color for non-ASCII text.\n- Fix the color space of the block cursor\n in Metal.\n- Fix wrong session used for GPU frame\n capture.\n- Fix tmux italic rewriting not being\n skipped when TERM matches *tmux*.\n- Fix cursor shape being reset to a block\n instead of the profile default when\n programs use the terminfo Se capability\n (e.g. Neovim 0.12+).\n- Fix paste bracketing race that could\n cause out-of-order chunks.\n- Fix posix_spawn failing with “disclaim\n children” enabled.\n- Fix async_set_profile() failing with\n REQUEST_MALFORMED on certain keys.\n- Fix tmux close dialog Cancel button not\n preventing window close.\n- Fix shell integration not working with\n Login Shell mode.\n- Fix Nerd Font Bundle not persisting\n after restart.\n- Fix full-bleed drawing of unfilled\n Powerline arrow glyphs.\n- Fix multipart Kitty image transmission\n where independently-encoded padded\n chunks did not decode.\n- Fix Kitty image transmission rejecting\n paths containing /dev/.\n- Fix a “not clearing enough of character\n images” rendering bug.\n- Fix adaptive framerate always overriding\n the non-adaptive setting.\n- Fix status bar hidden behind the tab bar\n in fullscreen.\n- Fix clearing a filter in a maximized\n pane causing the window to shrink.\n- Fix divorced session drag-to-split\n losing divorce state.\n- Fix a background-tab token execution\n hang when tabs were not rescheduled.\n- Fix a per-window settings save bug.\n- Fix “dynamic” tag being added to dynamic\n profiles when unwanted.\n- Migrate legacy dynamic profiles that\n remained in user defaults.\n- Mark navigation now visits marks created\n by triggers, not just prompt marks.\n- Fix tmux -CC alternate screen not showing\n in apps like vim.\n- Fix memory leaks.\n- Prompt before opening a .iterm2arrangement\n file that has been downloaded from the\n internet, because arrangements can embed\n profiles that run arbitrary commands.\n Reported by @naxus-audit.\n- Fix “Save Current Window to File with\n Contents…” producing a file that had no\n contents.\n- Add dual-mode SGR colors so programs can\n emit different fg/bg colors for light vs\n dark mode, keeping scrollback readable\n across appearance toggles. Use CSI\n 38:12:Rl:Gl:Bl:Rd:Gd:Bd m for 24-bit and\n CSI 38:13:Nl:Nd m for 256-color (and 48\n for backgrounds).\n- Extend dual-mode SGR to underline color\n via CSI 58:12:Rl:Gl:Bl:Rd:Gd:Bd m and\n CSI 58:13:Nl:Nd m.\n- Fix tab graphic matching when the\n foreground binary is a versioned symlink\n target (e.g., claude → 2.1.121) by also\n consulting argv0 and the command line,\n and by walking up to a non-shell ancestor\n when the deepest foreground job has no\n icon match.\n- Fix the session’s hostname variable\n going stale when gethostname() at session\n init returned a value (e.g., from a\n transient DHCP-provided name) that\n differed from the OSC RemoteHost the\n shell later sent. Now a host change is\n also detected when the new value differs\n from the most recently pushed one, not\n only from the interval tree’s last-line\n host.\n- Add an advanced setting (off by default)\n to set IT2_APP_PATH on new sessions, so\n the “it2” CLI targets this iTerm2 build\n via AppleScript when multiple iTerm2\n builds are running. Useful for iTerm2\n developers.\n- Fix memory leaks in the doubly-linked\n list backing iTermCache and the Swift\n LRUDictionary: every cached entry was\n retained forever once the cache was\n dropped. Also fix LRU bump leaving a\n stale node reference, which corrupted\n eviction order after a re-insert.\n- Fix paste-history scores using a\n different epoch for freshly saved entries\n vs. entries loaded from disk, which could\n cause inconsistent ordering if score-\n based sorting/eviction were used.\n- Request the badge authorization option\n from UNUserNotificationCenter so the\n dock-tile bell-count badge actually\n renders and the “Badge application\n icon” toggle appears in System\n Settings › Notifications › iTerm.\n- Hide a top-right status indicator while\n the mouse is hovering an inline button\n it overlaps, so the button stays\n reachable.\n- Honor IT2_APP_PATH in the iterm2\n Python library’s AppleScript cookie\n request, matching the “it2” CLI. Lets\n external scripts targeting a -suite\n dev build get a cookie from the right\n iTerm2 instance instead of whichever\n one macOS routes to. Issue 12850.\n- Honor IT2_SUITE when checking for\n disable-automation-auth in the iterm2\n Python library, so external scripts\n targeting a -suite instance no longer\n silently fail with 401 when stock\n iTerm2 has “Always Allow All Apps”\n enabled. Issue 12864.\n- Add advanced settings to customize\n the window border color for focused\n and unfocused windows (requires\n “Show border around windows” with\n no title bar).\n- Add a per-profile “Contextual Alternates”\n toggle to the font picker’s options menu,\n for fonts that expose calt. Lets you keep\n calt-based coding ligatures on for one\n profile while turning off surprising\n substitutions (e.g. Monaspace rendering\n [] as a checkbox) on another. Issue 12847.\n- Add an Erase All Settings and Data\n button in Settings > General >\n Settings. It offers to export first,\n then erases preferences, profiles,\n saved sessions, scripts, and the\n contents of ~/.iterm2 and the app\n support folder, then quits\n immediately. Issue 12852.\n- Add keyboard shortcut bindings to\n workgroup toolbar items. Navigation\n cluster (back, forward, reload) and\n the standalone reload toolbar item\n each support a per-action shortcut,\n with defaults of ctrl-cmd-left,\n ctrl-cmd-right, and cmd-R. Shortcuts\n take precedence over profile key\n bindings and main menu items but\n defer to global key bindings.\n- Add an optional per-peer shortcut\n for jumping to a peer in a workgroup.\n Configurable in the workgroup\n session settings; when unset, the\n built-in opt-shift-cmd-number\n fallback still works. Custom peer\n shortcuts route through the same\n pipeline as workgroup toolbar\n shortcuts, so they beat profile\n bindings and the main menu but\n defer to global key bindings.\n- Fix vertical window resize being\n silently rejected when the window\n sits partly below the screen.\n- Coalesce pending re-evaluations of\n interpolated strings. Previously,\n every dependency change enqueued a\n fresh dispatch_async on the main\n queue, so a tmux -CC pane title that\n changed many times per second could\n pile millions of blocks onto the main\n queue and exhaust memory. Issue\n 12857.\n- Built-in browser: hide our message\n handlers and skip the page-world\n Notification, geolocation, console,\n and audio bridges inside Cloudflare\n Turnstile, hCaptcha, and similar\n challenge frames so the captcha\n probe sees an unmodified Safari\n surface.\n- Built-in browser geolocation: stop\n silently dropping requests when\n permission is not granted; the page\n now receives a PERMISSION_DENIED\n error instead of hanging. Push the\n decision to the calling frame so\n navigator.permissions.query reflects\n reality, and route success and error\n callbacks back to the originating\n iframe instead of always the main\n frame. Stop persisting system-level\n denials (Location Services off, OS\n prompt declined) as per-site\n decisions; only an explicit Allow\n or Block in the per-site dialog is\n remembered. The same fix applies to\n notification permissions.\n- Honor the option-modified “Quit and\n Close All Windows” menu item. iTerm2\n now samples the option modifier at\n quit time and erases saved state when\n the user picked the option-modified\n menu item, so it actually closes all\n windows on the next launch. Issue\n 12852.\n- Clear tab status when a session’s\n program terminates (broken pipe or\n tmux detach) so stale status text\n and indicators no longer linger.\n- Fix a stall after large output bursts\n when the main thread was reading every\n line of the burst to announce it for\n VoiceOver. Two changes: the LineBlock\n metadata provider no longer allocates\n a heap block per inner-loop iteration\n (which was churning through the objc\n associated-objects table on release),\n and the new-output announcement is\n capped to the most recent 256 lines so\n giant floods stop overwhelming\n VoiceOver’s speech queue.\n- Fix Session Status toolbelt rows\n appearing in arbitrary order when\n the toolbelt is first opened. The\n initial population now sorts by\n priority like every other update\n path.\n- Add a Diff workgroup session mode\n that defers running the configured\n command until there are staged or\n unstaged changes to show. The Claude\n Code workgroup’s Diff peer uses it,\n so the peer no longer launches\n difftool on a clean tree. Existing\n Claude Code workgroups are migrated\n on first launch so the Diff peer\n picks up the new mode automatically;\n flip it back to Regular in Settings\n if you prefer the old behavior.\n- Add a urlHandlerCommand advanced\n setting. When set, clicking a URL\n (including OSC 8 hyperlinks and\n auto-detected URLs) runs it as a\n /bin/sh -c task with the URL in\n \\(url) and a background-open flag in\n \\(background), instead of handing the\n URL to the system. Its output goes to\n the Script Console. Lets you route\n links through your own script, e.g. to\n open in the background or pick a\n browser mode. Empty by default, so\n normal URL opening is unchanged.\n Issue 12878.\n- Script Console no longer jumps to the\n top when a new entry arrives. The\n script list keeps its scroll position\n and selection, and a filtered log view\n stays put as new lines come in.\n- Fix black, unreadable text in the\n Script Console Logs tab in dark mode.\n- Session Stats now shows the correct\n keyboard shortcut for workgroup peers\n that have a custom peer-switch\n shortcut configured, instead of always\n showing the built-in ⌥⇧⌘ number.\n- Jobs toolbelt and the status bar job\n popover gain a “Notify on Termination”\n right-click option. Monitored jobs show\n a bell next to their name, and when the\n chosen process exits a modal alert names\n the job and process ID.\n- The badge no longer hides behind the\n clippings panel. It now right-aligns\n at the inner edge of the right-gutter\n panel area in both the legacy and GPU\n renderers.\n- AI text-typing tools (send_text in\n orchestration mode and\n insert_text_at_cursor in\n session-bound mode) now accept a\n small backslash-escape vocabulary so\n the agent can drive control keys.\n Recognized: \\\\ for a literal\n backslash, \\n, \\r, \\t, and \\uXXXX\n (four hex digits) for any Unicode\n scalar. Set append_newline to false\n for a standalone control key (e.g.\n Escape) so iTerm2 doesn’t tack a\n newline onto it. Lets the agent\n send things\n like for Ctrl-D, for\n Ctrl-Z, for Ctrl-L, and\n for Escape.\n- Implement start_session for the\n orchestrator. The agent can now\n spawn a new window, tab, or\n vertical split via the existing\n start_session tool. Every spawn\n prompts for user approval inline\n in the chat, with the placement\n and command (if any) shown in the\n prompt. Returns the new session’s\n synthetic workgroup_id so the\n agent can immediately drive the\n new session with the other tools.\n- Dropping a file onto the AI chat\n text field now offers to attach it\n when the current backend supports\n that file type, instead of always\n inserting its path as text. The\n choice can be remembered.\n- AI chat can now attach images to\n OpenAI models (vision), and audio\n to OpenAI audio models on the\n chat-completions API. Attachment\n support is now decided by modality\n rather than a per-format allow\n list: if a backend can carry an\n image, PDF, audio, or video block,\n you can attach it, and an\n unsupported file is reported by the\n model instead of being silently\n refused.\n- Announcements now become narrower\n when the clippings panel is visible\n so they no longer overlap it.\n- Python API: GetPromptResponse now\n has excluded_subranges, listing the\n cell regions inside the command\n range that the shell marked as\n non-input via OSC 133;A;k=s|c|r\n (PS2 prefixes, right-prompt cells).\n Available on responses to\n async_get_last_prompt and\n async_get_prompt_by_id. Always\n empty on PromptMonitor.PROMPT\n notifications — fetch via the RPC\n after a COMMAND_END notification\n for the populated value.\n- OSC 133 aid= now tracks nested\n shell-integration sessions: a local\n shell that runs ssh into a remote\n shell with shell integration (or a\n REPL that emits its own A/B/C/D)\n no longer confuses iTerm2's mark\n bookkeeping when the inner D's\n arrive before the outer's. Each\n mark carries the aid from its\n A/B/C/D markers and a parentAid\n pointing at the open mark it\n nested under. D;aid=X closes the\n matching command instead of the\n topmost. If an outer command dies\n (ssh tunnel drop, etc.) without\n the inner ever closing, the outer\n D cascade-closes every still-open\n mark whose parentAid chain leads\n back to it so no inner mark leaks\n as still-running.\n- Disable automatic text substitutions\n in the AI chat input field, so things\n like -- no longer turn into an em\n dash and smart quotes, autocorrect,\n and text replacements stay off.\n- Fix a bug where installing the Claude\n Code integration added its workgroup\n triggers to the settings UI but never\n wrote them back to user defaults, so\n they vanished on the next launch.\n Uninstalling had the mirror bug. Both\n now flush the profile model to disk.\n- The default AI timeout is now 5 minutes\n instead of 30 seconds, giving slow\n models and long responses more time to\n complete before giving up.\n- AI orchestration: opening a new session\n with no command no longer prompts for\n permission. When the agent supplies a\n command, the auto-mode safety check\n vets it and approves automatically if\n it passes, only prompting otherwise.\n- The Claude Code integration setup\n wizard is easier to navigate. Show\n Toolbelt can now be skipped, the\n default button reflects whether each\n step is already done (Install when it\n is not, Next/Close when it is), and\n the Before You Start sheet has an\n always-visible link to a web page\n explaining the whole integration.\n- The orchestrator can now\n watch sessions that report no\n status (plain TUIs, coding agents\n that don't emit status, bare\n shells). register_watch falls back\n to reading the screen with a quiet\n background AI poller and delivers\n the usual status_update when the\n target state is reached, instead of\n noisily polling in the chat. It\n gives up after five minutes with a\n watch_timed_out update.\n- The orchestrator's watchers\n can now watch for a plain-English\n condition (e.g. “emacs has exited”),\n judged by reading the screen, in\n addition to idle/working/waiting\n states. Sessions also report whether\n their status is announced by the\n program or merely inferred, so the\n agent picks the reliable watch form\n instead of misreading a splash\n screen as “idle”.\n- An orchestrator watcher waiting on a\n session's reported status no longer\n hangs forever if that status gets\n stuck. After five minutes without the\n expected change it falls back to\n reading the screen to confirm the\n real state.\n- Workgroup code-review/diff sessions no\n longer hang on “waiting for changes” in\n a checkout full of untracked files. The\n per-file diff list now excludes\n untracked files, so git rename detection\n no longer hashes every untracked file in\n the tree and the poll finishes within\n the git timeout.\n- The Workgroups settings pane has a\n help button in the bottom-right\n corner that opens the Workgroups\n documentation.\n- Next/Previous Pane (cmd-[ and cmd-])\n work again after using Session >\n Restart Session on a maximized pane.\n Restart gives the session a new GUID,\n and the maximized reading-order list\n was keyed by GUID, so it could no\n longer find the restarted pane. The\n order now tracks panes in a way that\n survives a restart and other in-place\n session swaps.\n- Fix a bug where Fold All and Clear\n Buffer left inline views (such as\n imgcat images) behind. Folded views\n are now hidden and restored on\n unfold.\n- Fix a bug where the Jobs toolbelt was\n empty after restoring a session until\n the next job or shell prompt. Restored\n sessions now populate it immediately.\n- The Session Status toolbelt tool has a\n bell toggle in its bottom-left corner.\n When armed, the next time any session’s\n status (waiting/idle/busy) changes it\n shows an alert and turns itself back\n off. A brief flicker that returns to\n the original status does not count.\n Window > Notify on Status Change\n (shift-cmd-X) toggles the same thing\n from the menu.\n- The Cockpit window now shows each\n session’s detail string under its\n name when grouping by status.\n- The Cockpit window has a bell button\n that arms notify-on-status-change for\n the selected window or session, and\n rows show a bell when armed. The\n per-window watch is shared with the\n Window menu and the Session Status\n tool; per-session watches are new.\n Notify-on-status-change now works\n whether or not the Session Status\n toolbelt tool is open.\n- Killing or reloading claude in a Claude\n Code workgroup peer (such as the Code\n Review or Diff peer) no longer exits the\n whole workgroup. The Exit Workgroup\n trigger has a new leader-only option, set\n by the Claude Code installer, so only the\n main session's claude ending tears the\n workgroup down. Existing setups are\n upgraded automatically.\n- Job Ended triggers now fire reliably\n when a foreground job exits, including\n when it exits together with the\n session. Job started/ended detection is\n now event driven (from the process\n monitor) rather than inferred from the\n periodic title poll, which could miss a\n job's termination.\n- The notify-on-status-change alert has a\n Reveal button that jumps to the session\n whose status changed. A window watch\n now covers every session in the window,\n including ones created after it was\n armed.\n- Workgroups are now tracked by a stable\n identifier, so a workgroup whose main\n session had already closed can still\n be exited and torn down instead of\n leaking and stranding its remaining\n sessions invisibly.\n- Session lookup now also searches\n buried sessions' workgroup peer ports\n and the workgroup registry, so a peer\n whose only visible sibling was buried\n can still be found and revealed, e.g.\n from the Session Status tool.\n- A Session Status row whose session\n cannot be found renders blank instead\n of showing a recycled row's content.\n- Switching between workgroup peers no\n longer wipes a session's entry from\n the Session Status tool. The per-tab\n status rollup was borrowing the\n session's identity and clearing it\n when the visible peer changed.\n- Sort JSON keys in the Responses API\n request so OpenAI prompt caching gets\n a stable prefix and reliably hits the\n cache.\n- Terminal-initiated downloads (it2dl)\n now ask whether to save to the\n Downloads folder or to choose a\n destination. The choice is\n silenceable, so you can make it\n always save to Downloads or always\n show a save panel. Issue 12897.\n- Settings > Keys > “Force keyboard” gains\n an option to only force the keyboard\n locale once per session, the first time\n a newly created terminal receives\n keyboard focus, instead of forcing it\n continuously. Restored sessions are not\n affected.\n- Fix a bug where a pane could restore\n in your home directory instead of its\n previous working directory after\n restarting iTerm2. Sessions that used\n automatic profile switching saved a\n value that could not be read back,\n which silently dropped the rest of\n the restored session state.\n- OSC 9;4 progress bars now draw over\n the profile's background color\n instead of a fixed black or white\n background, so they blend with your\n theme. Issue 12916.\n- Fix OSC 8 file:// hyperlinks whose\n path mixes percent-encoding with raw\n non-ASCII characters (such as\n ripgrep's --hyperlink-format=file\n output) opening the wrong file. The\n percent signs were being double-\n encoded on macOS 14 and later. Issue\n 12914.\n- Fix a bug where a dashed underline on\n screen, with ligatures enabled, could\n make box-drawing lines (such as a TUI\n input box) render dashed instead of\n solid. Issue 12907.\n- In the Minimal theme, when panes in a\n tab have two or more different tab\n colors, each pane's title bar is now\n tinted with its own tab color so you\n can tell them apart. When the panes\n share one color (or none) the title\n bar keeps blending with the terminal\n background as before. Issue 12903.\n- Fix dead-key composition (such as US\n International apostrophe then e) losing\n its accent under the Kitty keyboard\n protocol when both “report all keys as\n escape codes” and “report associated\n text” are enabled. Issue 12906.\n- AI Chat messages now use a narrower,\n more readable layout with clearer\n command and error formatting.\n- AI Chat permission prompts now show\n proposed commands in code blocks.\n- AI Chat command output now renders\n as rounded Markdown code blocks.\n- AI Chat terminal-command transcripts\n now render captured output as rounded\n code blocks.\n- AI Chat's floating toolbar now omits\n the chat title and resizes when model\n controls appear or disappear.\n- AI Chat can select model-supported\n reasoning effort and OpenAI service\n tier from the chat toolbar.\n- AI Chat now hides reasoning effort\n and OpenAI service tier choices for\n models that do not declare support\n for them.\n- AI Chat can hide and show its chat\n list without reopening the window.\n- AI Chat's chat-list toggle now stays\n at the top of the chat area when a\n window is first restored.\n- AI Chat history entries can now be\n selected in groups and deleted with a\n single confirmation.\n- AI Chat history entries can now be\n renamed from the chat list.\n- AI Chat no longer sends unsupported\n low reasoning effort to OpenAI Pro\n models.\n- AI Chat now reloads past tool runs as\n transcript text, preventing OpenAI\n reasoning models from rejecting old\n function calls after a chat is\n reopened.\n- AI Chat now closes OpenAI Responses\n tool calls with an explicit tool\n output on local tool failures, which\n prevents follow-up turns from failing\n with missing function output errors.\n- AI Chat no longer crashes while opening\n if the toolbar refreshes before the chat\n view is loaded.\n- AI Chat now keeps API keys separate per\n provider, so Claude is not sent a key\n saved for another AI service.\n- AI Chat no longer asks for an API key\n when the active provider already has\n one saved in Keychain.\n- AI Chat now checks saved API keys\n before showing plugin setup warnings.\n- AI Chat now sends Gemini-compatible\n function schemas for tool arguments\n that accept multiple JSON types.\n- AI Chat now renders orchestration\n session command tool calls as command\n blocks instead of exposing raw session\n GUIDs and JSON arguments.\n- AI Chat now lets new chats choose their\n AI provider from the chat toolbar, while\n keeping the provider fixed after the\n conversation starts.\n- AI Chat's provider picker now includes\n local Llama models and a manual model\n configuration option when configured.\n- AI Chat can now manage multiple manual\n AI model configurations and select them\n from the chat model picker.\n- A manual AI model now takes precedence\n over a built-in model with the same\n name, so you can proxy a known model\n through a custom URL by reusing its\n name.\n- Manual AI models now have a “Supports\n temperature” checkbox, set from the\n preset, so a manual model cloned from\n Anthropic Opus 4.7+ no longer sends a\n temperature the API rejects. Existing\n manual models inherit this from the\n matching built-in. The OpenAI and\n DeepSeek request paths now honor this\n setting too, not just Anthropic.\n- Manual AI models now have a\n “Configurable thinking” checkbox, set\n from the preset, so a manual reasoning\n model (such as a GPT-5 or DeepSeek model\n behind a proxy) keeps its thinking\n toggle. Same-named clones also inherit\n the built-in’s reasoning-effort and\n service-tier choices.\n- Manual AI model settings now show saved\n models in a list instead of a pop-up\n menu.\n- AI Chat now locks the provider picker\n immediately after a conversation starts.\n- AI Chat now locks the model picker\n immediately after a conversation starts.\n- AI Chat now shows orchestration terminal\n command results as raw code blocks and\n keeps session GUIDs out of user-facing\n tool summaries.\n- AI settings now choose the default\n model for new chats from one selector,\n including saved manual AI models.\n- You can now designate a manual AI model\n as the “economy” model (leaf toggle in\n Manual AI Models). It is used for cheap\n background jobs like command-safety\n checks and screen-idle detection instead\n of your main model.\n- Fixed a crash when using Undo after\n closing a split pane that contained a\n command composer.\n- Closing the inline AI chat panel now\n returns keyboard focus to the terminal.\n- Companion relay connections now retry\n with capped backoff after failures,\n instead of reconnecting (and, during\n pairing, regenerating the QR code) many\n times per second when the relay is\n unreachable, for example due to a bad\n TLS certificate. Connection errors\n during pairing are now shown in the\n pairing window.\n- Companion pairing connection failures\n now report the underlying error (for\n example the TLS or HTTP problem)\n instead of an unhelpful generic\n PluginError message.\n- The Claude Code integration's session status\n now stays “working” while background agents\n or background tasks are still running, so\n AI chat watchers no longer fire on the idle\n lulls between subagent phases. Requires\n Claude Code 2.1.198 or later.\n- AI orchestration: workgroups and sessions in\n the agent's snapshot now carry provenance,\n such as which trigger entered a workgroup\n and which chat's agent created a session,\n so an agent is not confused by workgroups\n that other windows or chats created.\n- AI orchestration: session snapshots, screen\n contents, and watcher status updates now\n report how long ago the screen last\n changed, so agents can tell stale content\n from fresh activity.\n- The AI chat wire log now redacts API keys\n (in request headers, URLs, and bodies),\n keeping only a short fingerprint.\n- Chats can now be muted from the iTerm2\n Buddy app. A muted chat sends no push\n notifications to the phone, even for\n permission requests, until it is\n unmuted. The muted set is remembered\n on the Mac so it applies even while\n the phone is unreachable.\n- AI chats reopened after a restart now\n replay their tool-call history to the\n model faithfully. Reasoning state\n (OpenAI encrypted reasoning, Gemini\n thought signatures) is saved with the\n chat and replayed, so reasoning models\n no longer reject reopened chats or\n invent fake tool results. Chats from\n older versions keep working through a\n compatibility path.\n- A chat is now locked to the AI\n provider it started with, enforced in\n the model layer as well as the UI.\n Messages sent from the iTerm2 Buddy\n app now run on the chat's own model\n instead of the global default.\n- The iTerm2 companion relay servers moved\n to new addresses. If you paired an iPhone\n on an earlier build, pair it again so it\n keeps working.\n- AI orchestration: commands the agent runs in\n a session now go through the same automatic\n safety check as regular AI chat, instead of\n running once a session is approved. This\n covers both execute_command and commands the\n agent types at a shell prompt and submits.\n Risky commands are held for your approval\n rather than run automatically.\n- AI orchestration: keystrokes the agent sends\n into a full-screen program (vim, less, htop,\n a coding agent, etc.) are now safety-checked\n too. The AI reads the current screen and the\n conversation to judge whether the keystroke\n is safe given what you asked for, and holds\n anything dangerous or unrequested for your\n approval.\n- AI orchestration: the automatic command\n safety check no longer hard-blocks anything.\n Refusing a command outright could dead-end a\n task you asked for (like deleting a directory\n you had just created). Instead the AI now\n judges each command against what you asked\n for, and a scary-but-legitimate one is held\n for one-tap approval rather than refused.\n Deleting a subdirectory you requested just\n runs; only a real root or home wipe, or a\n suspicious command, stops to ask first.\n- Companion: a paired device that still worked\n no longer nags you to re-pair on every launch\n after installing a new version.\n- Companion: when the relay hits its daily\n data limit, the Mac now shows “Daily relay\n data limit reached” in Companion settings\n and backs off instead of retrying in a\n tight loop until the limit resets.\n- Companion: push notifications to a paired\n iPhone are now coalesced and more accurate.\n A burst of agent activity no longer sends a\n wakeup for each step, and the generic “Your\n agent has an update.” placeholder is gone\n for ordinary replies. A permission request\n that blocks the agent now shows what it\n needs and plays a sound instead of a silent\n placeholder, while the agent’s own automatic\n tool calls stay quiet.\n- The it2 command-line tool now works on hosts\n you reach through SSH integration. Its API\n requests are routed back to your local iTerm2\n over the conductor and run there, gated by a\n per-session permission prompt naming the host.\n it2 is installed automatically wherever shell\n integration loads. Shell > SSH > “Remote host\n can control iTerm2” toggles this permission for\n the current connection and shows a checkmark\n while it is granted.\n- Session-bound AI chats can now read the\n visible screen on request with the same\n get_screen_contents tool the orchestrator\n uses, gated by the “View Contents”\n permission. In a full-screen app it returns\n just the current screen, with no scrollback.\n- AI chats linked to only a web browser now\n use a browser-appropriate system prompt\n instead of one that described a terminal\n they were not attached to.\n- Workgroups: reloading a diff session now\n re-runs the command matching the file\n picker’s current selection. Previously, if\n the file you were viewing stopped having\n changes and the picker snapped back to “All\n Files”, reload would rerun the old per-file\n diff, find nothing, and exit immediately,\n closing the session.\n- The built-in web browser now prompts for a\n user name and password when a site returns\n an HTTP authentication challenge (Basic,\n Digest, or NTLM) instead of failing to load.\n- The keychain permission prompt that appeared\n after upgrading, for AI API keys and for a\n paired companion device, no longer recurs on\n every update. Those secrets now live in an\n entitlement-scoped keychain group that stays\n readable across signed builds. Upgrading to\n this version prompts once more to move them.\n- The Companion Device Settings window now\n shows the paired device’s relay room name\n in hex, so you can match it against the\n phone and the relay logs when reporting a\n problem.\n- Companion pairing moved to the new sharded\n relay resolver. Older iPhone apps can no\n longer pair and are told to update. A\n pairing made before the move must be made\n again.\n- Fixed a bug where flag emoji (such as a\n country flag) rendered as blank cells or\n flickered under the GPU renderer. The two\n regional indicators that make up a flag are\n now shaped together, matching the non-GPU\n renderer.\n- Debug logging now caps memory use at about\n 200MB (discarding the oldest entries when it\n grows past that) and no longer freezes the\n app while saving the log when you turn it\n off.\n- Companion: if you never paired a device,\n iTerm2 no longer touches the companion\n keychain items at launch. This stops a\n spurious keychain permission prompt for\n people who alternate differently-signed\n builds (such as a released build and one\n built from source).\n- A saved status bar layout whose RPC-provided\n text component is missing its registration\n request is now dropped instead of showing up\n as a blank “ghost” cell.\n- Fix a memory corruption bug that could crash\n iTerm2 intermittently, most often when\n closing a tmux window caused surviving\n sessions to resize while output was still\n streaming. Lazily cached line-wrapping info\n could be written into scrollback storage\n shared with another thread. Issue 12941.\n- Reduced redundant preference reads on hot\n paths (margins, dimming, and per-pane\n background image). Idle windows no longer\n read these settings thousands of times per\n second, which quiets excessive CoreFoundation\n logging and saves a little CPU.\n- Fixed modified keys such as Ctrl-J and\n Shift-Enter not reaching some apps (for\n example Codex) running under tmux\n integration (tmux -CC). iTerm2 now hands\n these keys to tmux by name so tmux encodes\n them just as it does outside control mode,\n honoring its extended-keys-format.\n- Fixed a three-finger drag over a mouse-\n reporting app (such as tmux with mouse\n mode on, or vim with mouse=a) producing\n both an iTerm2 selection and the app's own\n selection at once. Three-finger drags are\n now forwarded to the app like one-finger\n drags when mouse reporting is active, so\n the app owns the selection. Hold Option\n during the drag to make a local iTerm2\n selection instead. Issues 12953 and 12950.\n- Added a Copy URL context menu item that\n copies the URL under the cursor, stitching\n wrapped URLs back together so you can paste\n a clean link into a browser other than your\n default. Issue 12944.\n- Fix VoiceOver QuickNav navigation not\n engaging after interacting with the\n terminal, which previously required\n toggling QuickNav off and on.\n- Fix VoiceOver reading stale terminal\n text when the screen was cleared and\n new output arrived while interacting.\n- Fix VoiceOver focus jumping to the top\n of the terminal when new output\n arrived, and make new output reachable\n without re-interacting.\n- Web links handed to iTerm2 by the system\n (for example when it’s your default\n browser, or via “open -a iTerm2 ”)\n now open in the built-in web browser\n instead of a terminal, unless you’ve\n bound the scheme to a specific profile.\n Issue 12431.\n- Fixed the iPhone companion connection\n getting stuck after the relay dropped an\n idle socket. A keepalive ping that never\n got a reply could hang forever, so the Mac\n kept believing it was connected and never\n reconnected. The ping now times out and\n forces a reconnect.\n- AI chats that control or coordinate your\n terminal sessions now use far fewer tokens\n on long conversations. The live snapshot of\n session state sent each turn is no longer\n part of the cached prompt prefix, so the\n conversation history is reused from cache\n instead of being reprocessed every turn.\n- Fixed a bug where a tab reshaped by the\n Python API’s apply_layout could end up with\n no focused pane, so typing went nowhere until\n you clicked a pane. Focus is now restored\n after the layout is applied.\n- AI chat history is now replayed to the model\n from the exact bytes previously sent rather\n than rebuilt each turn, so the prompt cache\n stays valid across turns (including turns\n that ran a tool) and long conversations keep\n getting cheaper and faster.\n- Sending a message in a long AI chat is now\n faster: when the whole history is already\n stored as sent-bytes, it is no longer\n reprocessed to build each request, so the\n per-message work stays small no matter how\n long the conversation gets.\n- Fix “Save contents…” suggesting a\n filename containing colons/slashes in\n the timestamp, which macOS shows as “/”\n and disallows. Issue 12931.\n- The command info popover’s “Started at”\n time now honors the system 24-Hour Time\n setting instead of always using a\n 12-hour clock. Issue 12929.\n- A new advanced setting,\n CompactTabBarStoplightButtonsWidth, lets\n you adjust the width reserved for the\n window buttons before the inline tab bar\n in the Compact and Minimal themes.\n- Fixed a bug where Undo Close (⌘Z) could\n leave behind an invisible empty window when\n the closed session could not be restored.\n Besides being a leak, these phantom windows\n crashed Python API scripts with a\n RecursionError. Empty windows are now closed,\n and the Python API no longer reports a\n selected tab for a window that has none.\n- Fixed a bug where a Python API script\n launched from the Scripts menu of a\n secondary iTerm2 instance (one started with\n -suite) connected to the main instance's API\n server instead of its own, controlling the\n wrong app.\n- Fixed a bug where Make Screenshot did not\n render ASCII ligatures (like -> or =>) even\n when the profile enabled them and the font\n supported them. Screenshots now ligate the\n same way the terminal does.\n- Fixed a bug where tmux panes received stray\n focus-report escape sequences on focus\n changes even when no program in the pane had\n turned on focus reporting. Reports now go\n only to panes whose app requested them (by\n emitting DECSET 1004), matching how non-tmux\n sessions behave.\n- Fixed Semantic History (Cmd-Click) failing\n to open files in JetBrains IDEs such as\n IntelliJ IDEA, WebStorm, and Rider. iTerm2\n now runs the IDE’s command-line launcher\n directly (forwarding to the already-running\n instance) and passes the path before the\n --line option, which the current launcher\n requires. Issue 12958.\n- Fixed a bug where the OSC 9;4 progress\n bar was hidden by the border around the\n active pane. The bar now draws on top of\n the border's top edge. Issue 12960.\n- Fixed a bug where some profile settings\n that apply to all profile types (such as\n “Border around active pane”, “Use custom\n tab color”, and Color Presets) could not\n be found with the preferences search box\n while editing a profile.\n- Added iTerm2 > Suppressed Alerts, which lists\n warnings that are being answered automatically\n because you chose to remember your response\n (for example, declining terminal-initiated\n image display, which silently stops inline\n images from appearing). You can select an\n alert and un-suppress it so it will be shown\n again. The old View > Always Show Alerts with\n Remembered Selections command now lives in\n this panel as a checkbox.\n- Scripts > Manage can install and update\n the uv-based Python runtime when the uv\n advanced setting is enabled.\n- On macOS 27, which removes Rosetta, no\n longer prompt to install Rosetta for\n Python scripts, and rebuild or replace\n Intel-only Python environments for\n Apple Silicon instead of failing.\n- Fix a full-environment Python script run\n from Open Quickly (or before its\n environment finished installing) launching\n on the shared runtime and failing to\n import its own dependencies.\n- Fix the Script Dependency Editor showing\n [Error] for a package’s installed version\n when its dependency has a version\n constraint (e.g. aiohttp>=3.14.3).\n- The Script Dependency Editor can now\n change a uv script’s Python version to any\n version uv provides, rebuilding its\n environment, not just the installed one.\n- A failed Python version change (e.g. to a\n version where a pinned dependency has no\n wheel) no longer destroys a uv script’s\n working environment; the new one is built\n aside and swapped in only if it succeeds.\n- Replacing an installed script during import\n no longer deletes the existing script if you\n cancel the Python download; the original is\n kept and the cancellation is reported.\n- uv runtime updates no longer fail on slow\n connections (the download is no longer capped\n at a fixed total time).\n- Legacy scripts whose setup.cfg has no exact\n Python pin now get the same version-change\n warning as pinned ones when migrating to uv.\n- On macOS without Rosetta, iTerm2 now asks\n before replacing an Intel-only Python runtime\n with the Apple Silicon build, and stays quiet\n if you decline.\n- A script whose replacement is interrupted by\n a crash mid-install is now restored on the\n next launch instead of being lost.\n- The Scripts menu no longer lists files or\n folders whose names begin with a dot.\n- Migrating a script to the new Python runtime\n no longer runs Intel Python interpreters\n found on your PATH, which could trigger a\n macOS “Intel-only binary” warning.\n- When a keyboard shortcut stops matching\n because your input method changed the\n character a key produces, iTerm2 now offers\n to interpret key bindings by physical key.\n- Fixed a bug where decomposed Hangul, such\n as Korean filenames stored by HFS+, was\n drawn as separate jamo letters with the\n wrong width, which corrupted cursor\n movement. Conjoining jamo now compose\n into a single syllable cell.\n- OSC 9;4;4, the ConEmu progress protocol’s\n paused state, now works when the percentage\n is left out, which the protocol allows. The\n bar keeps the percentage it was showing and\n changes color instead of doing nothing. If\n there was none, it shows a small one so the\n paused state is visible.\n- Advanced Paste with bracketed paste on but\n “Wait for shell prompt” off now pastes a\n multi-line block as one editable buffer on\n zsh, bash, and fish, so you can review it and\n press Return once, instead of running each\n line as it arrives.\n- When a paste is paused waiting for a shell\n prompt, the paste indicator now has a keyboard\n button that sends your typing straight to the\n terminal instead of queueing it, so you can\n answer a prompt the paste is stuck on (such as\n a password or zsh’s spelling correction).\n- Exiting a workgroup, or closing one of its\n tabs, no longer leaves the main session’s tab\n showing a blank pane when a non-main peer was\n the visible one.\n- Fixed a workgroup whose tab (or split) hosts\n its own peer group coming up with no toolbar\n and no peer switcher.\n- Workgroup peers nested inside a tab or split\n now inherit their profile and directory from\n the session the workgroup was opened on, so a\n workgroup opened on a remote host keeps every\n peer on that host.\n- Closing a workgroup session that would exit the\n whole workgroup now warns you that its other\n sessions will close too, naming the workgroup\n and how many more sessions will end.\n- Tab groups can be collapsed: click a group's\n chip (or use its menu) to hide its tabs behind\n the chip, which shows a member count. Selecting\n a hidden tab reveals the group. Collapsed state\n persists across restarts.\n- Collapsing and expanding a tab group now\n animates: the member tabs slide shut and open.\n Works on horizontal and vertical tab bars in\n every theme.\n- Dropping a file on a local terminal no longer\n offers to upload it when the machine answers to\n more than one name (for example .local versus a\n DHCP-assigned name). Localhost is now recognized\n by any of the machine's names.\n- Fixed a bug on macOS 26 (Tahoe) where the tab\n bar's overflow menu left out the first hidden\n tab, so clicking the chevron with a single\n overflowed tab opened an empty menu.\n- Refreshed the built-in AI model list: added\n Claude Sonnet 5, GPT-5.6 (Sol/Terra/Luna),\n and Gemini 3.7/3.6 Flash and 3.5/3.1\n Flash-Lite. Removed retired Claude Opus\n 4.1 and older Gemini models.\n- AI Settings can now automatically update the\n built-in model list from iterm2.com (off by\n default), with an Update Now button to check\n on demand.\n- In orchestration chats, a session reference\n the AI writes without a leading @ now still\n renders as a clickable session link.\n- Fixed a bug where the Keys > General tab in\n the Edit Session window could appear empty\n for a terminal session after editing a\n browser session, because previously hidden\n controls were not restored.\n- Fixed the color of the locale description in\n Settings > Profiles > Terminal so it adapts\n correctly when switching between light and\n dark mode.\n- Fixed a white rectangle that appeared to the\n right of a collapsed tab group in the Minimal\n theme when the group was the rightmost item\n in the tab bar.\n- The Shell > Workgroups menu now shows a\n checkmark next to the workgroup the current\n session belongs to.\n- Fixed a crash that could occur when doing a\n global search that included a browser session.\n- Fixed a bug where the top-right session\n indicators left a horizontal smear while\n swiping between tabs with two fingers.\n- Fixed a bug where an SGR sequence or SGR\n trigger with more parameters than fit\n attached the extra parameters’\n subparameters to the last parameter that\n did fit, changing its meaning: underline\n could turn into dashed underline. SGR\n triggers also silently dropped their 16th\n parameter.\n- Fixed a bug where toolbelt lists\n (Command History, Recent Directories,\n Captured Output) could stay blank until\n the window was resized when a new window\n was opened with the toolbelt showing.\n- Fixed a bug where modified function keys (such\n as Shift-F5) lost their modifier in a tmux\n integration session, sending the unmodified\n sequence instead of the xterm form like\n ^[[15;2~.\n- Fixed a bug where a sixel image whose\n terminator had a doubled escape (ESC ESC \\\n instead of ESC \\) was discarded entirely,\n leaving a stray backslash on the screen. The\n image now renders.\n- Fixed a bug where a left- or right-of-screen\n window could suddenly change width (growing or\n shrinking to a second tab’s profile size) after\n a display-configuration change, instead of\n keeping its own width.\n- Fixed AI chat failing on OpenAI models with an\n error about an invalid tool schema. Also fixed\n a crash while parsing AI responses that\n contained a null value inside an array.\n- Fixed a bug where, on OpenAI models, the second\n and later messages in an orchestration or\n terminal-linked AI chat were ignored: the model\n received only the terminal/workgroups context\n and replied as if you had said nothing.\n- Fix Cmd+G skipping matches sometimes.\n- Fixed right-to-left text scrambling when a\n full-screen program scrolled part of the\n screen: the rows that moved were left drawn\n in left-to-right order. Credit to Nimanou\n (nuved).\n- Fixed the second and later rows of a wrapped\n right-to-left line losing their display order\n when drawn from scrollback, so the line read\n scrambled. Credit to Nimanou (nuved).\n\nYou can use the following to verify the zip file on https://keybase.io/verify:\n\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----\nHash: SHA256\n\n14b5131e9134d0012466574fba6d69fb9ef84eee66660ee861e2da483089574a\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\niQJPBAEBCAA5FiEEoSV4Xzv7YY5VD+aYJiz+XnkavhsFAmqgczEbFIAAAAAABAAO\nbWFu