  "text": " \n \n\n [2611.06910] FrontierPhysics: Evaluating Agents for End-to-End Physics Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search \n \n \n \n \n \n Submit \n Donate \n \n Log in \n \n \n \n \n Search arXiv \n \n \n \n \n \n Press Enter to search &middot; Advanced search \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n--> \n\n \n \n Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence \n \n\n \n arXiv:2611.06910 (cs)\n \n \n \n \n \n [Submitted on 4 Nov 2026] \n Title: FrontierPhysics: Evaluating Agents for End-to-End Physics Research \n Authors: Bingran You , Wenjun Ke , Ria Rosenauer , Bo Liu , Wenbo Chen , Min Chen , Shuqi Xu , Yue Ma , Jiechao Feng , Bo Chen , Xiangkai Sun , Jiarui Liu , Jinen Guo , Zeyu Zhou , Xu Huang , Zhuohan Li , Haoyu Li , Heguang Lin , Lijie Ding , Yadong Zeng , Kangqi Zhang , Xinge Liu , Hanwen Liu , Zhenghong Li , Alexander von Recum , Yuchen Zhu , Felix Schwarzfischer , Yizhou Zhao , Jicheng Wang , Binxu Li , Qian Yu , Chan Li , Cheng Dai , Di Wang , Haoyu Zhang , Jiankai Sun , Kaituo Feng , Mian Qin , Ruihong Yin , Yiqun Luo , Weiwei Wu , Shihong Pan , Tsung Yeh Hsieh , Xiao Huang , Yan Zhang , Sihan Li , Zirui Ren , Zengji Tu , Kyoung Whan Choe , Ke Sun , Vincenzo Galia , Vaisakhi Mishra , Jiashu He , Hongyang Wang , Zhiheng Yang , Shubham Gaur , Yifan Gu , Shuyi Wang , Zihao Zuo , Steven Dillmann , Yiyou Sun , Xinyang Han , Hanchung Lee , Molei Tao , Yue Zhao , Shilong Liu , Yuchen Wu , Xuandong Zhao , Xiangyi Li , Florian Brand , Gerbrand Ceder , Hartmut Haeffner View a PDF of the paper titled FrontierPhysics: Evaluating Agents for End-to-End Physics Research, by Bingran You and 71 other authors \n View PDF \n HTML (experimental) \n\n\n\n \n Abstract: AI agents are playing an increasingly important role in physics research. However, existing physics benchmarks only evaluate fixed questions, isolated sub-tasks, or paper reproduction with given solutions, and rarely check research quality alongside correctness. We introduce FrontierPhysics, an end-to-end benchmark with 56 tasks made by 42 physicists based on their own research, covering nine domains across theoretical, computational, and experimental physics. Each task requires agents to review the literature, develop a research plan, conduct research and experiments, and produce structured outputs and scientific manuscripts. Combining deterministic verifiers with expert-authored rubrics, FrontierPhysics evaluates whether agents' research is correct and publishable. Across 22 models in 26 configurations, no configuration passes more than 46 of its 168 rollouts. Manual review of agent trajectories shows that frontier agents' main weaknesses are insufficiently rigorous reasoning and limited expert intuition, rather than gaps in physics knowledge or execution errors.\n \n\n \n \n \n Comments: \n 41 pages (10 pages main text), 19 figures, 11 tables \n \n \n Subjects: \n \n Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) \n \n Cite as: \n arXiv:2611.06910 [cs.AI] \n \n \n \n (or \n arXiv:2611.06910v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\n \n \n \n \n https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.06910 \n \n \n Focus to learn more \n \n \n \n arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Submission history From: Bingran You [ view email ] [v1] \n Wed, 4 Nov 2026 18:56:22 UTC (2,848 KB) \n \n \n \n \n \n Full-text links: \n Access Paper: \n \n \nView a PDF of the paper titled FrontierPhysics: Evaluating Agents for End-to-End Physics Research, by Bingran You and 71 other authors View PDF HTML (experimental) TeX Source\n \n \n \n view license \n \n \n \n Additional Features \n \n Audio Summary \n \n \n \n Current browse context: \n cs.AI \n\n \n \n < prev \n \n | \n next > \n \n \n new \n | \n recent \n | 2026-11 \n \n Change to browse by:\n \n cs \n \n \n\n \n \n References & Citations \n \n SciX Google Scholar \n Semantic Scholar \n \n \n \n\n