 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50366999": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:47:33.421Z",
  "bytes": 49282,
  "text": " Kimi K3.1 Pro model weights are out | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login Kimi K3.1 Pro model weights are out ( huggingface.co ) 560 points by abrarthought 21 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 160 comments help \n \n __jf__ 20 hours ago | next [–] \n Subtle quote from their website: > Advancing the Frontier of Open Intelligence. Too bad you probably need something like an 8x B300 node to run this. Black Friday is coming soon though, but I fear this open intelligence will be pacing anyone's liquidity frontier so I'll probably keep trailing the frontier for a while with nvidia/qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4 on dual RTX PRO 4000 in TP=2. I can almost fit 450k context in there. reply bityard 19 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Models this size aren't targeting hobbyists running models in their bedrooms on gaming/surplus hardware bought with disposable income. This model is for researchers who already have racks of equipment, inference providers who sell access to models, and for businesses who must (or choose to) use on-prem models. All of which have to pay to use the model if their revenue rises beyond certain thresholds: https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3.1-Pro/blob/main/LI... reply SR2Z 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Considering the likelihood of Moonshot being able to collect a judgement for violating these terms in an American court is basically zero, and the relative ease of finding the weights without the licensing agreement, \"have to pay\" seems very generous. reply joshuaissac 11 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > likelihood of Moonshot being able to collect a judgement for violating these terms in an American court is basically zero Chinese companies sue American entities in American courts all the time. For example, Alibaba won a judgement against the American government last week.[1] 1. https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/alibaba-wi... reply KptMarchewa 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Considering the likelihood of Moonshot being able to collect a judgement for violating these terms in an American court is basically zero Why? Americans are always yapping how China growth is basically fueled by IP theft, are you saying that it works both ways? reply 15155 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Overusing a piece of software in violation of a TOS/license agreement is radically different than employing human agents to conduct on-the-ground corporate espionage. reply boomskats 46 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Which only _they_ do. And _we_ don't. After all, _their_ AI is just trained on stolen IP, right? Unlike _ours_? I still can't get over how irresponsible it is of _them_ to release these weights for free. That means _anyone_ could gain access to their AI model now. At least _we_ make sure access to _ours_ is fully controlled by _us_. reply dormento 2 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n People laugh, but it is impressive how often this has to be said, and how often it is ignored. I'm still waiting for the media juggernauts to start beasting on the american hypescalers due to all the theft going around. I know for a fact that both Anthropic and OpenAI had access to some private github repos of mine in the past (due to clear artifacting in their training set). The thing is, they make so much money (allegedly), so they cannot be wrong (unlike the chinese). And this does not take into account all the hacking and system intrusion facilitated (and often perpetrated) by their systems! largbae 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Most enterprises capable of running that much gear won't take that risk even if it is unenforceable. Oracle has made a great living threatening audits of companies who download its updates. reply SR2Z 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah, but only Western companies who might have to defend themselves in court. I have no doubt that pirated copies of Oracl