ould have seen kimi 2.8 preview alongside kimi 2.7 reply gessha 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Maybe they're talking about parameter count? reply LugosFergus 12 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n How bad did the electric bill get hit? reply FuriouslyAdrift 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We have a data center already and we're in heavy industrial manufacturing. It didn't even register in our usage. reply edg5000 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Very cool reply joecot 19 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The benefit is that you can use these models on a variety of different providers. You're not stuck with a single proprietary provider. Aggregators like OpenCode Zen and OpenCode Go can shop around providers on these. OpenRouter shows you the costs of a bunch of the providers, and you can route to the cheapest ones. I regularly use together.ai with models like this, where they're fully self hosting them and I can turn off all data retention, at a fraction of the cost of proprietary models. I recently discovered abliterated models (models edited to not refuse to work on security stuff) and hosts for them like refuseless.com, and that's an option for open source models, not for proprietary ones. reply penagwin 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Aggregators like OpenCode Zen and OpenCode Go can shop around providers on these. OpenRouter shows you the costs of a bunch of the providers, and you can route to the cheapest ones. Unfortunately due to their licensing requirements for modal-as-a-service providers, the pricing can’t go lower than Moonshot’s from what I can tell. This is still great if like your example you trust together.ai more, or if different providers have better speeds/cache rates (though usually the original lab has the best cache hit rate). And obviously if you’re a large enough service to afford to self host or finetune it then this is good. reply athrowaway3z 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I didn't dig into Kimi's MaaS legalese, but i dont really understand how that knee-high fence is supposed to work long term. All the service provider needs to do is provide the option to rent out VM's with access to the inference cards. Have the customer provide the model. Maybe provide a bit of a speedup, by letting the customer mount the model by hash. Sure, that all sounded way too technical 10 years ago, and somebody would have said that's already too much annoyance to deal with for average customers. Reluctantly i'd agree. That was 10 years ago. Nowadays, with some proper docs, this entire set up can be up and running in 5 minutes. reply knollimar 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Aren’t these only financially viable by sharing weights in some big setup? reply joecot 18 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Maybe for the API rate, but for OpenCode Go they're regularly making deals to get users a lot more than their money's worth on open source models. reply pigeons 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Sometimes it can be so hard to tell and track because of the cache differences. reply nbk_2000 12 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n There's no need to use ablated models right now for cyber work. Several open weight models don't refuse cyber requests (DeepSeek, GLM, Kimi K3, etc). And as you said, OpenRouter has many 3rd party ZDR inference providers and several of those serve these models. reply zozbot234 12 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Ablated models are not any more inherently knowledgeable about \"forbidden\" topics, either. Since such topics are not generally trained for, you just get weird creative hallucinations. reply stev1983 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n The problem with looking at different prices from different model providers is a lot of the cost is generally driven by cache hit rates. \nThat’s where you can accumulate expense and the cache hit rate can very wildly from one provider to the next. reply zozbot234 19 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n You could easily run it with SSD streaming on as little as 192GB RAM (The dense portion is around 115GB or so at native precision, and the remainder would fit KV and a cache for the most commonly referenced experts.) This fits Gorgon Halo, which is a reasonably priced platform. It would be slow, but still usable for high-value tasks. reply thway15269037 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Excuse me, could you elaborate how you came up with 192GB of RAM? I almost thought they downsized model, but it's still 2.8T tokens. Are we talking about not-yet-out 1-bit quant or assumption that something like ds4 (daskstar, not deepseek) will heavily optimize it? Not a trolling question, genuine interest. reply labcomputer 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n All these very large models are sparse mixtures of experts (SMoE), where typically only 2-3% of weights are active for a given forward pass. Usually there are some static experts in each attention layer, plus a whole bunch of dynamically-routed experts. Typically some form of LRU cache is used for the dynamic experts. So you can keep only the static expert(s) and routing layers in memory and then stream the dynamic experts from SSD, at a modest inference speed cost driven by the latency required to load layers from SSD when they aren’t already in the memory cache. The LRU cache works reasonably well because a given task will often hit the same experts on each iteration of the forward pass. For example, on a Mac Studio, Qwen3.8-flash-next (180b total parameters) with 50% of experts in memory and n-gram offloading has about 1/4 to 1/3 the inference speed of everything in memory. That requires 32GB of vRAM for the weights (plus a little extra for context, obviously) reply zozbot234 18 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's not based on loading the full 1.5TB model into VRAM or system RAM, it's just streaming parameters on demand from SSD - which is worthwhile since the MoE portion of the model is quite sparsely accessed. This has been demoed on a 128GB M5 MacBook Pro with lousy performance (0.3 tok/s or so) because it had to load at least ~25GB from SSD per token (comprising the entirety of the sparsely routed active params) and potentially even reload some of the dense weights, depending on what exactly managed to fit on that platform. With 192GB you'd avoid any reloading for the dense weights and even cache a few layer-experts. reply thway15269037 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, I understand the principle, but will it be usable? E.g. in my personal opinion border of usefulness starts somewhere at 7-10 tok/s. I currently have 192GB and 24GB VRAM, and I can run Qwen-Flash-Next (4 bit) and GLM5.3-Flash (4 bit) at 25 tok/s and 7 tok/s respectfully (with quantized cache on beellama). Parallel queries worsen result significantly but can push aggregate to around 50 tok/s. I can't, however, run DS4.1-1017, for example, but maybe I didn't find proper options and quantization yet. So I'm really interested how to be sure what can/cannot fit in specific hardware (for example, I want to buy a Mac Studio with 64 or 128 GB RAM, what MoE model it would run at comfortable speed? Will it be qwen-flash-next or something bigger? And so on).\nSo far websites with \"benchmarks\" were really terrible in that regard (and huggingface calculator even worse!) reply zozbot234 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > in my personal opinion border of usefulness starts somewhere at 7-10 tok/s. This is expected if you're actively steering the agent, but what about running unattended tasks - perhaps multiple such tasks in parallel, such that you can stay engaged by \"attending\" to each of them in turn? Then you're not restricted to such a requirement, and can benefit from much slower (individually, not wrt. total throughput) inference streams. > I currently have 192GB and 24GB VRAM I'm not familiar with beellama, but this seems like it could be the perfect setup for Strata which people are saying good things about, including for running DS4.1F (at least quantized). The whole point of it is to effectively leverage heterogenous RAM/VRAM setups. Of course llama.cpp itself is doing a good job of trying to catch up, but they've yet to support efficient SSD streaming (mmap doesn't quite cut it) in the main branch. reply stev1983 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I run this exact setup with 24GB vram and 288gb ran. I get 25 to 28tps. The catches you have to run the 2.25-bit version currently. Strata is not optimized to run the 3 ir 4-but versions, at least not yet.\nWith a 256K context window and vision I think it uses around 135 MB— I can’t remember if that’s total or just ram.\nEdit: 4.1 flash next reply zozbot234 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Strata is not optimized to run the 3 [o]r 4-b[i]t versions, at least not yet. How so? This is not obvious to me; AIUI Strata does support streaming weights for larger models via SSD. Of course the speed would drop quite a bit once you rely on SSD, but it ought to be workable. Since you're on a HEDT/server platform, you can also benefit by adding more SSD hardware to be accessed in parallel via the abundant PCIe lanes. reply labcomputer 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > I want to buy a Mac Studio with 64 or 128 GB RAM, what MoE model it would run at comfortable speed? Will it be qwen-flash-next or something bigger? This depends on how you plan to use the machine... but Qwen-flash-next is right at the top of what a 128GB Apple Silicon can do. QFN with 4bit quantization will run \"comfortably\" with all weights in memory and a 128k context on a 128GB machine. On M5 Max w/ 128GB, that will give about 75 tok/sec (using oMLX with MTP enabled). It spits out text much faster than I can read. However, this truly uses all the RAM. You'll have like 8-12GB left for the OS and apps, which isn't really enough to actually use the machine as a workstation while the model is loaded. But it would work fine as an inference server with Opencode running on your laptop. (Hence, \"how you plan to use it?\") Alternatively, you can offload QFN's n-gram table to SSD (this is just a settings option in oMLX), which drops generation speed on the same machine to ~60-65 tok/sec (assuming the model lives on the internal SSD) and reduces memory usage to ~90GB with even a large context. That leaves plenty of RAM to use the Studio as your main workstation. Also, you may run much longer contexts because of the extra memory headroom. Second alternatively, you can use oMLX's expert offloading feature to keep 50% of experts in memory (with n-gram offloading also). This reduces the RAM footprint to a little over 32GB + context. Offloading the experts drops generation speed to around 20-25 tok/sec on the same machine as above, but it would allow you to comfortably run on a 64GB machine, while still using it as a workstation. I have tested the 50% expert offload case on an M1 Max / 64GB, and the inference speed is only a little slower (more like 18-20 tok/sec) than M5 Max / 128GB because in both cases the bottleneck is SSD read bandwidth and the GPU is spending a lot of time idle. One last thing: GLM 5.3-flash doesn't use an n-gram table, so you can't use that trick to reduce memory footprint. You'd need to use expert offloading to make it fit a 128GB machine. Since it has over twice as many active parameters as QFN (which doubles the required SSD read bandwidth), my guess is that a 128GB Studio would not be much faster than your current setup for that model. reply reasonableklout 16 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Wow, TIL. Is there any calculator or rule of thumb you use to figure out the expected performance of a model on some DRAM/SSD split? For instance I am wondering what the performance could be like on a 512GB M5 Mac Studio. reply zozbot234 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The rule of thumb is to just test it on your chosen platform, with your workload. SSD performance matters a lot obviously, you'll want to run PCIe 5.0 if at all feasible, or a recent M5 Pro/Max/Ultra or M6 Mac with high storage capacity (lower tiers have much worse performance). You can figure out a worst case easily (assuming that the fraction of sparse params you manage to cache in VRAM or system RAM has a fair chance of being picked among the total) but in practice you do quite a bit better than that - which is why even very small caches can be potentially worthwhile. reply knollimar 34 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That sounds like not a rule of thumb tbh reply adgjlsfhk1 18 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n it's a mixture of experts so it's somewhat viable to stream weights from nvme reply LarsDu88 19 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Possible data center bans incoming + tons of GPUs sitting in warehouses due to lack of power infrastructure to power them. Perhaps a flood of 8x B300 will be homelab ready in a few months reply NitpickLawyer 19 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > a flood of 8x B300 will be homelab ready in a few months Haha, that's beyond wishful thinking, I'm afraid. We're not even seeing a flood of A100s (6 yo and several generations old). Every GPU is still being squeezed right now, and the trend will probably continue for the foreseeable future. reply throwup238 19 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Last I checked AWS still sells EC2 instances using 2018 era GPUs. Most people underestimate just how much long tail demand there is and how long these cards last, despite the higher power consumption. reply fauigerzigerk 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, but if data center space gets tight, it creates an incentive to replace old inefficient models with better ones, i.e. sqeueeze more performance out of limited data center space. reply thway15269037 11 hours ago |