a for open-weight models for a long long time, and I suspect K3.1 will be there for a long time too. It's pretty hard to fake that one since it's actual human preferences for the artifacts produced. reply seizethecheese 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Speaking of arena comparisons of K3.1 and DS4.1-Flash, we're spinning one up that's more relevant than design, I think: https://agentduel.com/leaderboard Here, DS4.1-Flash and K3.1 are basically tied, with K3.1 being slower and more expensive. Big error bars, though. Even with error bars, DS4.1-flash is clearly an impressive model. reply reissbaker 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If your benchmark puts DS4.1-Flash at the same tier as K3 or K3.1, I'm sorry to say your benchmark is broken. It really isn't, as much as I wish it was. reply seizethecheese 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We allow early voting. This is against what most arenas do, but is on purpose. If the model is faster to do simple stuff, it should be chosen. In this sense, DS4.1-Flash is better on many tasks. You can remove early voting from leaderboard and they are no longer the same. reply purerandomness 12 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n At the point where I choose an expensive model like Kimi 3.1, speed doesn't matter, at all. Usually, it's because all else failed, and I have to resort to burning Tokens with more expensive/better models, so a few minutes here and there really should not matter. reply seizethecheese 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think you’re alone to think time for task completion doesn’t matter reply rocho 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n But his point is that the faster models could _not_ complete the task satisfactorily. I've seen this myself too: DeepSeek 4.1 Flash is really good and incredibly cheap at constrained implementations, searches, etc. However, frequently the implementations don't take into account all possible cases they should cover. Then I'm either forced to step in and steer it very precisely, or have a heavier model review and provide detailed feedback. I've had good success with this mixed workflow using GPT 6.1 Sol as reviewer. Then my work focuses mostly at the beginning (architecture, goals, constraints), and at the end (final review or very quick intermediate reviews). I'm not aware of benchmarks that simulate this kind of mixed workflow with different models. reply nostrebored 13 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n But how do you verify a fair or representative distribution of complexity? reply seizethecheese 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We validate people are doing real work using LLM judges. reply haizhung 5 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I tried both of them for agentic coding and didn’t feel like K3 was adding so much benefit to justify its almost 10x price (on the modem provider I’m on). In fact, I barely see a difference between the results at all. The models being tied smells correct to me. I didn’t try 3.1 yet, though. reply eunos 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's quite hilarious that kimi K3.1 was competitive against Opus 5.5 and a bit lagging against DS V4.1F. While DS struggled against Opus reply potamic 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n And how do you reckon GLM and Qwen stack up? reply carterschonwald 17 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n every time i accidentally use flash versions of deep seek i get really frustrated with the model till i realize that it was the wrong model. im also a little confused because it seems like at least comparisons make it sound like ds is no longer focusing on their high reasoning model family … reply CamperBob2 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n 1017 is nothing like previous DeepSeek Flash models. It is vastly superior in all respects. reply verdverm 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm still unimpressed with the latest DS4 flash, it makes more mistakes or misses