O_H_E 13 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Wow, really interesting work. Run of the mil benchmarks has been so shallow in measuring real performance, especially outside agentic software development. Aside from this paper and K3.1 specifically, is there somewhere where I can read more a high-level introduction about the state of things in this niche? like the different types of faults like these models fail and ideally how to fish them out? reply conception 19 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Depends on what you're using it for. DSf is pretty good at executing planned coding tasks but it isn't very intelligent. K3.1 doesn't need to be a coding master if it can be a fable replacement. reply Scene_Cast2 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I wanted to switch to DS4.1F from K3 (not even K3.1). Even the 1017 snapshot takes 5x-10x the tokens that K3 uses for the task, and often misses more intricate gotchas that K3 catches. reply zozbot234 19 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n For reference, Hy4 Preview was a 770B model (49B active) + 10B MTP (0.7B active). Not nearly the same potential as Kimi K3 (which is 2.8T) wrt. real-world knowledge - which matters at the new frontier of advanced research and scholarly tasks. Coding benchmarks are pretty much universally saturated by now which is why newer intelligence indexes have chosen to weigh tasks quite differently. reply segmondy 19 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm not talking about Hy4 Preview. I'm talking about Hy4, it was announced this morning. - https://hy.tencent.ai/research/hy4 reply zozbot234 19 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Is there any evidence that Hy4 proper is a new architecture compared to Hy4 Preview? Mind you, the Preview page explicitly says \"we're not even done with pretraining, let alone model fine-tuning\". reply segmondy 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Oh ok, I see what you mean, you are just comparing them by weight. In that case, it tracks that K3.1 will have more knowledge. It's the same architecture. I'm just saying that for how large K3.1 is, intelligence by weight, it's not crushing DSV4.1 or Hy4 reply 7777777phil 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I wonder how much the harness hurts/ helps here. Been using both the Oct V4.1-Flash and K3.1 in Pi for some time and now almost exclusively use V4.1-Flash in dsh and it feels superior. reply minimaxir 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Third-party inference providers are now hosting it, with more popping up by the hour: https://openrouter.ai/moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro Together appears to have double throughput of the Moonshot AI endpoint at the same price point (with Modal having double that throughput), so I suspect there is room for inference providers to optimize...and therefore headroom to cut costs for competition. reply dmckinno 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Lots of chatter about how this model is benchmaxxed, but didn’t observe this in our testing. Very strong open model release that is a bit off the frontier but totally amazing for most use-cases. https://x.com/danielmckinn0n/status/2115213822957117535?a=fO... reply JacobAsmuth 15 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Hypothesis for why K3.1 dropped so much in relative position to other models when AA switched to private evals? Primarily FrontierCode, CritPt V2, and the new private version of AA Omniscience where the model massively underperforms relative to its performance on public benchmarks? reply NitpickLawyer 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think chatter is putting it mildly. There's plenty of evidence in this work [1] that is hard to explain. At the very least it's a failed attempt at benchmaxxing the model, but more likely is straight up training on the test set (and the attempt at hiding it failed). Something leaked pretty heavily in their setup. And there's indications of it happily replying with fake encrypted data in oAI format here [2]. While this is a bit less conclusive than [1], it's still dubious. [1] - https://r40.org/papers/kimi_k31_benchmark_audit.pdf [2] - https://x.com/PeterNutter1/status/2118084568419045704 reply DavCreator 54 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n https://xnotme.com/PeterNutter1/status/2118084568419045704 reply throwa356262 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n These releases are meant for companies, not average Joe: https://www.theregister.com/ai-and-ml/2026/11/03/british-fin... reply codethief 18 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Average Joe likely can't host the model to begin with but the average company won't be able to do so either, at least not for others[0]: > If the Licensee or any of its affiliates operates a Model as a Service business,\nand the aggregate revenue of the Licensee and its affiliates exceeds 20 million\nUS dollars (or the equivalent in other currencies) in total over any consecutive\n12 months, the Licensee must enter into a separate agreement with Moonshot AI\nbefore using the Software or its derivative works for any commercial purpose. [0]: https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3.1-Pro/blob/main/LI... reply 7734128 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It's not impossible that the separate agreement in question imposes light limitations. I think one of the initial reasons why otherwise open source models started with the limitations on model-as-a-service use cases was because of quantised or entirely different models being served as the model in question on open router, and they needed a legal method to limit that. It might just be that, thought it might also be the direct opposite, hard to know. reply ls612 18 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n This doesn’t stop any company spinning it up for their internal use. It only bites if you try to resell it. reply zozbot234 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n ¿Por qué no los dos? reply speedping 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Even if you can’t run this at home, this is still a step toward commoditization 