ut a given set of weights. reply mike_hearn 13 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > It would be very, very, very nice to provide a set of model weights and a proof that they had only been trained on a dataset. To be clear, this is a really hard problem, but it would change fundamentally what levels of trust we could have about a given set of weights. Is it hard? Seems like it isn't that hard if you use NVIDIA's remote attestation/enclave support instead of trying to do everything with ZKPs which have always been something of a dead end. CC overhead 2%, ZKP 100x+ and gigabyte sized proofs - just for inference. It's never been competitive. I'm very skeptical anyone would care about this for training though. I mean, if there's someone out there who genuinely really really wants proofs of the dataset used to train model weights, let's talk? But it seems the value is mostly for open weights inference providers. They can prove what weights they're serving and they can prove they can't see people's prompts. It would take a lot of the risk out of using a no-name LLM cloud service. Unfortunately, after many years of this stuff I have become old and cynical. The stated/revealed preference mismatch is huge. The majority of people say they want privacy in theory but in practice won't trade anything off to get it, and especially not convenience. reply vessenes 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I agree ZKP is the wrong approach. I also agree most people don’t care. I’d love to read a plan that lets someone trust a pile of weights that comes with some sort of provenance proof. I’ll happily call it and you brilliant even if it was really easy to write and come up with. All my ideas for it look really difficult. reply mike_hearn 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Damnit Peter, now you nerd sniped me. What ideas were you looking at? Here's the architecture I'd use. I'm going off of NVIDIA's current recommended stack / what seems most mature, if I understand correctly after a quick chat with GPT. Basically I'd start with a bunch of Xeon 6s (Granite Rapids) connected to Blackwells. You could do it with AMD too but I trust their stack less. That gives you TDX as a starting point, which is pretty mature by now. I'd prepare a very minimal Linux image in which the OS boots straight into running a training server. You need the usual stuff like dm-verity or IMA to ensure the disk image is covered by the kernel image. The image also contains NVIDIA's driver and attestation SDK. Boot it all inside a TDX VM with GPU passthrough. I believe that currently uses encrypted bounce buffers so there'd be some perf loss, but supposedly there's some PCIe extension that can be used to eliminate that (I haven't investigated). The trick is to combine NVIDIA's RA with Intel's. The best way to do that is just hash the evidence from the GPU attestation process into an intermediate data structure which also contains the usual stuff (e.g. ECC pubkey, nonce), that itself is hashed into the user slot of the TDX RA and then bundled all up together. After doing this you have a single über-data structure which can be used to prove that the given public key was generated inside a genuine Xeon TDX VM connected to a genuine NVIDIA GPU running in CC mode. I'd probably just use a zip file to collect all these bits of data together, for convenience when implementing the verifier. It might be worth including an RFC 3161 timestamp over all that as well. The RA protocols for something like this are always very tricky: I'd need to do some careful design work before committing to anything so don't over-read this comment. Now the TDX RA covers the training server (indirectly, via the dm-verity root via the kernel args+hash). What is this server? It's training code (e.g. PyTorch) combined with a server that accepts document batches and - for more advanced training - maybe stuff like AllReduce traffic. Each time a document batch is accepted each doc is hashed into a sorted Merkle tree. The weight updates are then computed on the GPU and the results are hashed into a second accumulator. Each node creates a blockchain-like log in which each record is signed by the static key embedded into the TDX RA. The records point at the hash of the previous records, and contain the current value of the Merkle root and weight accumulator. A verifier can rebuild the roots by iterating over the training set themselves in the right order and just hashing the text. I won't go into the details as you know how all this stuff works already. I'm handwaving a lot of details around parallelism here. For a large scale training run with many nodes you'd need to solve a bunch of details like making the training servers verify each other's remote attestations before they join the cluster, etc. During the AllReduce step nodes would need to verify the data comes from a authenticated TDX VMs before accepting the gradients. Anyway from time to time the weight updates have to be applied and the log also records the hash of the full sets of weights at that checkpoint. The final model consumer can be given the weights, along with the signed logs generated during the training run. They verify the chained RAs, verify the signatures on the logs, and if they wish, verify [a subset of] the hashes of the documents in the open training set. Because the document Merkle tree of each batch is sorted, you can build both inclusion and exclusion proofs. Verifying an exclusion proof is more expensive as you'd have to check each batch versus a quick Merkle branch check for inclusion, but exclusion checks can be done in parallel. Normally in ML documents are shuffled rather than sorted, but the training server can sort the set for building the log entry Merkle tree, and then submit to the GPU in a shuffled order. The bigger issue for exclusion proofs is that SHA256 is too rigid. You might need some sort of simhashing scheme to run along side it. Dunno, needs more tightly specified requirements. reply lrvick 13 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > The majority of people Most do not even realize they have a choice and are not educated enough to make the one in their own best interest. Fast food wins as a staple for many people over a healthy home cooked meal. Creating healthy and safe choices and making them the default is some of the most important work there is. reply notrealyme123 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It would be fantastic to compare benchmark values. The current trust-me-bro economics leave little other choice than interpreting those numbers as PR. reply cmrx64 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I have been addressing this problem head on aggressively; https://github.com/emberian/whatember is the toplevel index. Things like arithmetizing parser/other automata to write semantic predicates (incl graph rewrites!) over training sets, inference requests, etc. reply vessenes 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Thank you! I will review. Super interesting. reply gopalv 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > In both cases the question is the same: how can a company be sure of which weights are actually running on its servers? > the resulting commitment is good for about 16 million requests, after which the provider has to commit again. > The client downloads the commitment, ~6.5 MB in all, and then one proof per request. Checking a proof takes 17 seconds on 24 threads (and 21 seconds on a single thread), including the offline phase's check I know they mentioned $ETH in the technical report, but this is exactly the mechanic for a crypto currency Proof-of-Work which is expensive by design. When they announced it, I was hoping for a 5% overhead validation that the model that served you is really Fable and not Opus. The quantization bit caught me out, because we saw the boundary crossing from good-context-size to bad is so sharp it feels like longer context requests are sent to quantized models to make more room