ploying themselves ... how can a company be sure of which weights are actually running on its servers? I find this a bit odd and backwards. Why do we choose not to make the deployment code to be robust, but instead to observe the model inference as proven? This moves unobservability layer to the system that collects the proofs. Given the assumption that code is fluid and LLMs are rewriting it often, how can we know it isn't being abused against the observer? Goes without saying, it's also disappointing that this has nothing to do with making the model inference observable for consumers. reply Loquebantur 18 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Anthropic here is solving one of their own problems, how to make sure you don't combine other company's models with their stuff for example, and make it sound as if they did it for you. It makes the inference more expensive and helps controlling the users. reply fritzo 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think some model providers are implementing \"eyes glaze over\" where if you ask a boring simple question they route it to a cheap model but charge you for an expensive model, so consumers may want a proof of work certificate to know they're getting what they pay for. reply xpct 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think a consumer facing system would be fantastic, but from how they're framing it in the article, it's all about internal company monitoring, with some fearmongering around LLM observability. reply seurimas 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I assume this alludes to their prior work with models running on smart TVs being able to setup inference on additional smart TVs. The idea being that you can't be sure that, having gone through the whole deployment process, your hardware has not been subverted to run a different model. Any level of \"robust\" code you can imagine to prevent this in the traditional ways could be subverted on the fly by a capable model. > This moves unobservability layer to the system that collects the proofs. I'd say it's adding security through a secondary system, rather than displacing security to a secondary system. Security by layers. reply EliKarasik 15 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We're working on provable inference at Attestable ( https://attestable.com/ ) - our solution is one of the comparisons provided. We've published initial performance metrics in August of 87 t/s on Muse Glimmer 30B (a dense model) - orders of magnitude faster than existing OS projects. Antropic's FlashProve supports only a fully integer model with a small context length (significantly hurting evals). There is significant progress since then, and we'll publish more results soon. reply stevemk14ebr 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n This obviously an attempt at being able to say a response came from an anthropic model vs say some other model via a model router. As for me, I will keep using my model router, I don't care to be charged more for a name brand. I don't care about the brand at all, it's a commodity. reply dnnehgf 18 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n until your company gets sued for using Dangerous and Shoddy Foreign models and the boss says you can only use the Trustworthy and Premium Aligned labs' models. reply thereader12 15 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It feels kinda yucky reading through a document that spends half the time saying how this great for our regulatory capture efforts and how they won't have to show anyone the wieghts. It's not even a bad thing, you want to know you are using what you think you are, but they are advertising the side effects as features. reply willy_k 15 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n In modern sv-wallstreet corporateness you need to be bashful over any attempt to do good for good’s sake. reply saagarjha 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I'm a little confused how this is even feasible to do in general. Most inference is batched and often implemented on heterogeneous hardware. In this environment, it's hard to get bitwise identical results, much less prove that the results came from a certain model. Is this actually practical? In the post near the end it doesn't seem like the authors think they have done anything for this and it seems pretty important for it to be used for any production environment. reply aruss 3 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n This sort of result is about steady progress. It's not practical, yet. This system only allows for 1k context, not even enough for a system prompt. But the authors have contributed several orders of magnitude of speedup on the current state of the art. reply khalic 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n What a great way to enforce a (still hypothetical) open models ban reply notrealyme123 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I really don't like that the first more elaborated real-world case is to stop people from training their own models. (Challenge 1) reply ygouzerh 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Are they doing that because they are scared that Moonshot might route queries to Claude? reply aetherspawn 14 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n So can we get proof that the inference was done on the model we actually paid-for and not some nerfed version. What’s the point of a SOTA model if, by 3 weeks later, it’s dumber than the previous generation at launch. (If you did this random substitution with physical goods it would probably be illegal or very frowned upon…) reply fscaramuzza 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n My PhD is on auditability of AI systems, and we are exactly working on the engineering of ZKP in this context. I can confirm through our industry partner (AI Assurance and auditing), that black box auditing is indeed a huge problem for them, and snarks have shown their potential in this context. It's great to see Anthropic confirming our early results. reply ngruhn 18 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm blind to the problem being solved here. Can you motivate it? Why do I need more than normal HTTPS on the token API? reply fscaramuzza 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n HTTPS makes sure you’re communicating on a secure channel with the API endpoint. ZKPs guarantees that the computation happened correctly behind the endpoint. The work from Anthropic isn't a new idea, but they made the process more efficient, as inference verification is one of the biggest bottlenecks in the AI lifecycle. Beyond that, there's a lot more that can be proven, like showing a model was trained on a specific dataset with certain properties. From an auditing perspective, this used to be handled either by showing the data (which is pretty rare), checking samples (which has been shown to be easily hackable), or similar workarounds. For inference, an auditor might want to interact with a model on demand and have absolute certainty that the model they're talking to is actually the one declared, and not a substitute. reply grey-area 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I’m not sure what you mean by correctly here - do you mean with the model expected? That’s not related to correctness. Pleased to hear somebody cares about this. What is it a bottleneck for though? Are people actually doing this right now in non-academic settings? I had the impression this is not in fact usable with larger models. reply fscaramuzza 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n ZKPs verify \"things\" by encoding the verification in a computation, and verifying that the computation happened in the right way, means to assess it was computed correctly, i.e., with the right inputs, the right operations, etc. \nSo this is what I meant with \"correctness\". The bottleneck has two sides: first, there's a technical bottleneck. ZKPs are quite expensive, and finding the right, production-ready protocol is challenging. The other side is a socio-technical bottleneck. There is always quite a large friction with adopting these kind of technologies, also due to the first bottleneck. As you said, it is difficult to adopt because of how fast the complexity grows with big models. It's great indeed to see that Anthropic is tackling the challenge. reply ngruhn 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > guarantees that the computation happened correctly behind the endpoint but why do I care about that? can you some example \"user stories\"? reply brcmthrowaway 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n So, you got beaten to the punch? reply fscaramuzza 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Well luckily not really, their work is more foundational-y, the idea is not new, but they made it more efficient and practical. On our side, we work more on the integration of these protocol in production systems, but we are pretty much on the frontier and is not very common to see such big companies experimenting with this technology. Microsoft already tried something [1] but I'm not sure if it ever went further than a research prototype. [1] https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.02675 reply arcwhite 15 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wager regulatory capture lies this way. Look for this becoming an in-hardware feature, with big fat patents on it, and then a push to demand all inference providers use \"TPM chipped\" hardware. reply ronfriedhaber 17 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Attestable does great work on the subject:\n https://attestable.com/blog/proving-llms-scale reply dnnehgf 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n i guess the idea here is that if the end user cannot discriminate based on capability above some threshold, the premium that you can charge for more capable models either disappears entirely (if the end user is the principal source of truth on quality) or gets arbitraged down (if the end user is merely an agent of the principal source of truth on quality). to maintain that premium in perpetuity, you just need (a) trusted benchmarking services that can distinguish between premium and generic, and then (b) a way to prove to the end user that a given output is premium. so this solves for (b). reply wcoenen 17 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > The goal of provable inference (PI) is to enable a user with limited computational resources and without any information about the weights to verify that a response came from the requested model Isn't this already solved by watermarking? This page said they would provide an API to check whether a given text has the watermark for a Claude model: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-text-watermark reply hobofan 17 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n That's two entirely different types of things that are being verified. Watermarking checks whether a _specific text_ came from a provider. PI checks that the text (that you already know came from the provider) was produced with _a specific set of weights_. PI is mainly useful for AI supply-chain security. reply seurimas 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Not to the degree needed for actually verifying the things this verifies. You could probably quite easily produce a non-Claude model that generates text that conforms to Claude watermarking. reply wcoenen 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n In the page I linked above, Anthropic explains that they use the techniques from SynthID-Text, where a secret watermarking key is involved. Only Anthropic knows this key, so only Anthropic can produce and detect the watermark. (But they also promised to provide an API through which other parties can check the watermark.) SynthID-Text paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-08025-4 reply saifelse 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think there are two gaps with the watermark: - it only gives a high probability that the text was outputted from the model, whereas a crytographic proof is computationally infeasible to forge - it cannot be traced back to the input. You could \"wash\" the output from one model into another and it would be watermarked by the latter. With the crytographic proof, if you have the input, output, and \"commitment\", you can verify your exact input was processed by the model (or so the model provider says). reply lrvick 13 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Very cool. Now prove you are unable to read chat transcripts with provable end to end encryption down to the CPU registers and TPM endorsement chain of the software involved. Provable privacy via remote attestation effectively gives you provable inference for free. reply jsrozner 13 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"The commitment to the model, and subsequent inference proofs, hide the weights of the model; hiding the architecture of the model is not a goal of this system.\" Your Claude is showing. reply ljlolel 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n already solved provable inference with encrypted chats on trustedrouter (using TEEs which most people don’t know about) reply jmort 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n What are the tradeoffs of an approach like this vs confidential computing primitives and attesting everything from untampered hardware all the way up to the served request? It seems more performant. Is it simply that they would have to reveal too much? reply FrasiertheLion 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n More so that attestation requires the model to be running on secure hardware, which is hard to do across all models that a company like Anthropic serves. In part because many of the accelerators they use may not support attestation, and even with ones that do, such as NVIDIA Blackwell, support is restricted to a single node (8 GPUs). Vera Rubin supports rack-scale encryption, but that is not broadly deployed yet and will not be the only accelerator they use to serve inference. reply anonymous908213 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > A user querying a model today cannot check that the responses came from the specific model that a developer claims is responding Indeed, I wonder what kind of developer would make users worry about that...? Funny PR stunt coming from a company that was in the news for silently routing ML-related requests to an inferior model. https://fortune.com/2026/06/10/anthropic-accu-claude-fable-5... > Unlike Fable’s other restrictions, such as around cybersecurity and biology, which openly redirect users to a less powerful model with a visible notification, the system card emphasized that this is “not visible to the user.” The model still responds, but uses “interventions to limit Claude’s effectiveness” without telling the user it’s doing so. reply with 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n isn't model substitution a vendor accountability thing? and token tampering, is that not infra security? do either of these need 70-95x the GPU time in \"zksnark\" proofs? reply ex-aws-dude 17 hours ago | prev [–] \n > Some proposals to regulate and pace AI development rely on monitoring hardware and how it is used. For example, a provider might be required to devote its hardware to inference rather than to training a powerful new model. The provider might try to cheat by serving cheaper models or using compute in an unexpected way, freeing up resources for training. How can a regulator reliably ensure that the hardware is used for inference? Is this even a real problem? Forcing a company to only do inference? Sounds like Anthropic's dream scenario reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
