 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50369106": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:48:04.509Z",
  "bytes": 61726,
  "text": " A new Go API for JSON | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login A new Go API for JSON ( go.dev ) 342 points by olexsmir 18 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 173 comments help \n \n kvemkon 19 minutes ago | next [–] \n To preserve the order when processing JSON a workaround has been introduced: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=44893833 Still searching the true solution: https://github.com/golang/go/issues/27179#issuecomment-53634... A proposal of the missing datatype for this operation mode has been just accepted: https://github.com/golang/go/issues/69559 reply modulovalue 17 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I'm working on better SIMD support in Dart and one of the motivations for that work is specializing parser specifications based on schema specifications of that language. Or more concretely, if we had a parser generator + json parser specification that can generate efficient simdjson style parsers, then we should be able to parse json restricted to a subset of json based on a schema at least as efficiently as simdjson can parse general purpose json. That way we could have the best of both worlds, a (potentially lazy) statically type-safe model and simdjson performance! I still think that this is definitely doable, it's just that a lot of infrastructure needs to be built for it. I remember reading that Meta is doing something similar with binary formats. I find it weird that there's nothing like that for json yet. reply weitendorf 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I was working on metaparsing earlier this year (specifically over grpc because of its better support for types and better serialization format). I think most people who use JSON don't want strong typing or to use schemas. If you do, you just use a different serialization format: JSON is designed to be a generic, human-readable way to basically represent any kind of data structure (since it deserializes to JS objects). So everything about JSON is working against you if you do try to enforce type/schemas on it. A grpc-like protocol with more prescriptive type registries/discovery (over the network) and better interop with http (like grpc-gateway) would be the best approach IMO. Definitely not JSON schema reply tclancy 40 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think it may be fairer to say, “most people pick up JSON because of the flexibility.” Those who continue to use it for anything where there’s a consumer of your API probably do come to want types. In my day job I am starting to enforce end-to-end typing as best I can (given it’s Python and JSON) because our payloads are partially content from agents and I want to be able to flag up weird shit if we can. Certainly not the only gate that we have for looking for prompt injection and not even a great one, but it provides some piece of mind. reply tcfhgj 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > think most people who use JSON don't want strong typing or to use schemas. If you do, you just use a different serialization format: JSON is designed to be a generic, human-readable way to basically represent any kind of data structure (since it deserializes to JS objects). So everything about JSON is working against you if you do try to enforce type/schemas on it. I have been using serde without issues, and never resorted to `serde::Value`[0], the untyped option, so far. I have yet to experience anything working against me. [0]: https://docs.rs/serde_json/latest/serde_json/enum.Value.html reply ahtihn 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > I think most people who use JSON don't want strong typing or to use schemas. We use JSON because it's standard, human-readable, mostly self-described and works natively with Javascript. You can read JSON object without having to know the schema even if one exists. Protobuf is unusablr garbage without a lot of supporting tooling. reply dankobgd 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I definitely want jsonschema, it's a standard and i can use it for many tools.\nopenapi spec is also a standard and built on top of jsonschema. I can use jsonschema to generate models, types, servers, client sdks, automate forms etc. \nI think it's better than grpc for some of these things, plus protobufs and grpc have bunch of tooling that i can pretend to understand but in depth i don't really. reply thayne 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > I think most people who use JSON don't want strong typing or to use schemas. Not necessarily. It's biggest advantage is probably that it has very good interoperability. Basically every language has a readily accessible library for json, and even if you don't, it isn't that hard to write a parser and/or generator for it. And being human readable isn't something to sneeze at either. reply lightandlight 16 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This approach is called \"staged parsing\": https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/2714064.2660241 https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3314221.3314625 reply brunoborges 15 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > \"JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) is a simple data interchange format [...]\" Clearly it isn't, otherwise every language would have sorted it out by... a long time ago. reply yubblegum 12 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Meet the \" Simple Object Access Protocol\": https://www.w3.org/TR/2000/NOTE-SOAP-20000508/ reply thiht 41 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It IS a simple format. And you can make a parser yourself very easily. Doesn’t mean that it will be the fastest or most memory efficient. Don’t confuse format and implementation reply kccqzy 13 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Some languages have a culture of doing things correctly and conforming strictly to the spec. These languages have indeed sorted it out years ago. Haskell for example. Any language that doesn’t have a special number type for JSON is a half-assed implementation, because 1e1000 is a perfectly valid JSON number that is beyond the typical IEEE754 double precision floating point numbers; does your language handle that correctly? Haskell does. This is but a small example of a strong culture bias towards correctness. reply ncruces 13 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Ironically Go has(d) json.Number which almost no one used, so it got dropped from v2: https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Number You can still use it though, and I built my tiny decimal library around it: https://github.com/ncruces/decimal reply layer8 13 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n The spec (RFC 8259) leaves significant leeway to implementations: A JSON parser MAY accept non-JSON forms or extensions. An implementation may set limits on the size of texts that it accepts. An implementation may set limits on the maximum depth of nesting. An implementation may set limits on the range and precision of numbers. An implementation may set limits on the length and character contents of strings. Add to that things like duplicate keys being allowed by the spec. There’s a lot of implementation choices to decide on. And then things can get really complex with JSON Schema. reply kccqzy 12 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah that’s what I mean by strictly. Does your implementation take shortcuts or does it hold itself to a higher standard and not do that? Does it only use available memory as the ultimate limit? A lot of specs contain these optional limitations. The C standard allowed implementations to truncate variable names to 31 characters if they have external linkage and 63 if internal linkage. Would you be happy if your implementation actually does that? Of course not. Neither LLVM nor GCC does that. That’s because people writing compilers also hold themselves to a higher standard. Now imagine this kind of attitude being pervasive. reply layer8 26 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The thing is this goes both ways. For example, JSON with duplicate keys is valid JSON, and the standard specifies two ways to handle them. To support all ways JSON is used, an implementation will have to provide for both ways, and decide which one to pick as the default. The stricter default on the parsing side is at the same time the less interoperable one. Same for numeric precision: seamlessly allowing higher precision on the generating side lowers interoperability towards the receiver (but lessens demands on the driving application), while accepting it on the parsing side increases interoperability (but also places higher demands on the consuming application). So what do you do? There is no easy and simple answer, unless you want to limit the scope of applicability of your implementation. reply queenkjuul 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I don't get your argument here. You say Haskell conforms strictly to the spec and that's a good thing, okay. But then if the C spec allows for truncated names, a compiler could strictly follow the spec and truncate names. So strictly following the spec is not in fact the whole story reply kccqzy 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yeah very sorry for the confusion. I should not have used the word strictly . I explained my definition of strictly in the above comment and it’s not really strictly in the dictionary sense. I apologize for my bad writing. reply anthk 9 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Which C standard? ANSI C? C99? reply DrBazza 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n My company uses JSUN (/sarcasm) where O = object, and U = union. Who knew the same object could have completely different definitions based on some flags? reply ivanjermakov 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Simple data format does not imply that API to work with it is easy to get right. reply DanielHB 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I mean, it is better than the disaster that is CSV... reply jayd16 14 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Ok so nothing is simple then? reply CamouflagedKiwi 17 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I generally like `encoding/json/v2` and I'm glad they did it. I wish they'd left `MarshalIndent` in there though. The equivalent code is super complicated and non-obvious, so it's a pain to migrate, and it's a pain when you're banging out some quick code and want to dump some readable JSON. I suppose these days the LLMs can write the complicated version for us, of course. reply athorax 17 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm not sure if I'm missing something, but isn't this functionality just moved into jsontext? https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json/jsontext#Value.Indent v := jsontext.Value(b)\n v.Indent()\n fmt.Println(string(v)) reply irq-1 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n func WithIndent(indent string) Options\n \n https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json/jsontext#WithIndent reply arccy 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n json.Marshal(v, jsontext.WithIndent(\" \"))\n\n? reply txomon 14 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I used the jsontext package at my company when we needed to make a parser for challenging clients (specific errors for each kind of mistake) while still keeping performance, and it works like a charm. There is no introspection and speed is superb. I would definitely recommend jsontext as a parser for anything non-straightforward. reply dofm 17 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I… think this is good? I'm just getting the neuron-band back together where Golang is concerned. I have done some in the past for little system tasks, but I am switching away from interpreted languages entirely for web stuff, and initially to Go, as I switch away from hand-coding to at least LLM-supported coding. So I am a bit out of date on the pain points (I never really felt pain points when I last used it, because the jobs I was doing had very small, quite Go-friendly shapes). reply cbarrick 16 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n The fact that this took 6 years to land stood out to me. I'm not entirely surprised, and I'm glad the Go team is carefully considering their interfaces before launching. But 6 years seems like a lot of work to land a JSON API. reply awesome_dude 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Sometimes I wonder about Go's velocity - generics (something that I still haven't used beyond the tutorials) took an age as well JSON processing, quite markedly, is renown in Go for being slow, so I don't really understand why it took so long reply Philip-J-Fry 13 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n For like 99% of use cases encoding/json was fine. Of course improvements are welcomed. But personally I've lead development of the backend of part one of the biggest sites of its kind in the world, and we just used encoding/json and net/http. Most of our use cases are just building APIs, client facing ones, internal ones, trusted third-parties. These systems have pretty high throughput, more than most people will ever work on. Within our company I saw people in other teams writing their own optimised JSON libraries for whatever hellish reason. I always reiterated the point that as soon as that app made a single IO request all those nanosecond savings are invisible. Yes, it affects your total CPU time for processing a request but compute was never ever a limitation for us and it's likely not a limitation for most people. And if the trade off is your app needs scale slightly larger versus writing custom JSON code or pulling in a third party dependency, then I'm accepting the larger scaling. The biggest benefit of encoding/json/v2 isn't the speed, it's the improved API. reply hnlmorg 16 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think in the case of JSON, it was “good enough”. People who found this to be a problem would use one of the many 3rd party options. I kind of like that the standard library doesn’t move fast. It makes it easier to fit the ecosystem into my head (unlike, for example, the JavaScript ecosystem where I find code doesn’t survive 2 years before failing to compile). But I do agree that slow and steady produces its own problems. Your example of generics is a good one. But think the example of Go modules is another, perhaps even better, example of how sometimes the Go team really should have accelerated their efforts on. reply awesome_dude 14 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n lol, I don't mean to be contrary, but I looked into dependency management (partly because of the modules implementation, partly because of early teething issues, and partly out of pure curiousity. Dependency management is a freaking nightmare, nobody has managed to create something that is bulletproof, not cargo, npm, apt, yum, nix, homebrew, nothing. The key issue is that you're managing 3rd party software that may or may not adhere to the prescribed lifecycle/versioning The Linux variants do it by each having dedicated teams watching upstream sources, taking what they believe to be whatever they feel is right, building for their nominated target(s), then testing, before deploying (on top of that, all of these steps vary in importance and rigor depending on numerous variables) For programming libraries (which is what depmod, goose, vgo, and modules are managing) it's just as bad, if not harder, because their very nature is to provide a base upon which other developers can turn into whatever their imagination. Add to that, modules was (initially) written by people who were dealing with a slightly different ecosystem; Google rather famously has a monorepo, making their lives a little easier when it comes to managing dependencies. So, with those things in mind, dependency management is always going to be a hard problem that is opinionated (eg. I have a firm opinion that there should be an easier way to \"pin\" a dependency, other than vendoring - note that this isn't an invitation to every junior Go developer to come and claim go.sum does that. It doesn't. go.sum is a log of what dependencies were used for the last build by whomever wrote to it) reply hnlmorg 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I’ve written package management tools and I agree that dependency management is hard. Though I wouldn’t say it’s that hard compared to the other problems you encounter with language development. But difficulty aside, my point was Go waited until there were multiple established 3rd party package management tools for Go already in widespread use before even releasing their own experimental implementation. Their inaction caused far more disruption to the ecosystem because people got fed up of waiting, implemented their own, integrated it into their workflow and then had to reintegrate a whole new system all over again a year or two later. Your point about Googles needs is the one often cited for Gos inaction. But I think that actually makes the situation worse. Because if the Go team cannot empathise with use cases outside of one bespoke enterprise, then it really shouldn’t be released as a general purpose language available for all to use. And I say this as someone who generally likes the language. reply Mawr 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Interesting, I came to the opposite conclusions, but for the same reasons. I like that they waited to see what alternative proposals/implementations were out there before they decided on their own. Especially since \"Go team cannot empathise with use cases outside of one bespoke enterprise\" — it shows self-awareness of their shorcomings. I think it's wise to let the target audience explore the design space themselves a bit first. Considerably lowers the risk of cementing the wrong design. I do somewhat remember the drama around this at the time, but I my impression was that of a petulent community of various dependency management projects with too much ego attached to them to see the bigger, long-term picture. reply cxr 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > there should be an easier way to \"pin\" a dependency, other than vendoring You left out the part that explains why. reply win311fwg 16 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > generics (something that I still haven't used beyond the tutorials) took an age as well Only about a year from when the Haskell guys lent their expertise in designing it and it landing in a release. That's actually fairly quick given that it was a pretty significant change under the hood and constrained by only two releases annually. The Go team was always clear that they didn't have the necessary capabilities internally, despite a lot of failed efforts trying anyway, and were relying on the open source community to lend a hand. Unfortunately, open source doesn't mean open willingness to contribute. It turns out that for all the experts on HN, posting \"go doesn't have generics lol\" was preferable to helping out. reply pjmlp 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Those guys were the same that helped out with generics in Java, they even published a book in 2006, https://homepages.inf.ed.ac.uk/wadler/topics/gj.html Before Featherweight Go, there was Featherweight Java. So maybe a language designed in 2012 (v 1.0 release year), should learn from history instead of hand-waving it away as needless fluff. > In retrospect, we were biased too much by experience with C++ without concepts and Java generics. We would have been well-served to spend more time with CLU and C++ concepts earlier. https://github.com/golang/proposal/blob/master/design/go2dra... And not all on the team were convinced even after Featherweight Go, here Rob Pike, > I think the transition to getting polymorphism into the language through generics is has still got some years to work through there's still things that don't quite work right https://youtu.be/yE5Tpp2BSGw?t=2134 reply win311fwg 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > instead of hand-waving it away as needless fluff. If you go back and watch the original Go announcement it was made abundantly clear that they believed Go did need generics but that they didn't have the expertise to do it. Yes it is true that the book was already written on it, but that doesn't mean anyone on the Go team was familiar with it or had the time to become familiar with it. Programming languages do not require gods to design them. Imperfect humans can too. The experts on HN who know all the ins and outs of generics could have contributed but, you know, shitposting \"go doesn't have generics lol\" was a better use of their time. There are so many hours in the day. reply pwdisswordfishq 43 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The key point here is our programming language designers are Googlers, they’re not researchers. They’re typically, fairly young, fresh out of school… reply pjmlp 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Gonuts is full of content that they were not keen in generics, in fact gccgo has been abandoned, and I expect it to eventually follow gcj and be removed from GCC. Go culture is not welcoming to experts contributions, Rust, D, C#, Swift and others, heck even Java, are more open arms to language designers. reply win311fwg 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Nobody is keen about language features. Each new one adds complexity. That isn't the same as failing to recognize a need, though. And we know the Go team recognized the need as they clearly kept working on generics all the way from the very beginnings up until the Haskell muscle showed up. The receipts are there. The results just weren't good, as one would expect from a team who weren't shy to say that they didn't have the expertise necessary. You'd think one of the HN experts would have shown up to offer that expertise, but I guess there was a lot of \"go doesn't have generics lol\" needing to get out. reply pjmlp 8 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Some did try, and were welcomed with stones, clubs and pitchforks, until leadership had to reach out for help, from the same team that designed Java generics, years before Go v1.0 came to materialise. The Haskell m