  "text": " \n This Month in Ladybird - October 2026 - Ladybird\n \n About News Get involved Sponsors FAQ Donate This Month in Ladybird &mdash; October 2026 Hello friends! In October, we moved style and layout off the main thread, built a JavaScript JIT, replaced our WebAssembly engine, redesigned the browser UI, and passed Safari Technology Preview on Web Platform Tests. :^) \n Sponsors \n Ladybird is entirely funded by companies and individuals who believe in the open web. You can see everyone supporting the project on our sponsors page , and we’re grateful to all of them. If you’re interested in sponsoring, please contact us . \n Style and layout no longer wait for JavaScript \n Browsers normally run JavaScript, style, and layout on one main thread, one at a time. While a script runs, the page can’t update. A few simple animations can keep going on the compositor, but everything else has to wait. \n We moved style and layout to their own thread, and paint recording to another ( #12377 , #12400 ). They now run at the same time as JavaScript ( #12396 , #12432 , #12460 ). If a script asks for layout information, like an element’s size, it waits until pending layout work is finished, so it never gets stale data. \n \n \n So two things now work during a long script: \n \n CSS animations keep running while JavaScript is busy. This includes animations that change layout, like width and font-size ( #12433 , #12447 , #12451 ). \n :hover keeps working while JavaScript is busy. The render thread finds the element under the mouse, applies its hover styles, starts any transitions, updates the cursor, and draws a new frame. When the script ends, the main thread catches up and fires the hover events ( #12530 ). \n \n As far as we know, no other browser can do this today. Here’s a text animation while JavaScript blocks the main thread in 1.5 second bursts, with Ladybird in the top left: \n \n And here’s :hover on a page running JavaScript for 250ms at a time, with Ladybird on the left and Chrome on the right: \n \n You can also try it yourself. The button blocks the main thread for three seconds. Most browsers keep the square spinning, but freeze the bar and the hover target. \n transform width Hover me :hover \nRun JavaScript for 3 seconds\n Idle \n A JavaScript JIT \n On Linux and macOS, LibJS now compiles hot JavaScript to machine code, on both x86-64 and AArch64 ( #12613 ). \n We decided against a JIT in the past, and did what we could to push and tune the interpreter instead. It’s already competitive, so making it twice as fast would be a huge effort, and that would only turn a 300ms hang into a 150ms hang, which you’d still notice. So we needed an optimizing JIT. \n The design is deliberately boring: speculative optimization based on runtime type profiling, like other JavaScript engines. For now, we just want to go reasonably fast. \n \n \n While code warms up, we record things like which types and object shapes it sees ( #12588 ). The compiled code uses those recordings to make guesses, like “this value is always an integer”, and inlines small functions ( #12595 , #12598 ). \n To find bugs during development, we used a fuzzer that ran the same code in the JIT and the interpreter and compared the results ( #12601 ). \n A new WebAssembly engine \n We replaced our C++ Wasm engine and Cranelift with a new engine that has its own interpreter and JIT ( #12963 ). It supports newer parts of WebAssembly that the old engine didn’t, like exception handling and GC. On the JetStream 3 Wasm workloads both engines can run, it scores about 7x higher. \n \n \n In July, we built flap, a small language and compiler for writing LibJS’s interpreter. The Wasm interpreter is written in flap too, so one source builds it for x86-64, AArch64, and Windows x64. Hot functions move from there to the JIT, even in the middle of a running loop. \n We’re making great progress on performance. JSLinux , an x86 PC emulator, now boots Alpine Linux to a shell in 1.8 seconds, while Chrome takes 1.5. \n Benchmarks \n \n \n Web Platform Tests (WPT) \n On October 26, we passed Safari Technology Preview on WPT . :^) We ended the month at 2,184,722 subtests, a gain of 75,650. \n \n \n Most of that gain came in five days, from about 2.12 million on October 22 to about 2.18 million on October 27. We pointed AI coding agents at WPT and asked them to fill in missing functionality. They made lots of small fixes, and those added up. Our biggest gains for the month were about 30,000 in /css and 10,000 in /editing . \n A new browser UI \n \n \n Until now, our UI had grown organically, with tinkering here and there. This was the first time we sat down and mapped out a complete design. We kept it modest and quiet, so the attention stays on the page. \n It covers tabs, toolbars, menus, and our own about: pages on macOS and Linux, in light, dark, and a violet dark theme for private windows ( #13271 , #13169 , #12815 ). \n \n \n \n \n \n Tabs: you can pin tabs, search all open tabs, and restore your windows on launch ( #12757 , #13271 ). \n Form controls got a new look, and date and time inputs got a picker ( #13554 ). \n Scrolling: smooth mouse wheel scrolling ( #12593 ), elastic overscroll on macOS ( #12624 ), touchpad momentum on other platforms ( #12345 ), and scrollbars that hide until used ( #12997 ). \n \n Sites that work better \n \n Google Slides: presentations open instead of showing an error ( #12497 ). \n GitHub: Mermaid diagrams render ( #12383 ). \n ChatGPT: the text box grows as you type ( #13050 ). \n nytimes.com and reuters.com get past their bot checks ( #12718 , #12188 ). \n Instagram: videos show their first frame instead of staying blank ( #12898 ). \n Gmail: reloading no longer keeps the old page in memory ( #12689 , #12660 ). \n x86CSS , an x86 emulator made of CSS, is now usable ( #12911 ). \n \n New browser features \n \n Screen readers: we’ve added first support for VoiceOver on macOS and Orca on Linux ( #9156 ). There’s still a lot of work to do here. \n Spell checking in text fields on macOS ( #12638 ). \n Picture-in-Picture ( #12542 ). \n Links to text: links can scroll to a piece of text and highlight it, and you can create them from the context menu ( #11121 , #12744 ). \n Permissions: sites can now ask for your location and microphone ( #13336 , #12604 ). \n DevTools: Inspect Element ( #13348 ), console autocomplete ( #13355 ), and expandable objects in the console ( #13360 ). \n Smaller additions: Find in page can highlight all matches ( #12434 ), ad blocking can be turned off per site from the toolbar ( #12695 ), and we added Reload Without Cache ( #13056 ). \n \n More of the web platform \n We rendered this page in Ladybird, using features that landed in October: \n \n \n From the top left, that’s multi-column ( #13026 ), display: grid-lanes for masonry ( #13236 ), MathML ( #13062 , #13141 ), corner-shape ( #12942 ), CSS Motion Path ( #13027 ), much more of anchor positioning ( #13021 , #13079 , #13166 ), and text-wrap: balance ( #13461 ). \n We also added: \n \n CSS: calc-size() , which lets heights animate to and from auto ( #13006 ), plus vertical writing modes ( #13103 ), zoom ( #13237 ), the stretch sizing keyword ( #13006 ), shape() and border-shape ( #12942 ), field-sizing ( #13050 ), view progress timelines and animation-range ( #12943 ), and the start of SVG animation ( #12823 , #13498 ). \n Web APIs and more: the HTML Sanitizer API ( #13185 ), Touch Events ( #13249 ), Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy ( #12296 ), Paint Timing, Event Timing, Largest Contentful Paint ( #13110 ), WebVTT cues ( #12895 ), wide-gamut canvases ( #13097 ), WebDriver BiDi ( #13167 ), JavaScript’s using and await using ( #13102 ), and many execCommand and contenteditable fixes ( #13186 , #13038 ). \n \n Security and process isolation \n Web pages run in sandboxed renderer processes, so a malicious page that takes over its renderer should be stuck there. \n We kept fixing security issues from many sources, including ways around that sandbox. For example, a compromised renderer could ask the browser process to open any file on your computer. Now only a renderer showing local files can read them ( #12582 , #12590 ). Renderers could also fake a user click to open popups or read the clipboard. The browser process no longer trusts them on this ( #12597 ). \n We also split the browser into more processes, so a compromised one can reach less: \n \n \n \n Cross-site iframes get their own renderer by default ( #12647 ), and so do cross-site popups ( #12296 ). \n Each top-level site gets its own network process, and the HTTP cache and third-party cookies are kept separate per site ( #13144 , #12424 ). \n Each renderer has its own audio process ( #12604 ) and image decoder ( #12437 ). \n \n Media and Windows \n On Windows, video now plays and YouTube works ( #12646 , #13195 ). Hardware video decoding moved to its own process and now works on Linux through Vulkan ( #12868 ). We also read MP4 files ourselves now instead of using FFmpeg, which fixes problems with seeking and buffering ( #12641 ). \n More C++ moved to Rust \n LibJS’s runtime moved from C++ to Rust just before the JIT landed, so our JavaScript engine is now all Rust, apart from its flap interpreter and the garbage collector ( #12492 ). The new Wasm engine is Rust as well. WebGL, 2D canvas, IndexedDB, Streams, and URL also moved over ( #12699 , #12935 , #13287 , #13083 , #12998 ). We also deleted the C++ style computation, so style is now computed entirely in Rust ( #12346 ). \n Getting ready for alpha \n Our first alpha is planned for later this year. This month we added: \n \n Release builds: macOS archives, Linux tarballs, .deb and .rpm packages, and Flatpak bundles ( #13176 , #13313 , #13340 ). \n Crash reports: after a crash, you can review a report and choose to send it to us ( #12298 , #12342 ). \n A welcome page to pick your tab layout, search engine, and ad blocking ( #12333 ). \n Reporting broken sites from inside the browser ( #12880 ). \n \n That’s it for October. Thanks for reading, and we’ll see you next month! Stay in the loop Monthly updates about Ladybird, straight to your inbox. Subscribe About News Get involved Donate FAQ Organization \n&copy; 2024&ndash;2026 Ladybird Browser Initiative\n GitHub Discord YouTube X RSS \n "
 },
 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50369194": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-05T12:48:30.668Z",
  "bytes": 13469,
  "text": " This Month in Ladybird – October 2026 | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login This Month in Ladybird – October 2026 ( ladybird.org ) 115 points by BitPirate 18 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 39 comments help \n \n quux 18 hours ago | next [–] \n Ladybird has been really shaping up over the last month or so, I pull and build the latest bits every morning and use it for low stakes web browsing (HN, news sites, reddit, youtube, blogs, etc.) and it's getting very close to the point where I forget that it's a pre-alpha. Very exciting to watch a completely new browser/browser engine come together. Reminds me of the early days of Mozilla and Gecko Posted with Ladybird :) reply sashank_1509 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I suspect in a year or so, an entire browser can be prompted by AI and then it won’t feel all that cool anymore. Unless AI hits a wall soon (unlikely). reply Wowfunhappy 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I've actually used AI to create a WebKit port (not an original browser). The amazing thing is that it worked--but it still took months of issue triage. Even when the AI was working on its own, it had to spend enormous amounts of time just on compiling and testing. There are so many moving pieces, and each individual issue could mean the AI spinning for days. Even if a future AI can drive web browser development completely autonomously, I suspect it will take a huge amount of real-world time because that's just the shape of the work no matter how \"smart\" you are. reply Omniusaspirer 14 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yep, I've been building a custom H2/H3 server for nearly 6 months now maxing my Claude 200 plan every week. Very pleased with the results but it's definitely not a quick or cheap task, it's constant decision fatigue and tons of rigorous testing on every piece in turn. Fuzzing, mutation testing, Miri, protocol conformance suites, H2load, actual networked runs, so on and so on. reply sashank_1509 12 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I mean it worked, if scaling doesn’t stop and it can do this non stop at GPT Ultra speeds, this whole task will be done in weeks, maybe lesser. Doesn’t refute my point, ladybird is unfortunately going to be a pointless project soon. Though I guess if you have fun, who cares. reply Wowfunhappy 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No, because the bottleneck isn't just model speed. The model has to literally compile the engine and run the massive test suites. Some of that could be sped up if you had a real build farm instead of my little computer, but there's a limit to how much you can parallelize things. And remember, I was porting WebKit, not remaking it, which would be orders of magnitude more work. So you're not going to get to \"build me an original web browser from scratch, make no mistakes\" and get a new app the next morning or even next week, I don't think. reply senko 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm doing a web browser from scratch, no web or js engine reused (only general-purpose libraries allowed), in my spare time/tokens on a Claude $100 sub. After a month or so, many simple sites work, and now the bottleneck is the stupid amount of JS sites use for everything, and Cloudflare which doesn't like someone using a non-officially-approved browser. Absolutely not a replacement for Chrome or Ladybird, but it can be done already. reply xydone 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Why is it that no conversation can be had about anything without AI being shoehorned into it, what did the parent comment even have to do with this reply JumpCrisscross 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n AI is trained on human works. The preferences Ladybird expresses will influence those browsers. reply arecsu 16 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n \"[...] as far as we know, no other browser can do this today. Here’s a text animation while JavaScript blocks the main thread in 1.5 second bursts, with Ladybird in the top left:\" This is amazing!! I'm really surprised no other browser is able to do this. Is there an implicit \"security\" issue with it, or maybe standardization when it comes to keeping some values from DOM-Javascript in sync? Or simply an oversight in their part? In any case, congratulations to Ladybird's team. Computers are so much more capable of raw performance and computing, and it's nice that some developers out there do care heavily about it reply king_crimson 14 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n afaik the decision to run animations on or off the main thread is a recurring point of debate not just in web browsers.\nOn iOS for example SwiftUI runs them on the main thread while UIKit w/ CoreAnimation runs them off it. I don’t remember the specific reasons for these decisions but the point is, as with many things there are pros and cons to both approaches. reply nvme0n1p1 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Opera's old engine (Presto) was able to do this too, before Opera switched to Blink. This is a perfect example of why browser diversity is important. reply CitrusFruits 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Ladybird is such an exciting project, really excited to try it out for day to day browsing when they get into Alpha. reply quux 18 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n It's very easy to build it yourself if you want to try it right now. https://github.com/LadybirdBrowser/ladybird/blob/master/Docu... reply anthk 13 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'd love a Flatpak... and maybe Gopher support in the browser ;) reply mempko 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Honest question for people. I'm a C++ dev and I'm seeing a lot of moves to rust. Everyone is using agents to move stuff to rust and I tried it myself. The amount of insane hardrive space usage (over 90GB of build artifacts that constantly need cleanup) is that normal? What am I doing wrong. Are people seeing advantages after a move to rust that wasn't there in C++ (assuming no new features). Like is your project better after moving to rust in obvious ways? reply suriyaG 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Once I spent a month debugging a UAF bug, in a messy C++ codebase. With rust, those class of errors are very difficult to arrive at, unless you specifically make it so. with rust, the \"risk\" gets moved from runtime to compiletime. I'll take a 1TB build bloat over code crashing in prod. any day. reply reallyreason 16 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I have the same problem and am regularly `cargo clean`ing hundreds of gigabytes of build artifacts. It is a real problem. I feel like I need minimum 4TB of SSD space just to comfortably work at the scale I'm working now in Rust. That's just for the working space of the target folders across worktrees. I just think agents benefit a lot from having unavoidable feedback in the dev loop, and Rust affords that through its strictness. It's easier for agents to fall into the pit of success with Rust. I do think it's somewhat lacking in compile-time features, though. reply pornel 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's expected. Cargo has very conservative cache cleanup (once per month IIRC). There's incremental compilation cache that generates lots of churn (although Rust's compilation unit is a crate, \"queries\" in the compiler are cached individually, creating many cache entries per function per type). The other half of the disk space is debug info, because abstractions that optimize down to zero runtime code still carry all of their source-level debug info. These can be adjusted in build profiles if you're ok with coarser debug info or less fine-grained caching. reply afdbcreid 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Cargo has no cache cleanup for `target` on stable. reply nicoburns 16 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's normal. But you can somewhat mitigate by: - Making sure you compile with as few profiles as possible. - Disabling debug info (or setting it to \"line-table-only\") for the default profile. reply andoowhy 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's normal, but they're working on it: https://blog.rust-lang.org/inside-rust/2026/08/18/reducing-t... reply ahartmetz 16 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Shared libraries (i.e. not building every damn dependency) would fix this problem real quick in many situations, but of course, Rust people don't want to commit to an ABI in order to keep their options open. I don't think it makes sense much longer - at this point \"can't do shared libraries\" seems more like cultural inertia. Two or so ABI breaks with a few years between them also seem quite survivable. reply slopinthebag 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n 90gb? i've ran cargo clean on a single project and reclaimed over 700gb before. but i still love rust ofc reply ASalazarMX 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Jesus, that makes the JavaScript ecosystem look moderate by comparison! reply nicoburns 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The difference is that this accumulates over time. You'll only get this if you haven't run \"clean\" for some time (likely months). reply slopinthebag 12 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n it was days, but it was related to something the agent was doing with tests and targets, where it was basically compiling a billion variations of the almost 500,000loc, 1100 deps, and 50+ crate project it had slopped together lol. reply ranger_danger 17 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Isn't the advantage memory safety? When I mentioned, after C++26 failed its vote, that maybe they should use this extra time to focus more on memory safety, I got downvoted instead. Surely that attitude isn't helping their situation. reply jraph 16 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You probably read too much into the downvotes, and people who downvoted you in the other thread might not even be the same people deciding on the future of C++. I didn't downvote you (that's not something I do) but I didn't find your comment particularly relevant or on topic. This disapproval thing and working on memory safety don't seem related at all. Someone told you they had the impression that you were pushing for Rust without mentioning it. Your comment might have felt like you were backseat driving as well. More memory safety in C++ would be cool, but do you have actual ideas on how to achieve this? There are a lot of reasons people could have downvoted you. You shouldn't worry too much about it though, just relax and forget about it. It's fine. Happens to the best of us. reply anthk 16 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n >Flatpak It was about time. BTW, when this gem uses EGui instead of QT6 (nothing against QT, but once everything uses Rust, it will be no reason to keep it) it will shine. reply iamnothere 16 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Wow, they now beat Safari in tests passed. Catching up to Firefox too. Lots of momentum here. reply moron4hire 16 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Would love to see where all the folks from 5 years ago, breathlessly declaring it's futile to start a new browser project, are now and what they are doing with their lives. reply Rabbit504030201 16 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Because it IS futile. Occam's Razor shaped hobby project, that what it is. Where is the market share? Nowhere, because existing browsers are good enough and no corporation pushes Ladybird. Showing how far one can go with modern AI tooling does matter only a little when all you do is looking at Chromium/Firefox code and recompiling the logic of it in another language. I'll stick with Firefox. reply kvuj 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Showing how far one can go with modern AI tooling does matter only a little when all you do is looking at Chromium/Firefox code and recompiling the logic of it in another language. Is that what Ladybird does? Can you point to a real world example? From the blog post, it seems there are real world differences (look at the performance when resizing with heavy JS!). You're also mixing arguments. The original arguments against ladybird (which I vividly remember as I loved following them back then) were that it would be impossible to arrive at something that works for all use cases. Ladybird would be able to render toy websites like hacker news, but nothing of worth. It seems they were proven evidently wrong. reply JumpCrisscross 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Where is the market share? Where was the market share for browsers in the 1980s? reply jljljl 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n But that's the thing: it's not 1980 anymore, browsers exist, and they have marketshare reply JumpCrisscross 15 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > it's not 1980 anymore, browsers exist, and they have marketshare Okay, sure. What was the market share for Chrome in 1999? There are legitimate arguments against Ladybird. But pointing to the market share of a non-existent product, in a category on the verge of a paradigm shift from AI, is not one of them. reply anthk 13 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Waterfox switched their AD blocking engine to Rust. It's several times faster than UBo. reply KingMob 8 hours ago | prev [–] \n I will stop using the web entirely before I use anything made by Charlie Kirk-praising, DHH-defending, white supremacist-retweeting Andreas Kling. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
