nd almost all of them could be correct. reply jonplackett 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n At least 49.999 percent of them anyway reply kleiba2 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Are you sure? reply dbdr 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I would hope writing C code for the Linux kernel qualifies you as a real C programmer. And yet, here we are. Being good, careful, and extensively reviewed by others helps, but is clearly not enough. reply meowface 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Sort of, but one has to be pragmatic. reply gb2d_hn 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Isn't it said that with enough users and use cases, every bug becomes a feature that someone depends on. I'd bet that a lot of these vulnerabilities are the result of intentional trade offs in design. reply AlienRobot 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The only thing I know is that if I wanted to learn how to program, I have thousands of tutorials on the Internet and Youtube teaching everything from if/else to data structures and big O. But if I wanted to learn software design and architecture, I'd probably not even know where to start. reply tzury 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n If a web application is vulnerable to sql injection, there are 4,732 ways you can ruin/still a database. Yet it is all one single vulnerability. Now, either those are 1k+ unique distinct techniques, or closer to my Sql injection example. There is no real data attached to the post. And in general, open ai marketing already gained mileage of reputation amongst us. Most likely kernel is not that vulnerable as they made it sounds. reply lpapez 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > When the autonomous scan and manual analysis finished after 10 days, 61 LPEs had already been patched upstream, 43 had staging patches, and 299 were being reported and discussed publicly. If only the kernel maintainers considered the thought which first came to your uninformed mind, they would have saved