| parent | prev | next [–] \n We humans are vulnerable to anthrax or sarin as well, but countries exhibit restraint in using such agents, because retaliation would be gruesome. Having someone going full rogue with cyberattacks could even unite the US and China temporarily in order to whack them. reply locallylinear 33 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n The ROI on this is massive if the bounties would actually be accepted (they won’t, right?) It begs the interesting question on the future of bug bounty programs: when it’s so easy to find and solve issues there’s less incentive to offer a bounty, which in turn leads to less incentive for humans/agents to find and fix security issues..? As someone not plugged in on the current times with respect to bug bounty programs, curious for peoples take on this / is this already the reality ? reply amai 8 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n How many new paths do these patches open? reply 9cb14c1ec0 33 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n Are places like NSA and Unit 8200 panicking right now that all their vulnerabilities are being patched? reply halz 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I assume this work resulted in the recent-ish TAINT_FORCED_BIND kernel feature https://lwn.net/Articles/1092680/ This api over the years has been abused and recently come under a major\n fuzzing \"attack\" through tools like syzbot which decided that it would\n attempt to just randomly bind any driver to any type of device, causing\n loads of unneeded errors and pointless kernel patches to be generated by\n unsuspecting new developers. reply ShadowOfThePit 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n > The flag is 'Y' which was unused, and can remembered as the user is\n\"yeeting\" the device being operated on here (thrown with force without\nregard for the thing being thrown). lol reply evan_a_a 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n >For safety reasons, we omit sharing the full technical details of these new exploitation techniques in this post. Convienient to claim \"safety\" when patches are already public. I await the full writeup on these new techniques so that they can be judged by the broader security community. reply rmbryan 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n With these vulnerabilities and the ones not listed that are sure to exist, how is the internet still up? How are my lights on? Why aren't power plants exploding by the dozens? What are the mitigating factors that prevent this level of vulnerability from being an existential threat to humanity? reply rcxdude 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Systems still take some effort to actually exploit. For example, the vast majority of bugs they found here are local privilege escalations, and most security people have long learned that user separation in the kernel is not a particularly strong security boundary. Network-exploitable vulnerabilities are a lot rarer, and it's noteworthy that there have been a few big ones discovered recently, but it's a much smaller number overall. reply tantalor 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n In general computer systems have been known unreliable since the 1980s, especially when networked. The entire cyber security industry operates not by preventing intrusions in the first place, but by rapidly mitigating them when they inevitably happen. Hopefully this type of work to automatically discover & patch vulnerability leads to much more reliable software, and the remove the need for cyber security. reply rmbryan 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Okay, thank you. That's a helpful reframing. reply imhoguy 47 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It is effort & probability game, and very often lack of information is the only advantage - security by obscurity. Even completly unplugged system probability of security breach is higher than zero, as somebody with enough determination can breach the physical place security protections and just sit at the machine and turn it on. reply nomel 10 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The governments buy them up [1]. Nukes wouldn't be required for WW3. [1] https://www.cyberdefensemagazine.com/zero-day-market-the-gov... reply rmbryan 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n article from 2013 reply nomel 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n 2022: https://www.darkreading.com/cyberattacks-data-breaches/googl... 2026: https://medium.com/@hammaadmughal/the-invisible-bazaar-insid... etc. Concept of governments being main buyers doesn't change by year. reply PinkMilkshake 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Just in the last month multiple governments had to shut down important online services due to a PHP vulnerability, and seven EU countries have had disruption to their food supply after millions of dollars' worth of it was cooked in hacked fridges. We aren't at existential yet, but we seem to be getting there. reply perlgeek 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n While \"disruption to their food supply\" is technically correct, it sounds way more dramatic than it actually was. Nobody went hungry because of these hacked fridges. reply asdff 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Not sure about this case but seems many of the vulns reported require physical access. At that point the vuln is irrelevant anyhow, your target is cooked a thousand different ways. reply paxy 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Because critical systems are mostly not accessible from the open internet reply Veserv 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Cybersecurity operates under the \"not enough bears\" theory of security. There are a million people in a valley and a male and female bear. You do not need to outrun the bears, you just need to outrun the two slowest people. Next year there are more bears, but you still only need to outrun a few people. Then, one day, there are a million bears and they eat you all. But that day has not happened yet. To explain more seriously, almost all visible cyberattacks are carried out by profit-motivated criminals. This has a very clear cost-benefit analysis; your revenue is hacking organizations and extorting them for available money, and your costs are developing and deploying attacks. The ROI is outrageously good easily being on the order of 10x to 100x. If it is so easy and so profitable why do not they just hack all of the millions of organizations on the planet with millions of dollars of available cash for trillions of dollars of revenue? Sure, all they have to do is create a trillion dollar revenue commercial organization bootstrapping themselves with no outside investors, loans, or access to financial resources or the financial system. I mean, it took Mark Zuckerberg 20 years and a metric ton of VC investment and public investment to make 200 billion dollars in revenue. So what kind of loser can not make a commercial organization 5x larger with a trillion dollars in revenue in less time with no outside investors? Are they even trying? Just make more revenue than any other commercial organization in the entire world and extract that money in a freely usable form, how hard can that be? They are not there yet, but they are trying. If you look at cybercrime reports you see something like 3x-10x growth year over year. Ransomware reports from a few years ago were showing a 3x year over year growth in number of attacks and simultaneously a 3x year over year growth in ransom amount with the averages being on the order of 100,000 dollars. Chump change for any serious organization, but give that a few years of 3x and that starts to look like real money. However, as it becomes real money it becomes harder and harder to launder it into a usable form. Sure, you could hit someone for 30 million dollars, but how are you going to get that into your bank account? Cryptocurrency helps some, but even there the big money tries to steer away from large overt crime. Maybe you could launder a few tens of billions a year that way, but that is still a far cry from the trillions for everything everywhere. And of course even assuming you can scale your organization 10x year over year it still takes a decade of sustained growth, scaling your hiring and organization across 9 orders of magnitude, to get to billions per year. A literal decade even if you were growing at a outrageous pace for a outrageous amount of time just to reach a fraction of the total addressable market. A decade of that and you are still nowhere near blowing up all the power plants. Give it time, we need a lot of bears to get there. reply bananaquant 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n It feels like this comment section has either gotten collective amnesia or didn't watch Greg KH's video on previous dump and grossly exaggerated claims from Anthropic [0]. From his talk, AIs can't reliably deduplicate or count vulnerabilities, dash for easy and meaningless escape hatches like malicious filesystems, and report the same things as found by everyone else. Indeed, this post literally says:\n\"Within 10 days while the scan was running, we observed over 100 Linux LPEs had been patched or had staging patches from other contributors, with ~300 more being publicly discussed.\" A few ready takeaways: this is just hype. The community is already doing a great job at finding and patching the actual vulns without the attention-desperate big labs. And no, you absolutely do not need to pay them a million dollars each year for the privilege. This behavior is the same thing that OpenAI has been doing to math -- produce tons of slop that are useless to humans. But this time, we don't even get to see how bad it is because they need to hide it to protect our precious little minds. We have got to be afraid though, very afraid. Our field really needs someone like Terry Tao to unambiguously stand up to this behavior and say that they are not welcome to take such dumps on everyone. [0]: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NnV_cWeoo5Q reply QuantumNomad_ 3 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > The community is already doing a great job at finding and patching the actual vulns without the attention-desperate big labs. It is however reasonable to assume that a lot of bugs that are being found by the community these days were found thanks to those people using AI to aid them. See for example what a PostgreSQL developer is saying: > All software contains bugs, and PostgreSQL’s code base is large, complex, and old. Our git history – converted from CVS – now reaches back more than 30 years, and the first code was written a decade before that. There’s a huge amount of code, and that code contains a huge number of bugs. That’s always been true, and it hasn’t stopped PostgreSQL from being popular or successful. What’s different now is that it’s become vastly easier to find those bugs. […] > […] > […] a huge number of people on the Internet are now reporting bugs in PostgreSQL that they found using LLMs, and the PostgreSQL committers have been very, very busy fixing them […] http://rhaas.blogspot.com/2026/10/postgres-19-is-late-and-it... https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50312408 reply bananaquant 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Your argument is literal red herring. Postgres is a different project with different codebase and vastly different-sized security team. Why not look at something like SQLite instead and conclude that things are great? reply QuantumNomad_ 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n All I’m saying is, that it is reasonable to assume that a lot of other people also benefit from AI to find bugs in Linux. The Linux kernel is also a huge amount of code that has been worked on by a lot of people for many many years. There are certain types of bugs that are too obscure to realize are even there for a person alone. And there are types of bugs that even if you see that something is funky it is too time consuming to dig into for a human. I am not saying that the Linux community is not doing great. They are doing a fantastic job, and have been for years, and they are giving all of that away for free too which is nothing short of incredible. What I am saying is that I think AI allows everyone to find even more bugs than was humanly possible without AI. reply bananaquant 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It seems we are in agreement then. It doesn't cost a million dollar a year super-restricted AI to find those bugs and the community is already successfully doing that. reply nas 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I've always considered local escalations to be possible in Linux (i.e. having user access almost certainly means you can get root). However, 948 vulnerabilities is kind of shocking. Hopefully we are getting to the end of the part where it \"gets worse before it gets better\". reply markus_zhang 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder what is the opinion of people who study and create exploits? Are you happy or not? reply treebeard901 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > Large, complex software projects likely require multiple rounds of scanning and fixing vulnerabilities to “bottom out” latent bugs. Ideally this process is repeated with each subsequent generation of frontier models. This applies to both proprietary and open source software. One of the most important paragraphs from the article. Real implications for software development from a security perspective for new and existing software. Every software developer and vendor has a choice... Submit your code base to a frontier model to have it reviewed and fix all the bugs. Each time a new model is released, this process must be done again. If your company is against submitting your code to the frontier model companies for the many good reasons to avoid doing it, your software will eventually become a target if it isn't already. Then your company will be forced to scan the software and deal with the fallout from getting hacked. If the Linux kernel has this many exploits, and it has been audited more than most other software... Then it seems obvious that all other software is even less secure. reply miki123211 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Have there been significant AI-assisted exploits found against software which is not written in memory-unsafe languages? As models get better and porting costs get lower, maybe it's better to bite the bullet and RRiR instead of having a minor bugpocalypse every 3 months, as each subsequent generation of frontier models finds sev-high CVEs? reply loremium 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Security has been treated by corporations as a cost factor only and therefore ignored for years and now it starts to hurt not having taken care of in the first place. reply lifeisloving 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This just translates to, \"you're going to need to do multiple rounds of scanning so we recommend budgeting $3.6 million a year for buying tokens from us not just $1.2 million. Dont worry just give us a few million dollars and you'll never get hacked, you can cut your security staff in half to pay for it! We definately wont cause more bugs!\" reply hypfer 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > If your company is against submitting your code to the frontier model companies for the many good reasons to avoid doing it, your software will eventually become a target if it isn't already. Yeah yeah we're all going to be left behind if we do not jump off the same cliff everyone else is jumping off. It is wrong to try to take your own lessons from this and do the thing but in a less harmful way. Just do exactly what the labs want you to do. Jesus man reply Luke2642 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Do we need to switch to seL4 type verified kernels? There's no way we can secure millions lines of code, the attack surface is too big? reply minimaxa 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n We are all just sitting ducks, until we have access to these models and enough tokens to pen test our systems. The other issue is GitHub allowing 1000's of black hat open-source exploit malware swarm tools being forked. The future internet is going to be perilous. Within 12 hours, the analyst agents produced 2,308 candidate findings after deduplication. This stage cost $316,549 in API credits. reply perlgeek 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n > We are all just sitting ducks, until we have access to these models and enough tokens to pen test our systems. You don't need SOTA models to pend test your systems. With enough effort put into proper harnesses, you can use good open-weights models and achieve a lot. You can also use coding agents to clear out technical debt, or improve the security architecture. Don't let yourself get paralyzed by lack of access to the latest-and-greatest models. reply solyin 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > We are all just sitting ducks You can the same $20/mo Claude code plan you've been using to build your system to red team attack the system you've been building with it and probe it for holes, and it'll do a decent job of making sure there aren't any glaring issues. reply EmX_3000 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n So... this is what I get out of my trillion dollar stimulus? OpenAI is developing a universal rooting system skeleton key to get at everybody's computers? Slap me if I'm not reading this correctly, but I think that's the gist of it. It's time everybody started their own air-gapped home network with every digital capability you hold dear, I think. That's what he's saying. Imagine somebody breaches their security, or the AI goes schizo, we've seen this so many times in fiction. Finally I have a reason to use Firewire again lol. reply 71bw 6 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Why wouldn’t your network be air-gapped? reply asdff 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You have home wifi or wired ethernet? Sorry but let’s have those without sin cast the first stone. reply zahlman 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > We've developed an autonomous vulnerability discovery, exploitation, and patching pipeline. In one run with our cyber model, it validated and generated patches for 948 local privilege escalation vulnerabilities in Linux at an API cost of $1.2 million. Imagine if open-source organizations other than the Linux Foundation (and Mozilla I guess) could throw $1.2M of API-rate LLM usage at security. reply komali2 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n This, but for anything Capital-gated. Imagine if all universities could get access to radiotelescope time, atom smasher time, quantum compute research lab access... frontier models on massive datacenters represents a huge new category of captured commons. reply nurumaik 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Tbh I feel less safe in digital world after reading this article. Would be cool to see \"And then we made a formal proof that there's no more vulnerabilities\" someday reply evan_a_a 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Everything is broken, it's just a matter of how bad. Just like it is impossible to write software that is free of bugs, it is impossible to write software that is free of vulnerabilities. Such is the nature of the field. The good news is we have well established tools in security to mitigate the impact of any one vulnerability. reply getcrunk 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > For the near and medium term, having access to a low-privileged account on an operating system, that isn’t up-to-date on its latest available patches should be considered functionally equivalent to root access for a well-resourced threat actor. *buries head in sand* *cries* reply kccqzy 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n LPEs are frequent enough that people no longer use low-privilege user accounts to sandbox potentially malicious software. They are only used to control access for well-behaved users without any intent to subvert the system, not attackers. The difference here reminds me of the distinction between the regular Paxos algorithm and the Byzantine Paxos algorithm. reply dbdr 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > people no longer use low-privilege user accounts to sandbox potentially malicious software Is it the same for containers and VMs? If not, what makes them less vulnerable? reply rubinlinux 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This has been true for DECADES. reply basilikum 12 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Regular LPEs are not novel. The frequency has just increased a lot. reply bakugo 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Some distros even include LPE as a feature ( cough Ubuntu Server) reply asdff 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Id count windows and macos in there because you just need an admin pass and you have full rights. Average users might reuse that password everywhere too, plus no password requirements, no expiration. So many guides online for various stuff have users typing sudo or run as administrator too, so they don’t look at it with a side eye either if the attack went in via social engineering instead of brute force or using a leaked pass associated with the user. reply keyle 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n While we were developing the pipeline, in late July 2026, GPT‑5.6 Sol found and exploited 166 unique LPE vulnerabilities for an end-to-end $513,690 cost in API credits.\n\n In the second scan, in mid-October 2026, the newer cyber model found and exploited 948 unique LPE vulnerabilities for an end-to-end $1,213,430 cost in API credits. For both campaigns, we scanned the latest Linux kernel master branch at the time.\n \nboy I really wonder though, how many of these 948 LPE are nearly impossible to execute though. reply bzmrgonz 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder what Sidero Labs has to say about this article. My hopes are pegged on their efforts. I have my wagon ready to hitch on their horses. They are not a one pony show anymore guys, Talos OS can be standalone server OS, a hypervisor server or cluster, and their flagship of k8s node or cluster. We get all that in an immutable gitops ready OS. I'm practically sold on their offering. reply shepherdjerred 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I love Talos. I've used it for years for a two-node k8s cluster. It just works. reply dmos62 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I love how we're finally putting numbers to our technical debt. reply rishabhd 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Curious question - if any organization sponsored a 1.2mn USD program to find vulnerabilities in Linux, will they find these many vulnerabilities? Or use a professional software like Checkmarx or Veracode to do it with 1.2mn USD in license + human effort. Is it a funding + will problem or is it an AI problem? reply asdff 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I’d think they’d go a long way. You have to remember many jailbreaks in major hardened systems over the decades have come from gifted eastern european teenagers. Imagine giving this talent actual resources and a mandate to do this full time. reply breisa 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Crazy times. These corporations and their governments have access to literal zero-day vending machines. Put $50 in - get another one.\nI mean, yeah, it was clear that certain agencies had heaps of useful exploits at hand for quite some time now. But AI really commoditizes these exploits. If there's source code or even just a binary available, they can hack it. And even if your code is robust and secure, they just pull the lever on the vending machine again and come back with some zero-day for your OS's networking stack. reply ksec 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wonder if they could run it on OpenBSD and FreeBSD as well. reply tiffanyh 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Anthropic ran Mythos against OpenBSD and only had 1 reported vulnerability. https://www.anthropic.com/glasswing reply asdff 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Is this because of security or poor representation in training set? Then again that might not matter, same outcome. reply IcyWindows 11 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I think this is one area where Windows has an advantage. ACLs, Appcontainers with capabilities, etc have been something in the OS for more than a decade. reply jasonjayr 11 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n There are a lot of LPE's in Windows too. Remember that it started out as a single user operating system, it took a long time to get software on board to not always running as admin. reply easton 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Was NT ever single user? I thought they built it with network/multiuser in mind. (I agree that maybe applications could’ve been built for 3.1/9x and expect a single user, but the os expects multiple). reply perching_aix 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Considering the amount of crazy shit even just user access allows you to do, by design, an LPE is not even really necessary. reply viraptor 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Linux has effectively same options and more via LSM. I'm not sure why ACL is mentioned here since it exists on Linux too. What advantage do you mean specifically? reply matt3210 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n cc <<< 'Rewrite the kernel, all libs and all GNU in rust so we can avoid the unsafe keyword. Make only one mistake and document it in mistake.md' reply sean_pedersen 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n How many of these would have been prevented by using safe Rust? reply clvx 10 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n and yet thousands of iot devices will not get these updates. This is gonna be fun. reply tarpitt 10 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Oh no, they will not be secured against their own users :( reply hoppp 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Well at least they are doing public service while hacking the planet. reply snvzz 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n And so many more are hidden in there, in those MLoCs. The only way forward is a better system architecture, as enabled by the seL4[0] microkernel. Any solution involving keeping Linux or the monolithic kernel is just stopgap or outright delusional. 0. https://sel4.systems reply tamimio 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n These are becoming more and more marketing materials than actual findings, because OAI probably hit the wall in making their models smarter (just like how google try to specialize their models in legal or whatever), but they need to remain relevant in the news. Sol 6.1 sucked so bad in reasoning that either it’s actually a bad model or users behind the scenes get degraded outcome while using all the resources for these marketing stunts. Stop, it’s embarrassing, make better models or be gone, at least we will get cheaper RAM to advance the development of open weights models. reply numlock86 5 hours ago | prev [–] \n Plot twist: They look for genuine exploit paths to fix as a cover up, while introducing and potentially selling genuine new backdoors that humans or other models are unlikely to find. Edit: Or maybe the AI does it just for its own interest. Just kidding (or am I?), but this is pretty cool. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
 "page:https://huggingface.co/ricursiveAI/Ripple-65B-Preview/raw/main/README.md": {
