  "text": " \n \n\n [2611.06294] A.S.E 2.0: Understanding How Repository Context Impacts the Security of LLM-Generated Code \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search \n \n \n \n \n \n Submit \n Donate \n \n Log in \n \n \n \n \n Search arXiv \n \n \n \n \n \n Press Enter to search &middot; Advanced search \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n--> \n\n \n \n Computer Science > Cryptography and Security \n \n\n \n arXiv:2611.06294 (cs)\n \n \n \n \n \n [Submitted on 4 Nov 2026] \n Title: A.S.E 2.0: Understanding How Repository Context Impacts the Security of LLM-Generated Code \n Authors: Jiongchi Yu , Keke Lian , Junqing Wang , Zhikang Fan , Zhaoxuan Li , Yongheng Huang , Ziming Zhao , Dong Zhang View a PDF of the paper titled A.S.E 2.0: Understanding How Repository Context Impacts the Security of LLM-Generated Code, by Jiongchi Yu and Keke Lian and Junqing Wang and Zhikang Fan and Zhaoxuan Li and Yongheng Huang and Ziming Zhao and Dong Zhang \n View PDF \n HTML (experimental) \n\n\n\n \n Abstract: Large language model (LLM) coding assistants and agents construct a repository context before generating code. For security-sensitive code, this context decides whether the model sees the project's safeguards (e.g., authorization checks) or insecure code to imitate. However, context construction is designed almost exclusively for functional correctness, and secure-code benchmarks treat the context as fixed, so how repository context affects the security of generated code remains unclear. We construct CASE-Bench, a benchmark of 145 repository-level tasks from real CVEs in 51 projects. Each task runs in Docker, has functional tests and a proof-of-concept exploit validated in both directions, and is annotated with the repository code that enforces or undermines its security. Using CASE-Bench, we compare 13 context construction methods and six controls with six LLMs, edit single context elements, and analyze coding-agent trajectories. We find that (1) functionality-oriented context raises the functional pass rate by up to 8.2 percentage points (pp) but the secure pass rate by at most 3.7 pp, (2) withholding a safeguard lowers the secure pass rate by up to 8.1 pp, while one inserted insecure exemplar raises correct-but-exploitable code by 6.3 pp, and (3) coding agents observe the relevant safeguard in only 27-36% of trajectories. These findings inform security-aware context construction and secure-code evaluation.\n \n\n \n \n \n Comments: \n 15 pages \n \n \n Subjects: \n \n Cryptography and Security (cs.CR) ; Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Software Engineering (cs.SE) \n \n Cite as: \n arXiv:2611.06294 [cs.CR] \n \n \n \n (or \n arXiv:2611.06294v1 [cs.CR] for this version)\n \n \n \n \n https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2611.06294 \n \n \n Focus to learn more \n \n \n \n arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Submission history From: Jiongchi Yu [ view email ] [v1] \n Wed, 4 Nov 2026 10:29:52 UTC (678 KB) \n \n \n \n \n \n Full-text links: \n Access Paper: \n \n \nView a PDF of the paper titled A.S.E 2.0: Understanding How Repository Context Impacts the Security of LLM-Generated Code, by Jiongchi Yu and Keke Lian and Junqing Wang and Zhikang Fan and Zhaoxuan Li and Yongheng Huang and Ziming Zhao and Dong Zhang View PDF HTML (experimental) TeX Source\n \n \n \n view license \n \n \n \n Additional Features \n \n Audio Summary \n \n \n \n Current browse context: \n cs.CR \n\n \n \n < prev \n \n | \n next > \n \n \n new \n | \n recent \n | 2026-11 \n \n Change to browse by:\n \n cs \n cs.AI \n cs.SE \n \n \n\n \n \n References & Citations \n \n SciX Google Scholar \n Semantic Scholar \n \n \n \n\n \n export BibTeX citation \n Loading... \n \n\n \n \n \n BibTeX formatted citation \n &times; \n \n \n loading... \n \n \n Data provided by: \n \n \n \n \n Bookmark \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \