  "bytes": 8357,
  "text": " \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n \n\n HA Heartbeat-Origin Authentication Bypass (CVE Pending) | \n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n\t\n\t\n \n\t\n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Products \n \n \n Support & Resources \n \n \n Get A Quote \n \n \n \n \n \n Solutions \n \n \n Company \n \n \n Sign In \n \n \n \n Close \n \n \n \n \n Products \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n V-Series \n Max Performance & Versatility \n \n \n \n \n \n F-Series \n High Performance NVME \n \n \n \n \n \n H-Series \n Versatility and Power Efficiency \n \n \n \n \n \n Mini Series \n Edge and Branch Office \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n M-Series \n High Capacity All-Flash or Hybrid \n \n \n \n \n \n R-Series \n Single Controller Appliances \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n TrueNAS Enterprise \n Mission Critical Storage. 24×7 Enterprise Support \n \n \n \n \n \n TrueNAS Community Edition \n Test Drive TrueNAS in Your Environment \n \n \n \n \n \n Streamlined Management for your TrueNAS Systems \n \n \n \n \n Solutions \n \n \n \n \n Industry \n \n Audio \n Automotive \n Broadcasting \n Education \n US Public Sector \n Gaming \n Healthcare \n MSP \n Manufacturing \n Research & AI \n Media & Entertainment \n \n \n \n Use Cases \n \n Analytics \n Backup & Archival \n Cloud \n Data Mobility \n File Sharing \n TrueCloud Backup \n Media & Entertainment \n Surveillance/CCTV \n Veeam Backup \n Virtualization \n Red Hat OpenShift \n \n \n \n TrueNAS as an Alternative \n \n Alternative to Dell \n Alternative to NetApp \n Alternative to Pure Storage \n \n \n \n \n \n Support & Resources \n \n \n \n \n Support \n \n \n \n Enterprise Support \n For customers with active support contracts \n \n \n \n \n Community Support \n For peer-to-peer, forum support \n \n \n \n \n \n Resources \n \n Case Studies \n Datasheets \n Documentation \n TrueNAS API \n FAQ \n Apps \n TrueNAS Blogs \n Software Status \n TrueNAS Security \n Solution Briefs and Guides \n Certifications & Integrations \n White Papers \n Videos \n TrueCommand Cloud \n Reseller Network \n ZFS Overview \n \n \n \n Community \n \n TrueNAS Community Edition \n Community Forum \n Discord \n TrueNAS GitHub \n TrueNAS Merch Store \n \n \n \n \n \n Company \n \n About TrueNAS \n Team \n Awards \n Careers \n Contact Us \n Newsroom \n Our Clients \n Reviews \n \n \n \n \n Sign In \n \n TrueNAS Connect \n Partner Portal \n Enterprise Support \n \n \n \n \n Get A Quote \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Search site \n \n \n\n \n Navigation \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n TrueNAS Security Advisories / TrueNAS | Enterprise Security Responses / TrueNAS SCALE | Impact Statements / HA Heartbeat-Origin Authentication Bypass (CVE Pending) \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n HA Heartbeat-Origin Authentication Bypass (CVE Pending) \n\t\n Status: Advisory\n Affected products: TrueNAS CE/Enterprise (SCALE) — middlewared . TrueNAS CORE: Not Affected.\n Severity (TrueNAS-adjusted): High — CVSS 3.1 8.3 ( AV:A/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H )\n CWE: CWE-290 (Authentication Bypass by Spoofing), CWE-306 (Missing Authentication for Critical Function)\n Fixed in: TrueNAS SCALE 25.10.8 (forthcoming) and later (25.10 train)\n Credit: Reported by spnctf through coordinated disclosure. \n \n \n Summary\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n The TrueNAS middleware ( middlewared ) includes a peer-authentication hook used between the\ntwo controllers of a High Availability (HA) appliance over the private heartbeat link. On\naffected versions this hook made its trust decision from the TCP peer address and source\nport of the incoming connection alone: a connection whose peer address was one of the\nhardcoded HA heartbeat addresses (169.254.10.1 / 169.254.10.2) and whose source port was\n1024 or lower was granted an internal node credential that is treated as a full\nadministrator for every API method. \n Because that decision relied only on attacker-influenceable properties of the connection,\nand because the hook was registered on all installations — including non-HA, single-node\nCommunity Edition systems with no HA configuration — an attacker able to meet those\nconditions could obtain unauthenticated administrative API access without presenting any\ncredential. Administrative access to the API can in turn be used to execute commands on the\nunderlying operating system as root. \n \n \n Applicability to TrueNAS\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n The hook was present regardless of whether the system is licensed for HA, has HA hardware, or\nhas any HA configuration, so single-node Community Edition and Enterprise systems were in\nscope, not only HA pairs. TrueNAS CORE does not share this middleware code and is not\naffected. \n \n \n Threat model and risk assessment\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Exploitation is not possible from an arbitrary Internet location. The trust check keys on\na 169.254.10.x link-local source address, and a stock TrueNAS system disables IPv4\nlink-local autoconfiguration and holds no route to the 169.254.0.0/16 range. As a result: \n \n The attacker must be on the same layer-2 network segment as the appliance&rsquo;s management\ninterface (for example a virtual machine on the same hypervisor switch, a host on the same\nflat VLAN, or the network gateway). \n Adjacency by itself is not sufficient. Because the system has no route back to a\n169.254.10.x address, the attacker must additionally alter the return path — either by\nmodifying routing on the TrueNAS system or by controlling the gateway/path — to complete the\nexchange. This raises attack complexity materially. \n \n Where those conditions are met, the impact is high: unauthenticated administrative control of\nthe API, which bypasses the management-interface IP allowlist and API rate limiting, can read\nconfiguration secrets, is not recorded in the audit log, and can lead to command execution as\nroot. On a typical deployment — a TrueNAS appliance whose management interface is on a trusted,\nswitched management network the operator controls — the practical exposure is limited by the\nadjacency and return-path requirements above. \n \n \n Available mitigations\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n Primary (apply now): Keep the TrueNAS management interface on a dedicated, trusted\nmanagement network. Ensure no untrusted host shares the management interface&rsquo;s layer-2 segment\n(isolate hypervisor virtual switches and management VLANs), so that no untrusted party can\noriginate traffic from a 169.254.10.x source address toward the management interface. This\nfully blocks the exposure and is the recommended action today. \n Update when a fixed release is available for your train: The corrected code — which\nrequires that the connection also terminate on the HA heartbeat listener, a local address and\nport the attacker cannot control, before the node credential is granted — is included in\nTrueNAS SCALE 25.10.8 , a forthcoming release on the 25.10 train (Community Edition and\nEnterprise), and later. Until that release is published, rely on the network mitigation above. \n \n \n Resolution\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n TrueNAS has corrected the HA node-authentication check so that trust additionally depends on\nthe connection terminating on the heartbeat listener, which is bound only on the heartbeat\ninterface. The fix is included in TrueNAS SCALE 25.10.8 — a forthcoming release — and later on\nthe 25.10 train. Until that release is available, apply the network mitigation above. This advisory\nwill be updated with the assigned CVE identifier and as fixed releases become available. \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n TrueNAS \n Community ™ | Enterprise ™ \n Security \n \n \n TrueNAS Connect Security \n \n \n TrueNAS® | Enterprise \n Hardware Solutions \n Security \n \n \n TrueNAS \n CORE ™ | Enterprise ™ \n Security \n \n \n TrueNAS \n Integrations \n Security \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 541 Division Street \n Campbell, CA 95008, USA\n \n \n \n \n \n Contact Us \n \n \n \n \n \n info@truenas.com \n \n \n \n \n \n Listen to our Podcast \n \n \n\n \n \n Support \n \n Enterprise Support \n Community Support \n Forums \n FAQs \n \n \n\n \n \n Useful Links \n \n Careers \n About Us \n Blog \n News & Articles \n TrueNAS Enterprise \n TrueNAS Community Edition \n TrueCommand \n TrueCommand Cloud \n TrueNAS Legacy \n Software Status \n Reseller Network \n \n \n\n \n \n Subscribe to our newsletter \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\t\n \n \n © Copyright iXsystems, Inc. dba TrueNAS. All rights reserved. \n \n \n Legal \n TrueNAS Logos \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n"
